ral Reconciliation, Transaction Monitoring and\nCard, Agriculture, MSME,\nRuSu & FI and Gold, Stressed Asset Management\nAnti Money Laundering,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c927","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":357,"text":"Anti Money Laundering,\nTechnology, Digitization, Digital Business, Support Services Department and Corporate Communication,\nAudit & Inspection, Banking Operations, Chief\nVertical, Board Secretariat,\nDeposit & CASA\nCorporate Strategy &\nInformation Security\nBusiness Excellence\nOffice (CISO) & Data Protection Office (DPO), Risk\nProcurement\nLarge Corporate\nManagement and Fraud Monitoring, Compliance\nRetail Asset & Credit Card\n Loan syndication, NBG\nTreasury\nAgriculture Business\n Business Development\nHuman Resources and Learning & Development\nFinance & Accounts\nMSME\n \u0007Co Lending and pool\nBuyout Cell\n \u0007IND AS, Regulatory\nRuSu & FI and Gold\n Administration\nReporting , Taxation, Operation Exp & Non- Interest Income\nMid Corporate\n RuSU & FI\n Industrial Relations\nDFB & International Banking including LC/BG/Forex\n Gold Loan\n LND & AMO\nCentral Reconciliation, Transaction Monitoring and AML\nStressed Asset Management Vertical\nInformation Technology\nEcosystem Banking\n Data Centre\n Asset Liability Marketing\nAudit & Inspection\nBoard Secretariat\n Network, Security &\n Wealth Management\nBanking Operations\nCorporate Strategy & Business Excellence\n Non-Core Applications\n Core operations\n \u0007Govt Business &\nRelationship\nDigitization\n Branch Expansion cell\n Strategy\n Digital Contact Centre\n \u0007Corporate Relationship\n \u0007Channel Design, Build\n \u0007Subsidiaries & Joint\ncell\n& Run","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c928","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":357,"text":"& Run\n New Initiatives\nVentures\n Transaction Banking\n Alternate Channels\n \u0007Platform Build, Digital\n ACoE\nJourneys\n \u0007Customer Service\nRetail Deposit & CASA\n Central Repository & MIS\nExcellence Cell\nDigital Business\n Deposit Mobilization\n EASE\nChief Information Security Office (CISO) & Data Protection Office (DPO)\nSupport Services Department and Corporate Communication\n CASA\n CEA (Banking Research)\nProcurement\nRisk Management and Fraud Monitoring\n Risk Management\n Fraud Management\nCompliance\n \u0007Compliance & Data\nValidation Cell\n5 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nSHRI NITESH RANJAN\nExecutive Director SHRI ASHEESH PANDEY\nManaging Director & CEO\nShri Asheesh Pandey is a seasoned Banker with experience of more than 27 years. He started his career from erstwhile Corporation Bank. Prior to assuming the current position, he worked as an Executive Director of the Bank of Maharashtra and Chief General Manager of the Union Bank of India.\nShri Nitesh Ranjan is Executive Director of Union Bank of India since March 10, 2021. He is driving the Bank’s digital transformation and leading strategic initiatives for balance sheet optimization, customer experience, and people development. While steering business outcomes, he emphasizes on continuous strengthening of risk and compliance standards.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c929","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":358,"text":"Shri Pandey has an experience of handling diverse portfolio ranging from Credit, Credit Monitoring, Treasury & Merchant Banking, Foreign Transactions & Joint Ventures, Marketing & Customer Relationships and Banking Operations. Being a Tech-Savvy banker, he has played a key role in successful digital banking transformation by adopting ideations and innovations. He has also played an important role in the re-engineering, harmonization, automation and digitization during amalgamation of e-Andhra Bank and e-Corporation Bank with Union Bank of India.\nAssociated with Union Bank of India since 2008, he has held key positions including Head of Treasury Operations & Strategy, Chief Investor Relations Officer, Chief Economist, and Regional Head. He began his career with the Food Corporation of India as a management trainee and later worked with Andhra Bank.\nHe is a member of the IBA Steering Committee on PSB Reforms. Previously, he served on the boards of NPCI, SUD Life Insurance Company, SWIFT India, SBI Global Factors Ltd, and FIMMDA, and was a founding member of the India Forex Committee (IFXC).","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c930","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":358,"text":"He is a member of the IBA Steering Committee on PSB Reforms. Previously, he served on the boards of NPCI, SUD Life Insurance Company, SWIFT India, SBI Global Factors Ltd, and FIMMDA, and was a founding member of the India Forex Committee (IFXC).\nShri Pandey is a Mechanical Engineer and also holds Post Graduation in Management with specialisation in Finance & Marketing. He is an alumnus of Indian Institute of Management – Bangalore. He is also a Certified Associate of Indian Institute of Bankers. Shri Pandey has also completed Executive Program in Strategic Digital Marketing & Analytics - 2023 by IIM, Rohtak and Directors Development Program-2023 by Egon Zehnder in collaboration with Harvard Business Publishing. He has also completed Leadership Development Program for Senior Management of PSBs conducted by IIM Bangalore as well as program on IT & Cyber Security for Senior Management conducted by IDRBT, Hyderabad.\nShri Ranjan holds a postgraduate degree in Economics and has also completed the Leadership Development Programme of IIM Bangalore. He has also contributed to social initiatives, dedicating 100 days to Teach India’s Spoken English Programme for underprivileged youth.\n6 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nSHRI RAMASUBRAMANIAN S\nSHRI SANJAY RUDRA\nExecutive Director\nExecutive Director","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c931","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":359,"text":"Executive Director\nShri Ramasubramanian S. assumed office as Executive Director of Union Bank of India w.e.f. from November 21, 2022. Prior to joining Union Bank of India, he was Chief General Manager of Canara Bank. He has to his credit more than 25 years of rich experience in various facets of Banking like Corporate Credit, MSME/Retail Credit, International Credit and forex. He holds a Bachelor degree in Science along with CAIIB qualification.\nMr. Sanjay Rudra assumed office as the Executive Director of Union Bank of India with effect from October 09, 2023. Prior to joining Union Bank of India, he served as General Manager and Chief Risk Officer of Bank of Maharashtra. He brings with him over three decades of rich Banking experience across diverse functional areas including Credit, Priority Sector, MSME and Integrated Risk Management. He was also in charge of the Learning & Development (L&D) Vertical and held additional responsibility for Development Testing of Digital Lending initiatives.\nThroughout his banking career, he has worked at different locations and branch categories including Prime Corporate Credit Wing, Large Corporate, Mid Corporate Branches and Hong Kong Branch of Canara Bank and administrative offices including Head Office.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c932","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":359,"text":"Throughout his banking career, he has worked at different locations and branch categories including Prime Corporate Credit Wing, Large Corporate, Mid Corporate Branches and Hong Kong Branch of Canara Bank and administrative offices including Head Office.\nMr. Rudra holds a Post Graduate degree in Physics and a Diploma in Financial Management from Welingkar Institute of Management. He is an Associate Member of the Indian Institute of Banking & Finance (IIBF). He has undergone the Leadership Development Programme at IIM Bangalore, conducted by FSIB and has also attended the Global Advanced Management Program at ISB Hyderabad in association with the Kellogg School of Management, USA. Additionally, he has earned a Certificate in Public Policy from ISB Hyderabad.\nHe is one of the Top Executive Grade officers who underwent Leadership Development Strategy Program organized by Financial Services Institutions Bureau (erstwhile Banks Board Bureau). He is in the standing Committee for Corporate Credit of IBA. He is also nominated to participate in Kamath Committee on COVID related loan Restructuring.\nHe is well versed in operations and administration equally.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c933","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":359,"text":"He is well versed in operations and administration equally.\nA key member of the senior leadership team at Bank of Maharashtra, Mr. Rudra played an active role in spearheading the Bank’s turnaround initiatives. He has served as a Director on the Board of Maharashtra Executor and Trustee Company Private Limited, a subsidiary of Bank of Maharashtra.\nFurther, Mr. Rudra has served as a Director on the Boards of Union AMC Pvt. Ltd. and PSB Alliance Pvt. Ltd. He is presently continuing as a Director on the Boards of Swift India Domestic Services Pvt. Ltd., IIBM Guwahati and India Infrastructure Finance Company Limited (IIFCL), where he contributes to strategic oversight, governance and policy direction at the highest level.\n7 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nSHRI ROHAN CHAND THAKUR\nSHRI AMRESH PRASAD\nGovernment Nominee Director\nExecutive Director\n(Till 13.05.2026)\nPrior to assuming the office as the Executive Director of Union Bank of India, Shri Amresh Prasad was the Chief General Manager of Punjab National Bank posted at Corporate office.\nShri Rohan Chand Thakur is an officer of Indian Administrative Service (IAS) belonging to 2009 batch of Himachal Pradesh Cadre. At present, he is working as Director in Ministry of Finance, Department of Financial Services, Government of India.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c934","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":360,"text":"Shri Rohan Chand Thakur is an officer of Indian Administrative Service (IAS) belonging to 2009 batch of Himachal Pradesh Cadre. At present, he is working as Director in Ministry of Finance, Department of Financial Services, Government of India.\nHe has rich banking experience of over 32 years from branch banking, Zonal Offices to Head Office level, which includes Corporate Credit, Credit Review and Monitoring, Transaction Monitoring etc.\nHe is an economics graduate from St. Stephen’s College, Delhi and a Postgraduate in Management from IIM Ahmedabad.\nExperience in Government of Himachal Pradesh\nHe is a graduate in Chemistry and Certified Associate from Indian Institute of Bankers (CAIIB).\n• District Collector of Hamirpur and Shimla.\nShri Prasad has attended various training and Leadership Development Programs and has taken part in Leadership Development Program at SBIL Kolkata. He has also completed Aarohan 2023 FSIB Program conducted by Financial Services Institution.\n• Director of Information Technology.\n• Managing Director of Skill Development & Entrepreneurship Department.\n• Managing Director of Himachal Pradesh Financial Corporation,\n• Excise and Taxation Commissioner.\n• Managing Director of Himachal Road Transport Corporation.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c935","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":360,"text":"• Managing Director of Himachal Road Transport Corporation.\nHe has previously worked with TAS before joining the civil services. He is also a Chevening Gurukul Fellow from the University of Oxford and a Master of Public Administration from Harvard Kennedy School, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts.\n8 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nRBI Nominee Director DR. DEBASISH PRUSTY\nSHRI PRAKASH BALIARSINGH\nGovernment Nominee Director (Since 13.05.2026)\nShri Prakash Baliarsingh has been nominated as RBI Nominee Director on the Board of the Bank with effect from 14th July 2023.\nDr. Debasish Prusty is a member of the Indian Administrative Service and now serving as the Additional Secretary in the Department of Financial Services in the Union Ministry of Finance in the Government of India. He has more than 26 years of experience in the designing and implementing polices in the realm of Public Finance, Sustainable Development, and Development Cooperation and Environmental and Urban Governance.\nShri Prakash Baliarsingh was Chief General Manager of Department of Regulation, Reserve Bank of India. He was a career central banker and having rich experience of more than 3 decades with various Departments of Reserve Bank of India (majorly with Department of Supervision and Department of Regulation).","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c936","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":361,"text":"He had contributed to India’s initiative for collaboration and partnership with various countries to launch the International Solar Alliance (ISA) platform in the year 2015 when he was the Director in the Climate change division of the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change in Government of India.\nHe has acted as Principal Inspecting Officer/Senior Supervisory Manager for leading commercial banks. He was also member of various committees/sub committees formed by the Reserve Bank of India related to Asset Quality Review (AQR) of banks, Risk Based Supervision as well as Supervisory Programme for Assessment of Risk and Capital (SPARC). He also represented Reserve Bank of India as a member in International Committees.\nIn the international negotiations in the conference of parties held under the United Nations Conference on Climate Change (UNFCCC) he had represented India’s negotiating team. He was a member of the standing committee on Finance of the UNFCCC in 2016.\nHe is a Post-Graduate in Political Science and also completed M.Sc. (Finance) from Oxford Brookes University, UK. He was also a recipient of Reserve Bank of India’s Golden Jubilee scholarship. He is a Certified Associate of Indian Institute of Bankers.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c937","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":361,"text":"He is a Post-Graduate in Political Science and also completed M.Sc. (Finance) from Oxford Brookes University, UK. He was also a recipient of Reserve Bank of India’s Golden Jubilee scholarship. He is a Certified Associate of Indian Institute of Bankers.\nHe has been conferred the Doctor of Philosophy from the Management Department of the Manipal University Jaipur and the Master of philosophy in Environmental sciences from the Jawaharlal Nehru University, Delhi, India. Dr. Prusty is well trained from the International Monetary Fund (IMF) in the field of Finance for Macro-economists\n9 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nDR. PRITI JAY RAO Shareholder Director\nSHRI SURAJ SRIVASTAVA Part Time Non-Official Director\n(till 10.04.2026)\nDr. Priti Jay Rao has been re-elected as a Shareholder Director of the Bank and assumed office of the Director of the Bank on 29/07/2024 for another period of 3 years till 28/07/2027 and she is M.Sc. (Mathematics) from IIT Bombay with specialization in Computer Science. She is a passionate advocate of amplifying business value, leveraging Technology, Human Resources and Process Automation.\nShri Suraj Srivastava is a Fellow Member of The Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI) and also a Bachelor of Laws (LL.B.).","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c938","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":362,"text":"Shri Suraj Srivastava is a Fellow Member of The Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI) and also a Bachelor of Laws (LL.B.).\nShri Srivastava has a rich professional experience of over 19 years as a practicing Chartered Accountant in taxation and audit practice. As a Partner of M/s Batra Sapra & Co., Shri Srivastava has done statutory audits, concurrent audits and branch audits of various Public Sector Banks, Public Sector Undertakings and other Companies.\nShe has to her credit, 26 Years of diverse experience in building and delivering range of IT services for customers located across all the five continents. During her stint in various Companies, she played a pivotal role in supporting IT teams, delivering software services and managing large scale recruitment, training and assimilating large employee base.\nShri Srivastava has also completed Information Systems Audit (ISA) Assessment test from ICAI.\nShe is inclined towards a leadership skill that requires both knowledge and vision of technology with the ability to envisage how technology might affect organizational dynamics to help organisations accelerate technology adoption.\nShe is the member of Board of Directors of Public and Private companies related to various aspects of technology services and products for last several years.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c939","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":362,"text":"She is the member of Board of Directors of Public and Private companies related to various aspects of technology services and products for last several years.\nShe is also actively engaged in various CSR activities with a mission and objective to provide financial assistance, special coaching, counselling, other requisite support to deserving bright young girls from disadvantaged background to enable them to grow into empowered, confident professionals with strong values.\n10 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nSHRI PRAKASH CHANDRA KANDPAL\nShareholder Director","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c940","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":363,"text":"Shareholder Director\nMr. Prakash Chandra Kandpal has been elected as a Shareholder Director of the Bank and assumed office of the Director of the Bank on 27/07/2024 for a period of 3 till 26/07/2027 years and he has extensive experience of over 36 years with State Bank of India and its subsidiaries. He retired as Deputy Managing Director (Retail business - Personal Banking and Real Estate) of SBI. Mr. Kandpal has held multiple leading responsibilities at SBI including MD & CEO of SBI General Insurance and ED&COO at SBI Funds Management Ltd. He has an in-depth expertise in Banking, HR, Operations, Insurance and Mutual funds space and has contributed immensely to the Banking and Financial Services Industry. He has led many renowned initiatives in HR, Training, IT, Business Process Re-engineering and Governance.\nMr. Kandpal has held positions as Nominee Director, SBI Foundation and PSB Alliance Ltd and believes in contributing back to the environment and society with strong governance in place.\nHe is currently serving on the Board of Tata AIG General Insurance Company Ltd., Nucleus Software Exports Ltd. HDFC Pension Management Company Ltd., Early salary Services Pvt Ltd and Social worth Technologies Pvt Ltd.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c941","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":363,"text":"He is currently serving on the Board of Tata AIG General Insurance Company Ltd., Nucleus Software Exports Ltd. HDFC Pension Management Company Ltd., Early salary Services Pvt Ltd and Social worth Technologies Pvt Ltd.\nMr. Kandpal has been honoured by prestigious awards such as Business Leader of the Year Award, in 2021 and the Company with Great Managers Award on behalf of SBI GI, in 2021 and secured the Brandon Hall Award in 2018 for SBI for his pathbreaking initiatives in Learning and Development at SBI to name a few.\nMr. Kandpal holds a M.A(Economics) degree from Kumaon University, Nainital and Masters in Financial Management from Jamnalal Bajaj Institute of Management, Mumbai. He also has multiple professional certifications such as an Executive MBA program (curated for SBI) from ISB Hyderabad, and Global Advance Management Program– AIIMA with Berkley University, USA\n11 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCVO / KMPs / CRO / SMPs / GMs (as on 31.03.2026)\nChief Vigilance Officer\nShri Ajay Kumar Singh\nKey Managerial Personnel (KMP) other than Wholetime Directors\nShri P K Samal CCO\nShri Avinash Prabhu CFO upto 31.01.2026\nShri Dhirendra Jain CFO w.e.f. 01.02.2026\nShri Ashish Mishra CS w.e.f 28.01.2026\nShri Mangesh Mandrekar CS up to 28.01.2026\nChief Risk Officer\nShri Ashwini Choudhary Upto 21.05.2026","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c942","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":364,"text":"Shri Ashwini Choudhary Upto 21.05.2026\nSmt Sowmya Sridhar w.e.f. 22.05.2026\nChief General Managers (Senior Management Personnel)\nShri A K Vinod Shri Pravin Sharma\nShri Abhijit Basak Shri Suresh Chandra Teli\nShri Vasudevan Biju Shri Kabir Bhattacharya\nShri Arun Kumar Shri Anil Kuril\n12 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nChief General Managers (Senior Management Personnel)\nShri M Ravindra Babu Shri Sanjay Narayan\nShri Tata Venkat Venugopal Shri Biraja Prasad Das\nShri G.K. Sudhakar Rao Shri Vipan Singh\nShri Sarvesh Ranjan Shri Pramod Kumar Reddy w.e.f. 01.04.2026\nShri Sumit Srivastava w.e.f. 01.04.2026\nGeneral Managers\nShri Amarendra Kumar Shri Gyana Ranjan Sarangi\nShri Santanu Kumar Dash Shri Rajib Lochan Pattanayak\nShri Alok Kumar Shri Navneet Kumar\nShri Vikash Kumar Shri Manoj Kumar\nShri Gokulananda Das Shri Vithal Banashankari\n13 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nShri Vipin Kumar Shukla Shri Omprakash Shriniwas Karwa\nShri Rajiv Kumar Jha Shri Maniram Jithender\nShri Gugulotu Shankarlal Shri S Sakthivel\nShri Girija Bhusan Mishra Shri Manoj Kumar Nanda\nShri P Krishnan Shri Guna Nand Gami\nShri Baij Nath Singh Shri Chaganti Venkata Naga Bhaskara Rao\nSmt. Renu K Nair Shri Satyaban Behera\nShri Ajay Kumar Shri Patri Srinivasa Kumar\nShri Hare Krishna Das Shri Saroj Kumar Dash","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c943","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":366,"text":"Shri Hare Krishna Das Shri Saroj Kumar Dash\nShri Hrishikesh Mishra Shri K Mahendra Reddy\nShri Sunil Kumar Jadli Smt Archana Shukla\nShri Manoj Kumar Shri Rajesh Kumar\n14 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nShri T Kameswara Rao Shri Akhilesh Kumar\nSmt. Shalini Menon Shri Lokanath Sahoo\nDr. Chetna Pandey Shri Hara Mohan Sahoo\nShri Ravi Shanker Shri Satyajit Mohanty\nMs. Juna Roselin Dr. H T Vasappa\nSmt Tajvinder Kaur Shri Siddharth Kiran Mujumdar\nShri Kumar Rahul Shri Rajender Kumar\nShri Chittari Amaravati Kalyan Varma Shri Rajkumar S A\nShri Dillip Mishra Smt. Valluru Madhavi\nShri Sujit S Tariwal Shri Niraj Singh\nShri Sunil Ahuja Shri Prasanta Kumar Sahoo\nShri Sanjeev Kumar Shri Shalaj Kumar Sinha\n15 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nShri Sunil Kumar Shri Vikas Babu Chittiprolu\nShri Pranjal Bajpai\nInternal Ombudsman\nShri Anthony Juliano Rodrigues Shri Dharmveer Singh Shekhawat\nHead Tbd And Crc Chief Economic Adviser\nShri Prasad Ramaswamy Ms. Kanika Pasricha CEA\nHead Digital Call Centre\nShri Hitesh Sindwani Head Digital\n16 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nMessage from Managing Director & CEO\nDear esteemed Shareholders,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c944","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":369,"text":"Dear esteemed Shareholders,\nThe Indian economy and banking sector have continued to demonstrate resilience and stability amid persistent global uncertainties. Over the past few years, the world has witnessed multiple disruptions — from the COVID-19 pandemic and the Russia-Ukraine conflict to evolving global trade dynamics, tariff- related uncertainties, and geopolitical tensions arising from the West Asia conflict. Despite these persistent global shocks, India has continued to maintain its growth momentum, supported by strong domestic fundamentals, prudent policy measures, financial sector resilience, and the strength of its institutions.\nAgainst this backdrop, Union Bank of India remained focused on delivering sustainable growth while strengthening the foundations of a future-ready institution. Guided by our philosophy of “Business First, Compliance Always”, the Bank continued to pursue customer-centric growth, operational excellence, disciplined risk management, and responsible banking.\nAsheesh Pandey Managing Director and Chief Executive Officer\nlast year. Retail advances grew by 16.75%, MSME advances by 18.75%, and Agriculture advances by 2.75%, reaffirming our commitment towards inclusive and diversified credit growth. More than 99% of the retail portfolio had CIBIL scores above 700 and over 95% of the corporate portfolio remained rated BBB and above.\n1. Bank’s ‘ Business","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c945","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":369,"text":"1. Bank’s ‘ Business\nFY2025-26 was a landmark year for your Bank, reflecting strong business momentum, improved financial strength, and enhanced stakeholder confidence.\nThe Bank achieved its highest-ever annual net profit of `18,697 crore. This performance reflects the Bank’s continued focus on profitability, efficiency, quality growth, and prudent balance sheet management.\nProfitability remained stable with Return on Assets at 1.25%, Return on Equity at 15.86%, and Net Interest Margin at 2.70%. Capital strength remained robust with CRAR at 18.10% and CET-I ratio at 15.69%, providing a strong foundation to support future growth.\nThe Bank’s global business reached a historic high of `23.86 lakh crore, comprising global deposits of `13.07 lakh crore and global advances of `10.79 lakh crore.\nAsset quality improved further with Gross NPA declining to 2.82%, Net NPA improving to 0.48%, Provision Coverage Ratio strengthening to 95.03%, and credit cost moderating to 0.23%.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c946","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":369,"text":"Asset quality improved further with Gross NPA declining to 2.82%, Net NPA improving to 0.48%, Provision Coverage Ratio strengthening to 95.03%, and credit cost moderating to 0.23%.\nThe focus was on a disciplined approach towards cash management, ensuring profitability, conserving capital and improving key financial metric such as Return on Capital Employed (ROCE) while safeguarding the Bank’s financial health. We shed bulk deposits of close to around `71,000 crores during the financial year 2025-26. Our CASA ratio jumped to 35.20% in FY2025-26 from 33.51% last year, reflecting the success of our new Ecosystem banking initiative. Also, retail term deposit plus CASA increased by almost `1 lakh crores with a YoY growth of close to 11.40%. Hence, we maintain a good strategic mix of CASA & RTD to bulk deposit, which is 79:21 now vs 73:27 in March 2025.\nThe Board has recommended a record dividend of `5 per equity share for FY2025-26, reflecting our commitment towards shareholder value creation.\n2. Major Milestones Achieved\nFY2025-26 witnessed significant progress across business performance, technology, sustainability, and institutional recognition.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c947","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":369,"text":"FY2025-26 witnessed significant progress across business performance, technology, sustainability, and institutional recognition.\nThe Bank’s market capitalization crossed more than `1.0 lakh crore, reflecting enhanced confidence among investors and stakeholders. The Bank received prestigious recognitions including Best MSME Bank Award, IBA Best Technology Bank of the Year Award, and recognition for Best Security Practices in the Banking Sector. The Bank was also recognized as a “Role Model Organization” at the NCPEDP–Mphasis Universal Design Awards for its inclusive banking initiatives and accessible digital solutions.\nA healthy Credit-Deposit ratio of 83.27% also reflects a balanced growth and prudent liquidity management. Gross advances crossed the significant milestone of `10 lakh crore, demonstrating the strength of our franchise and customer trust. The Bank maintained a balanced growth approach with RAM (Retail, Agriculture and MSME) advances reaching `5.98 lakh crore. Our strategic goal remains to boost high NIM accretive RAM lending, with the RAM to corporate lending ratio improving to 55:45 in FY2025-26 versus 54:46\n17 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c948","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":370,"text":"GRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nDuring the year, the Bank witnessed improvement in its external recognition profile with rating upgrades from leading global rating agencies including S&P and Fitch, reflecting enhanced financial strength, improved fundamentals, and institutional resilience.\nbeyond individual relationships to engage with the wider ecosystem around institutional anchors, enabling deeper relationships and stronger CASA mobilisation.\nThe focus remains on Government entities, public sector organisations, corporates, educational institutions, healthcare organisations, MSMEs, and emerging business clusters. The efforts have already begun to bear fruit with the spike seen in our CASA ratio in 2025-26 and threshold shift in absolute CASA levels.\nThe Bank further strengthened its sustainability credentials with improvement in ESG ratings, including CARE ESG Rating of CareEdge ESG 1+ and Moody’s Sustainability Quality Score of SQS2 under the Sustainable Finance Framework.\nThese achievements reflect the collective efforts of our employees and the continued trust of our customers and stakeholders.\nStrategy and Business Excellence","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c949","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":370,"text":"Strategy and Business Excellence\nThe Corporate Strategy and Business Excellence Vertical has been strengthened as the central platform for transformation by integrating strategy, analytics, business excellence initiatives, project management, and execution monitoring. By integrating key functions such as Research, Analytics, CR&MIS, and EASE into a unified framework, the Bank aims to enable data-driven decision-making, improve coordination, and enhance operational efficiency. This transformation is expected to support faster execution, better customer experience, and sustainable business growth..\n3. Long term strategy\nUnion Bank @2031 represents our aspiration to build a future-ready, agile, digitally empowered, and high- performing institution. We prepared a 5-year Strategy document in this regard to define our key aspirations and identify key strategic levers to achieve the same.\nOur strategy is anchored around four key pillars:\nDigital | Strategy | Reconciliation | Ecosystem Banking\nThese pillars aim to deepen customer relationships, improve operational efficiency, strengthen execution capabilities, and create sustainable value.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c950","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":370,"text":"These pillars aim to deepen customer relationships, improve operational efficiency, strengthen execution capabilities, and create sustainable value.\nRevamped Corporate Strategy and Business Excellence Vertical, has taken several initiatives such as Project Muskaan, Union Innovation League etc. as structured Bottom-up engagement approach to strengthen field support, streamline processes, enhance digital oversight, deepen compliance & security posture and accelerate execution across the Bank. The Bank undertook several transformative initiatives during the year to strengthen branch productivity, operational resilience, and workforce capabilities. Simultaneously, branch workload was reduced through centralization, automation, digital sourcing, and streamlined workflows.\nAs India progresses towards the vision of Viksit Bharat 2047, the Bank is committed to supporting the country’s growth journey across infrastructure, manufacturing, MSMEs, renewable energy, logistics, technology-led sectors, and emerging industries.\nAs part of the Union Bank @2031 vision, the Bank has identified clear strategic aspirations:\n Doubling the MSME business, reinforcing the Bank’s commitment towards entrepreneurship, employment generation, and India’s growth engine.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c951","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":370,"text":" Doubling the MSME business, reinforcing the Bank’s commitment towards entrepreneurship, employment generation, and India’s growth engine.\n Achieving a CASA ratio of 38%, strengthening the quality and stability of the deposit franchise through deeper customer relationships and ecosystem-led banking.\nDigital Transformation – At the Core of Business Strategy\nTechnology, data, and innovation continue to support the Banks transformation journey while maintaining strong cyber security, data governance, and customer protection frameworks. During FY2025-26, the Bank enhanced its digital acquisition and servicing capabilities through digital onboarding journeys, ecosystem-led banking solutions, process automation, and scalable digital platforms. Investments in AI-enabled solutions, advanced analytics, hyper-automation, reconciliation systems, and data-driven decision making are helping improve customer experience, operational efficiency, and risk management capabilities. The Bank also continued to strengthen its digital payments and transaction banking ecosystem in line with India’s rapidly expanding UPI-led digital economy.\n Improving operational efficiency with a target to bring the Cost-to-Income ratio below 45%, supported by Ease of Doing Business, digital transformation by creating a ‘Digital Bank within the Bank’, process optimisation, automation, and enhanced productivity.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c952","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":370,"text":" Improving operational efficiency with a target to bring the Cost-to-Income ratio below 45%, supported by Ease of Doing Business, digital transformation by creating a ‘Digital Bank within the Bank’, process optimisation, automation, and enhanced productivity.\n We stay focused on compliance and assurance as key functions of the Bank.\n In an attempt to bolster investor returns, our target is to achieve Return on Assets of 1.50%\nUnion Bank @2031 envisions becoming a future-ready, agile, and digitally empowered institution, building on its strong legacy of trust and nation-building.\n4. Strategic functions\nDigital transformation remains central to the Bank’s business strategy, enabling scalable growth, enhanced customer experience, and operational efficiency. The establishment of the Digital Business Vertical has strengthened the Bank’s focus on digital distribution, customer journeys, analytics- driven decision making, automation, and next-generation banking solutions.\nEcosystem Banking vertical launched\nThe launch of the Ecosystem Banking Vertical represents a strategic move towards relationship-led and flow-based banking. Institutions today operate within wider ecosystems comprising employees, vendors, contractors, service providers, customers, and beneficiaries. The Bank is moving\n18 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c953","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":371,"text":"Financials Reports Notice\nRecovery Transformation\ncommitted to strict underwriting discipline, stronger early warning systems, enhanced transaction monitoring, reconciliation strengthening, and proactive control of slippages and stress formation. Integrated verticals for reconciliation, AML, fraud monitoring, audit, compliance, and risk management reflect the Bank’s forward-looking approach toward governance and assurance.\nStrengthening asset quality and improving recovery outcomes remained key priorities. The Bank enhanced recovery mechanisms through focused monitoring, accountability, faster resolution, and greater engagement across recovery channels.\nDuring FY2025-26: 2,013 properties were sold under SARFAESI with sale value of approximately `1,414 crore. 68,702 accounts involving around `2,000 crore were settled through Lok Adalat.\n6. Social responsibility\nUnion Bank of India remains committed to inclusive banking and nation-building through initiatives in financial inclusion, accessibility, digital empowerment, education, healthcare, and community development. The Bank continues to align its efforts with national priorities of inclusive growth, sustainability, and empowerment. The Bank’s CSR initiatives through Union Bank Social Foundation Trust (UBISFT) focused on inclusive growth across health, education, and community development.\nMSME Clusters","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c954","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":371,"text":"MSME Clusters\nMSMEs continue to remain a key growth engine. Through MSME Processing Centres, MSME-focused branches, and cluster-based financing initiatives, the Bank continues to provide customised solutions and strengthen support for entrepreneurship and inclusive growth. We have formulated specialised cluster-specific schemes tailored to the unique requirements, opportunities and challenges of each cluster, ensuring that we move beyond a “one-size-fits- all” approach.\nWomen empowerment remained a key pillar of the Bank’s agenda. During Q4FY26 alone, the Bank sanctioned 3,080 applications amounting to Rs 560 crore under the Union Nari Shakti scheme. Through its 42 Rural Self Employment Training Institutes (RSETIs), the Bank has cumulatively trained over 4.33 lakh individuals with a settlement ratio of 73%, supporting entrepreneurship and livelihood generation. Financial inclusion remained another key focus area. Under PMJDY, the Bank expanded outreach to 3.40 crore accounts with balances of Rs 15,964 crore as on March 31, 2026. The Bank also recorded strong participation under PMJJBY, PMSBY, APY, PM Mudra Yojana, PM Vishwakarma, PM SVANidhi, and PM Surya Ghar schemes.\nReconciliation Excellence","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c955","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":371,"text":"Reconciliation Excellence\nThe Bank has established Reconciliation Vertical to strengthen timely reconciliation/ resolution of transactions and improve the customer satisfaction. During FY2025-26, the Bank reconciled over 2,030 crore digital transactions, handling approximately 5.56 crore transactions daily.\nHuman Resources Transformation\nThe Bank continued to invest in capability building, leadership development, employee engagement, and creation of a future-ready workforce.\n5. Strengthening assurance function –\nDuring FY26, the Bank strengthened its sustainability and ESG agenda through enhanced green financing, robust climate risk governance, and responsible banking practices. The green finance portfolio reached `37,435 crore, including `33,572 crore in renewable energy financing, reflecting the Bank’s commitment to sustainable and responsible growth.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c956","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":371,"text":"Strengthening assurance functions remains a strategic priority as the Bank continues its transformation journey. The Bank has enhanced governance frameworks across Risk Management, Compliance, Internal Audit, Cyber Security, Transaction Monitoring, ESG, Credit Monitoring, and Vigilance. The function is evolving from traditional control-based approaches to data-driven, analytics-enabled systems that improve transparency, enhance decision- making, and support sustainable growth, while reinforcing customer trust and regulatory alignment.\n7. Outlook\nThe Bank remains focused on sustainable growth, customer excellence, digital transformation, strong governance, and responsible banking. With a strong balance sheet, robust capital position, improving asset quality, and a clear strategic roadmap, Union Bank of India is well positioned to support India’s growth journey. I express my sincere gratitude to our shareholders, customers, regulators, Government of India, Reserve Bank of India, and all stakeholders for their continued trust and support. I also acknowledge the dedication of every employee of Union Bank of India whose commitment continues to strengthen our institution. Together, we remain committed to building a stronger Union Bank of India and contributing to a stronger India.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c957","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":371,"text":"The three lines of defence model ensures effective risk ownership, independent monitoring, and objective assurance. The Bank continues to strengthen credit risk management, operational controls, market risk oversight, fraud prevention, ESG risk management, and regulatory compliance. Technology-enabled transaction monitoring, analytics-driven supervision, and machine learning-based solutions support fraud detection and customer protection.\nThe Internal Audit function continues to provide independent assurance on controls and governance. During FY2025- 26, Risk Based Internal Audit was completed across 5,760 branches and units, strengthening operational discipline.\nWith heartfelt best wishes,\nCyber Security remains a strategic priority, with robust security frameworks supporting safe and resilient digital banking operations. Together, these assurance functions provide a strong foundation for responsible growth, resilience, and stakeholder confidence. The Bank remains\nAsheesh Pandey Managing Director & CEO\n19 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nValue Creation Story: Building India's Sustainable Future\nExecutive Summary: Bank's Value Creation Journey","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c958","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":372,"text":"Executive Summary: Bank's Value Creation Journey\nUnion Bank of India's FY 2025–26 performance exemplifies how integrated thinking transforms banking from transactional relationships to sustainable value creation. The Bank delivered a Net Profit of `18,697 crore, representing a growth of 3.95% over the prior year's `17,987 crore. This sustained earnings momentum — achieved even as operating profit moderated on account of a declining interest rate environment — demonstrates how strategic integration across all six capitals drives inclusive growth, environmental stewardship, and digital innovation simultaneously.\nFY 2025–26 was a landmark year in the Bank's transformation journey. Global advances crossed the `10 lakh crore milestone for the first time, touching `10,78,611 crore. Asset quality reached its best level in more than a decade, with the Gross NPA ratio declining to 2.82% — an improvement of 78 basis points over FY25 and 194 basis points over FY24. The Provision Coverage Ratio strengthened to 95.03%, providing a robust buffer against future credit losses. Capital adequacy remained strong at a CRAR of 18.10%, well above the Basel III regulatory minimum of 11.5%.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c959","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":372,"text":"Bank's Core Value Proposition: Union Bank of India is India's catalyst for sustainable economic transformation, leveraging its extensive network of 8,697 branches and 28,035 Business Correspondent Points to deliver financial inclusion while pioneering climate-positive financing and digital innovation. The Bank's 107-year legacy of service to millions of individuals, farmers, small businesses, and communities continues to guide its purpose as a responsible public sector institution.\nBank's Value Creation Model\nUnion Bank of India's value creation model is premised on the integrated deployment of its six capitals — Financial, Manufactured, Intellectual, Human, Social and Relationship, and Natural — across its core business activities. The interplay of these capitals through banking operations generates sustainable financial, social, and environmental value for all stakeholders.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c960","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":372,"text":"The Bank's inputs comprise financial capital in the form of equity, deposits, and borrowings; manufactured capital in the form of its branch, ATM, and BC network; intellectual capital comprising digital platforms, technology systems, and cybersecurity capabilities; human capital in the form of 73,885 trained and motivated employees; social and relationship capital built through customer trust, government partnerships, and community engagement; and natural capital aligned with the Bank's commitment to climate-positive finance and net-zero operational goals by 2035.\nThese inputs are transformed through the Bank's business activities — corporate and retail credit delivery, financial inclusion, digital banking, treasury operations, and ESG-linked financing — to generate outcomes that create value across all stakeholder groups. The Bank's outcomes include a Net Profit of `18,697 crore, digital business mobilisation of `1,18,000+ crore, a green finance portfolio of `37,435 crore, priority sector lending of 41.04% of ANBC, and training of 4.33 lakh individuals through RSETIs.\nOur Six Capitals — Inputs at a Glance (March 2026)\nCapital Key Inputs / Resources\nFinancial Capital Global Deposits `13,06,891 Cr | CRAR 18.10% | Strong retained earnings | GoI equity (74.76%)\nManufactured Capital 8,697 branches | 8,656 ATMs | 28,035 BC Points | Overseas presence (Dubai, Sydney)","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c961","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":372,"text":"Manufactured Capital 8,697 branches | 8,656 ATMs | 28,035 BC Points | Overseas presence (Dubai, Sydney)\nIntellectual Capital 15+ end-to-end digital journeys | AI/ML platforms | Cybersecurity CoE | Finacle CBS\nHuman Capital 73,885 employees | 31.24 learning hrs/employee/quarter | ISO 30414:2025 framework\nSocial & Relationship Capital Customer trust | Priority sector commitment | Government partnerships | 42 RSETIs | UBSFT\nNatural Capital `37,435 Cr green portfolio | PCAF signatory | Net-Zero 2035 | ESG governance framework\n20 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nValue Creation Outcomes FY 2025–26\nOutcome Category Key Achievements Stakeholder\nImpacted\nFinancial Value Net Profit `18,697 Cr | RoA 1.25% | RoE 15.86% | EPS `24.49 | GNPA2.82% Shareholders\nDigital Value `1,18,000+ Cr digital business | 15+ digital journeys | 75+ lakh UVConn users Customers\nInclusive Value PSL 41.04% | `1,83,394 Cr agri credit | UV 3.40 cr accounts | SHGs `33,212 Cr Communities\nSocial Value CSR `61.69 Cr approved | RSETI 4.33 lakh trained | PM Vishwakarma 15,191 artisans Society\nGreen portfolio `37,435 Cr | Renewable energy `33,572 Cr | Net-Zero 2035 Environment\nEnvironmental Value\nHuman Value 23.10 lakh learning hours | 42 leadership programs | 867 executives trained Employees","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c962","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":373,"text":"Human Value 23.10 lakh learning hours | 42 leadership programs | 867 executives trained Employees\nEconomic Value `10,78,611 Cr credit | 9.74% advances growth | `5,484 Cr tax provision Economy/Govt\nMateriality & Stakeholder Priorities\nIn the Bank's recent ESG materiality assessment, 15 key ESG topics were examined, reflecting the evolving expectations of Union Bank of India and its stakeholders, and the shift from perception-based to impact-driven materiality. Based on the resultant Materiality Matrix, ESG topics on Business Ethics & Corporate Governance, Risk Management, Regulatory Compliance, Climate Change Risk & Resilience, and Data Privacy, Cybersecurity & Digitization and Employee Health, Well-being, Attracting & Retaining talent are identified as being of highest importance to both the business and stakeholders.\nMateriality Priority ESG Topics Stakeholder\nImportance\nMost Material Business Ethics & Corporate Governance | Data Privacy & Cybersecurity | Climate Change Risk & Resilience | Risk Management | Regulatory Compliance | Employee Health & Well-being, Attracting & Retaining talent\nVery High\nHighly Material Customer Orientation & Fair Lending High\nMaterial Green Operations | Social & Financial Inclusion | Diversity, Equity & Inclusion Medium-High\nHuman Capital Development | Carbon Emissions Medium\nModerately Material\nModerate","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c963","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":373,"text":"Moderate\nEmerging Energy & Resource Management | Supply Chain Management | Product Innovation\nStakeholder Value Propositions\nStakeholder Value Delivered\nCustomers Enhanced digital experience through 15+ digital journeys | Inclusive access to banking services | Sustainable products aligned with lifestyle choices\nShareholders Superior risk-adjusted returns | Net Profit `18,697 Cr | EPS `24.49 | Book Value `154.41/ share | Transparent governance\nEmployees Career development | 23.10 lakh learning hours | Inclusive workplace | ISO 30414:2025 human capital reporting\nCommunities Economic development | Financial inclusion | Environmental stewardship | RSETI training for 4.33 lakh rural youth\nRegulators Compliance excellence | Risk management leadership | Market stability | 100% regulatory compliance maintained\nEnvironment Climate-positive financing `37,435 Cr | Net-Zero 2035 commitment | PCAF signatory | ESG integration in credit decisions\n21 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nMateriality Matrix\nMATERIALITY MATRIX\nClimate Change Risk & Resilience\nBusiness Ethics,Corporate\nGovernance\nData Privacy,Cybersecurity,Digitization\nRegulatory Compliance\nRisk Management\nEmployee Health,Well-being, Attracting &\nRetaining talent\nGreen Operations, Environmental\nIMPORTANCE TO STAKEHOLDERS\nCustomer Orientation & Satisfaction, Fair","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c964","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":374,"text":"Customer Orientation & Satisfaction, Fair\nResponsibility Human Capital Development\nResponsible Lending & Pricing\nDiversity, Equity,Inclusion,Human Rights\nEnergy, Resource Management, Supply Chain\nManagement\nSocial & Financial inclusion, Community\nDevelopment\nCarbon Emissions\nFinancing decisions\nProduct Innovation\n3.3 3.4 3.5 3.6 3.7 3.8 3.9 4\nIMPORTANCE TO BUSINESS\nCapital Interconnectedness\nUnion Bank of India's six capitals are deeply interconnected, with value created in one capital reinforcing and amplifying value creation across others. This integrated approach distinguishes the Bank's model from a purely financial profit-maximisation framework and positions it as a driver of holistic, long-term stakeholder value.\nCapital How It Enables Other Capitals\nFinancial Capital Funds technology and digital transformation (Intellectual Capital) | Finances branch expansion and modernisation (Manufactured Capital) | Enables CSR investments and RSETI operations (Social Capital) | Supports green bond issuance and green finance growth (Natural Capital)\nIntellectual Capital Digital platforms reduce costs and improve efficiency (Financial Capital) | Enables scalable customer service delivery (Manufactured Capital) | Powers AI/ML-driven learning systems (Human Capital) | ESG data analytics enable climate risk assessment (Natural Capital)","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c965","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":374,"text":"Intellectual Capital Digital platforms reduce costs and improve efficiency (Financial Capital) | Enables scalable customer service delivery (Manufactured Capital) | Powers AI/ML-driven learning systems (Human Capital) | ESG data analytics enable climate risk assessment (Natural Capital)\nManufactured Capital Extensive branch/BC network enables financial inclusion (Social Capital) | Platform for deploying digital innovations (Intellectual Capital) | Green building and renewable energy at offices (Natural Capital) | Revenue from banking operations (Financial Capital)\nHuman Capital Skilled employees drive credit growth and customer satisfaction (Financial Capital) | Employee innovation supports digital transformation (Intellectual Capital) | Community programs and financial literacy (Social Capital)\nSocial & Relationship Capital Customer trust drives business growth (Financial Capital) | Market intelligence informs product innovation (Intellectual Capital) | Social license for expansion and operations (Manufactured Capital)\nNatural Capital Green finance generates new revenue streams (Financial Capital) | Climate risk assessment enhances credit quality (Financial Capital) | Green products attract ESG-conscious customers (Social Capital)\n22 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nBank's Response Strategy — Four Strategic Pillars","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c966","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":375,"text":"Bank's Response Strategy — Four Strategic Pillars\n1. Financial Resilience & Profitable Growth\nThe Bank focused on sustaining profitability through improving asset quality, optimising the cost of funds, growing fee- based income, and deploying capital efficiently. The dramatic decline in provisions from `13,103 crore to `9,922 crore was the critical driver enabling net profit growth even as operating profit moderated. The GNPA ratio declined to its lowest level in over a decade at 2.82%, and fresh slippages were reduced by 30.8% to `7,867 crore.\n2. Digital-First Customer Experience\nThe Bank's shift from a product-centric to a customer-centric model was operationalised through the expansion of 15+ digital lending and deposit journeys. Digital business mobilisation surged to `1,18,000+ crore, demonstrating the commercial impact of the Bank's technology investments. The UVConn WhatsApp Banking platform achieved 75+ lakh users with 4.50 crore requests serviced during the year.\n3. Inclusive Banking & Priority Sector Excellence\nAchieving 41.04% priority sector lending against a 40% benchmark, the Bank maintained its leadership in financial inclusion. Agricultural credit reached `1,83,394 crore, with credit to Small and Marginal Farmers at `97,787 crore — well above the 10% ANBC benchmark. The Bank's BC network expanded to 28,035 points, serving customers in the remotest geographies.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c967","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":375,"text":"4. Sustainable Banking & ESG Leadership\nThe green finance portfolio grew by 30.7% to `37,435 crore, with renewable energy financing reaching `33,572 crore. As India's first major bank to become a PCAF signatory, Union Bank demonstrated leadership in transparent climate risk reporting. The planned `5,000 crore Green Infrastructure Bond issuance is expected to position the Bank among few of India's largest green bond issuers.\n23 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nFinancial Capital\nSustaining Profitability | Preserving Capital | Delivering Stakeholder Returns\nIntroduction\nFinancial Capital comprises the pool of funds available to Union Bank of India — equity capital, deposits, borrowings, and retained earnings — that are deployed across business activities to generate financial and non-financial value for all stakeholders. Strong financial capital is the foundation that enables the Bank to invest in digital transformation, expand its branch network, develop its human resources, finance sustainable projects, and fulfil its financial inclusion mandate.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c968","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":376,"text":"In FY 2025–26, Union Bank of India delivered a sustained financial performance characterised by net profit growth of 3.95% to `18,697 crore, a decade-low GNPA ratio of 2.82%, and a strengthened CRAR of 18.10%. While operating profit moderated from `31,090 crore to `28,620 crore on account of NIM compression in a declining rate environment, the sharp decline in provisions by 24.28% to `9,922 crore — reflecting significantly improved portfolio quality — enabled net profit growth. The Bank's balance sheet crossed `20 lakh crore in total assets, with global advances crossing the `10 lakh crore milestone for the first time.\nFinancial Performance at a Glance — FY 2025–26\nKey Metric FY 2024–25 FY 2025–26 YoY Change Net Profit (`Crore) 17,987 18,697 +3.95% Operating Profit (`Crore) 31,090 28,620 -7.95% Net Interest Income (`Crore) 37,214 36,659 -1.49% GNPA Ratio (%) 3.60% 2.82% -78 bps NNPA Ratio (%) 0.63% 0.48% -15 bps Provision Coverage Ratio (%) 94.61% 95.03% +42 bps CRAR (%) 18.02% 18.10% +8 bps CET-1 Ratio (%) 14.98% 15.69% +71 bps Return on Assets (%) 1.26% 1.25% -1 bp Return on Equity (%) 17.20% 15.86% -134 bps EPS (`) 23.56 24.49 +3.9% Book Value Per Share (`) 136.98 154.41 +12.7% Credit Cost (%) 0.77% 0.23% -54 bps Price-to-Book Ratio 0.94x 1.06x +0.12x\nIncome & Profitability\nProfit & Loss Statement — Annual Comparison","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c969","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":376,"text":"Profit & Loss Statement — Annual Comparison\nParameter FY 2024–25 (` Crore) FY 2025–26 (` Crore) YoY Change (%) Interest Income 1,05,909 1,05,992 +0.08% Interest Expense 68,695 69,333 +0.93% Net Interest Income (NII) 37,214 36,659 -1.49% Non-Interest Income 19,813 19,435 -1.91% — of which: Fee-Based Income 10,285 9,743 -5.26% — of which: Recoveries from Written-off Accounts 4,311 3,997 -7.28% Operating Income(NII+non interest Income) 57,027 56,094 -1.64% Operating Expenses 25,937 27,474 +5.93% Operating Profit 31,090 28,620 -7.94% Provisions 13,103 9,922 -24.28% Net Profit 17,987 18,697 +3.95% Tax Provision 5492 5,484 -0.14% Cost-to-Income Ratio 45.48% 48.98% +350 bps Net Interest Margin (NIM) 2.91% 2.70% -21 bps\n24 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c970","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":377,"text":"Financials Reports Notice\nUnion Bank of India delivered a net profit of `18,697 crore in FY 2025–26 — a growth of 3.95% over the prior year — reflecting sustained earnings momentum despite a moderation in operating profit. Net Interest Income remained broadly stable at `36,659 crore, with the marginal compression of `555 crore driven by a declining yield-on-advances environment as the RBI's easing cycle gathered pace. Provisions declined sharply by 24.28% to `9,922 crore from `13,103 crore in FY25, reflecting the significant improvement in portfolio quality, dramatically lower fresh slippages, and reduced credit cost of 0.23% — one of the lowest levels the Bank has achieved. Revenue composition remained anchored by Net Interest Income at 64.28% of operating income, complemented by fee-based income of `9,743 crore and recoveries from written-off accounts of `3,997 crore, demonstrating a well-diversified income architecture.\nQuarterly Performance — FY 2025–26\nQuarter NII (`Cr) Operating Profit (`Cr) Net Profit (`Cr) NIM (%) RoA (%)\nQ1 FY26 (Jun-25) 9,113 6,909 4,116 2.76% 1.11%\nQ2 FY26 (Sep-25) 8,812 6,814 4,249 2.67% 1.16%\nQ3 FY26 (Dec-25) 9,328 6,942 5,017 2.76% 1.35%\nQ4 FY26 (Mar-26) 9,406 7,955 5,316 2.64% 1.36%\nFY 2025–26 (Full Year) 36,659 28,620 18,697 2.70% 1.25%\nQ4 FY25 (Mar-25) (for comparison) 9,514 7,700 4,985 2.87% 1.35%","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c971","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":377,"text":"Q4 FY25 (Mar-25) (for comparison) 9,514 7,700 4,985 2.87% 1.35%\nThe Bank's quarterly trajectory demonstrated a robust recovery through FY 2025–26. Q4 FY26 recorded the highest quarterly net profit in five quarters at `5,316 crore, and operating profit rebounded sharply to `7,955 crore — the strongest result since Q2 FY25. The strong Q4 rebound, driven by credit cost at a record low of 0.16% in the quarter and RoA at 1.36%, validates the sustainability of the Bank's asset quality improvement journey.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c972","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":377,"text":"Balance Sheet & Business Scale\nBusiness at Scale — Key Balance Sheet Items\nParameter Mar-25 (`Crore) Sep-25 (`Crore) Mar-26 (`Crore) YoY Growth\nGlobal Deposits 12,72,247 12,34,621 13,06,891 +2.72%\nGlobal Advances 9,82,894 9,75,207 10,78,611 +9.74%\nRAM Advances 5,31,679 5,54,306 5,98,484 +12.56%\nLarge Corporate Advances 4,05,670 3,79,420 4,31,609 +6.39%\nOverseas Advances 36,919 32,934 37,657 +2.00%\nOthers 8,626 8,547 10,861 +25.91%\nCASA Deposits 4,26,325 4,01,854 4,60,077 +7.92%\n— Savings Deposits 3,39,916 3,38,844 3,75,990 10.61%\nCASA Ratio (%) 33.51% 32.56% 35.21% +170 bps\nTotal Investments 3,56,954 3,47,965 3,31,682 -7.07%\nThe Bank's balance sheet expanded meaningfully in FY 2025–26, with global advances crossing the `10 lakh crore milestone to reach `10,78,611 crore, registering year-on-year growth of 9.74%. Global deposits grew by 2.72% to `13,06,891 crore. RAM (Retail, Agriculture, MSME) advances grew strongly by 12.56% to `5,98,484 crore, reflecting the Bank's deliberate strategy to grow the higher-yielding RAM book. The CASA ratio improved by 169 basis points to 35.20%, driven by focused deposit mobilisation campaigns and improved current account acquisition. Global Business stood at `23.86 lakh crore as on March 2026, a growth of 5.78%.\n25 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c973","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":378,"text":"GRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nDeposit Composition & Liquidity Metrics (March 2026)\nDeposit Parameter Value\nCASA + Retail Term Deposits (` Crore) 10,29,852 Bulk Term Deposits (` Crore) 2,76,436 Savings Deposits (` Crore) 3,75,990 CASA Deposits (` Crore) 4,60,077 Average Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) 113.83% Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR) 115.34% 2-Year CAGR — Global Deposits 3.44% 2-Year CAGR — Domestic Deposits 4.37% Global Credit-Deposit Ratio 82.53% Domestic Credit-Deposit Ratio 79.69%\nAdvances Portfolio — Segment-wise Performance\nDiversified Credit Portfolio (`Crore)\nSegment Mar-25 Sep-25 Dec-25 Mar-26 YoY (%) QoQ (%)\nRetail 2,16,777 2,38,506 2,45,541 2,53,083 16.75% 3.07%\nAgriculture 1,78,479 1,68,405 1,75,294 1,83,394 2.75% 4.62%\nMSME 1,36,423 1,47,395 1,56,203 1,62,007 18.75% 3.72%\nRAM Advances 5,31,679 5,54,306 5,77,038 5,98,484 12.56% 3.72%\nLarge Corporate 4,05,670 3,79,420 3,94,999 4,31,609 6.39% 9.27%\nOverseas Advances 36,919 32,934 36,165 37,657 2.00% 4.13%\nOthers 8,626 8,547 8,682 10,861 25.91% 25.10%\nGlobal Advances 9,82,894 9,75,207 10,16,884 10,78,611 9.74% 6.07%\nPortfolio Mix Mar-25 Mar-26 Change\nRAM Share in Domestic Advances 56.20% 57.49% RAM dominant\nLarge Corporate & Others Share 43.80% 42.51% Balanced portfolio\nDomestic Advances 2Y CAGR — 9.16% Consistent growth\nRAM 2Y CAGR — 11.36% Higher-yielding growth\nRetail Advances — Product-wise (`Crore)","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c974","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":378,"text":"Retail Advances — Product-wise (`Crore)\nProduct Mar-25 (` Cr) Mar-26 (` Cr) Growth (` Cr) % Growth\nHome Loans 94,825 1,07,687 12,862 13.56%\nVehicle Loans 22,462 28,415 5,953 26.50%\nEducation Loans 15,912 18,166 2,254 14.17%\nMortgage Loans 16,152 18,023 1,871 11.58%\nPersonal Loans 11,225 11,505 280 2.49%\nOthers 56,202 69,288 13,086 23.28%\nTotal Retail Advances 2,16,777 2,53,083 36,306 16.75%\nRetail lending delivered an outstanding performance in FY 2025–26, with total retail advances growing by 16.75% to ` 2,53,083 crore. Vehicle Loans recorded the highest growth at 26.50%, driven by the launch of digital vehicle loan journeys for both Existing-to-Bank (ETB) and New-to-Bank (NTB) customers. Home Loans grew strongly by 13.56% to ` 1,07,687 crore, supported\n26 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nby builder tie-ups, festive campaigns, and the Business Retention Discount scheme. Education Loans grew 14.17% to ` 18,166 crore, with 721 digital education loan accounts sanctioned, including 192 loans to female students of premier institutions. PAN India, Retail Loan Points (RLPs/CPCs) sanctioned retail loans of ` 50,064 crore during FY 2025–26. Additionally, 51,700 digital PM Surya Ghar (rooftop solar) loans were sanctioned out of a total 69,601 sanctioned under this initiative.\nMSME Advances — Category-wise ( ` Crore)","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c975","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":379,"text":"MSME Advances — Category-wise ( ` Crore)\nMSME Category Mar-25 (` Cr) Mar-26 (` Cr) % Growth\nMicro Enterprises 68,416 87,029 27.21% Small Enterprises 43,896 43,947 0.12% Medium Enterprises 24,111 31,031 28.70% Total MSME Advances 1,36,423 1,62,007 18.75%\nMSME advances registered an impressive growth of 18.75% to ` 1,62,007 crore in FY 2025–26, driven by expanded credit guarantee coverage, digital MSME journeys, and the Bank's proactive outreach programmes. The Credit Guarantee coverage was significantly expanded: CGTMSE sanctions reached 2,13,516 accounts with a guarantee amount of ` 22,589 crore, while CGFMU (including Agri Allied) covered 4,14,113 accounts with ` 29,549 crore — totalling 6,27,629 accounts and ` 52,138 crore in guarantee coverage.\nCapital\nYield, Cost & Margin Metrics\nRatio FY 2023–24 FY 2024–25 FY 2025–26 Change (FY25FY26)\nYield on Advances (YoA) 8.73% 8.74% 8.27% -47 bps\nYield on Investments (YoI) 6.70% 6.87% 6.79% -8 bps\nYield on Funds (YoF) 7.51% 7.44% 7.06% -38 bps\nCost of Deposits (CoD) 5.22% 5.52% 5.39% -13 bps\nCost of Funds (CoF) 4.76% 4.83% 4.62% -21 bps\nNet Interest Margin (NIM) 3.10% 2.91% 2.70% -21 bps\nProfitability & Efficiency Ratios\nRatio FY 2023–24 FY 2024–25 FY 2025–26 Change (FY25FY26)\nReturn on Assets (RoA) 1.03% 1.26% 1.25% -1 bp\nReturn on Equity (RoE) 15.58% 17.20% 15.86% -134 bps\nEPS (`) 18.95 23.56 24.49 +3.9%","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c976","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":379,"text":"EPS (`) 18.95 23.56 24.49 +3.9%\nBook Value per Share (`) 114.76 136.98 154.41 +12.7%\nCost-to-Income Ratio 46.42% 45.48% 48.98% +350 bps\nCredit Cost 0.74% 0.77% 0.23% -54 bps\nPrice-to-Book Value 1.34x 0.94x 1.06x +0.12x\nPrice-to-Earnings (P/E) 8.10 5.46 6.70 +\nAsset Quality — Preservation of Financial Capital\nAsset quality directly determines the durability of the Bank's financial capital stock. Union Bank of India has demonstrated sustained improvement across all asset quality parameters over the four-year period FY23–FY26, reflecting strengthened credit underwriting, active resolution of legacy stressed assets, and prudent provisioning. The GNPA ratio has improved by 471 basis points from 7.53% in FY23 to 2.82% in FY26 — one of the sharpest turnarounds among large public sector banks.\n27 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nNPA Movement — FY 2025–26 vs FY 2024–25\nFY 2025–26 (`\nNPA Parameter FY 2024–25 (`\nCrore) Change\nCrore)\nGross NPA — Opening 43,098 35,350 -` 7,748 Cr\nFresh Slippages 12,073 8451 -` 3,505 Cr\nCash Recovery 4,924 4,404 -` 520 Cr\nUpgradation 3,263 2,430 -` 833 Cr\nWrite-offs 11,634 6,567 -` 5,067 Cr\nGross NPA — Closing 35,350 30,401 -` 4,949 Cr\nNet NPA (` Crore) 5,969 5,067 -` 902 Cr\nGNPA Ratio (%) 3.60% 2.82% -78 bps\nNNPA Ratio (%) 0.63% 0.48% -15 bps\nProvision Coverage Ratio (%) 94.61% 95.03% +42 bps","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c977","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":380,"text":"Provision Coverage Ratio (%) 94.61% 95.03% +42 bps\nSlippage / Delinquency Ratio (%) 1.40% 0.89% -51 bps\nCredit Cost (%) 0.77% 0.23% -54 bps\nAsset Quality Multi-Year Trend\nAsset Quality Metric FY 2022–23 FY 2023–24 FY 2024–25 FY 2025–26\nGNPA Ratio (%) 7.53% 4.76% 3.60% 2.82%\nNNPA Ratio (%) 1.70% 1.03% 0.63% 0.48%\nProvision Coverage Ratio (%) 90.34% 92.69% 94.61% 95.03%\nCredit Cost (%) 1.64% 0.74% 0.77% 0.23%\nSlippage Ratio (%) 1.97% 1.59% 1.40% 0.89%\nGross NPA declined from ` 43,098 crore in March 2024 to ` 30,401 crore in March 2026 — a reduction of `12,697 crore in two years. Fresh slippages declined sharply from `12,073 crore in FY25 to ` 8,451 crore in FY26 (-30.01%), indicating significant improvement in credit underwriting and early-warning systems. Net NPA reduced to ` 5,067 crore, with the NNPA ratio at 0.48%. Credit Cost declined dramatically to 0.23% — one of the lowest levels the Bank has achieved — reflecting both the quality of fresh underwriting and strong recovery momentum from existing stressed assets.\nCapital Adequacy & Capitalisation\nCapital Parameter Mar-25 Mar-26 Change\nCRAR (Total Capital Adequacy Ratio) 18.02% 18.10% +8 bps\nCommon Equity Tier-1 (CET-1) Ratio 14.98% 15.69% +71 bps\nTier-2 Capital Ratio 1.77% 1.47% -30 bps\nTotal Capital — Tier I + II (` Crore) 1,26,825 1,37,758 +`10,933 Cr\nRBI Minimum CRAR Requirement 11.50% 11.50% —","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c978","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":380,"text":"RBI Minimum CRAR Requirement 11.50% 11.50% —\nBuffer above RBI Minimum 6.52% 6.60% +8 bps\nCRAR at 18.10% is well above the regulatory minimum of 11.5% prescribed under Basel III, providing a capital buffer of 660\nbasis points. The CET-1 ratio improved by 71 basis points year-on-year to 15.69%, reflecting strong internal capital generation\nthrough retained profits. Total Capital (Tier I + II) rose to `1,37,758 crore from ` 1,26,825 crore in the prior year, providing ample\nheadroom for credit expansion and absorbing potential stressed assets.\n28 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nRevenue Diversification & Treasury Operations\nNon-Interest Income Composition (FY 2025–26)\nIncome Head FY 2025–26 (` Crore)\nFee-based Income 9,743\nRecoveries from Written-off Accounts 3,997\nOther Non-Interest Income 5,695\nTotal Non-Interest Income 19,435\nTreasury — Target vs Achievement (FY 2025–26)\nTreasury Parameter Target (` Crore) Actual (` Crore) Achievement\nProfit on Sale of Investments 2,900 2,929 Achieved \nExchange Profit (Forex) 950 1,064 Exceeded \nTotal Treasury Income 3,850 3,993 Exceeded \nTreasury operations continued to support overall profitability through prudent investment management, active participation\nin financial markets, and efficient liquidity management. The Finacle Treasury Software was upgraded to Version 11.20,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c979","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":381,"text":"in financial markets, and efficient liquidity management. The Finacle Treasury Software was upgraded to Version 11.20,\nincorporating SWIFT-MX (ISO 20022) support for international messaging standards, INDAS support for EIR method of securities\naccounting, and online real-time accounting through API integration. Treasury also upgraded its Dealing Room Trading System\nto enhance operational efficiency, faster decision-making, and improved trade execution capabilities.\nFinancial Capital Enablers — How Financial Capital Powers Other Capitals\nCapital How Financial Capital Enables It\nNatural Capital Planned `5,000 crore Green Bond issuance | Green finance portfolio `37,435 crore | Climate risk investments\nManufactured Capital Funding 8,697 branch network and 100 new branch additions | ATM, CBS, and digital infrastructure investment\nIntellectual Capital Technology R&D | DevSecOps platform | Finacle upgrades | Cybersecurity infrastructure investments\nSocial Capital `61.69 crore CSR through UBSFT | RSETI funding | Priority sector credit allocations\nHuman Capital Employee training investments | ISO 30414 implementation | Leadership development program funding\nFinancial Capital — Forward Strategy (FY 2026–27 & Beyond)\nThe Bank's financial capital strategy for FY 2026–27 and beyond is anchored on three pillars: accelerating growth, sustaining","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c980","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":381,"text":"The Bank's financial capital strategy for FY 2026–27 and beyond is anchored on three pillars: accelerating growth, sustaining\nprofitability, and optimising capital efficiency. Credit growth is targeted to remain in double digits, driven by retail, MSME, and\nagriculture segments. The green finance portfolio is planned to become the largest contributor of incremental business growth.\nFee income enhancement is targeted at 25% of total operating income. The cost-to-income ratio is to be optimised below 45%\nthrough process automation and digital channel migration.\n• Advances growth through digital channels, with continued expansion of the 15+ digital lending journeys\n• Expand green finance as flagship portfolio, targeting `5,000 crore Green Bond issuance in FY 2026–27\n• Maintain GNPA below 3% and sustain PCR above 94% through proactive NPA management\n• Maintain CRAR comfortably above regulatory requirement — targeting headroom of at least 600 bps above RBI minimum\n• Achieve consistent ROA above 1.20% through operational efficiency improvements\n• Set up dedicated structured derivatives desk for real-time hedging of interest rate risks for clients\n29 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nRisk Management Framework\nUnion Bank of India has established an Integrated Risk Management Framework covering credit risk, market risk, operational","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c981","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":382,"text":"Union Bank of India has established an Integrated Risk Management Framework covering credit risk, market risk, operational\nrisk, liquidity risk, and climate risk. The framework is anchored by a Board-approved Risk Appetite Statement that articulates the\nquantum and type of risk the Bank is willing to accept in pursuit of its strategic objectives. Three lines of defence — business units,\nrisk management and compliance functions, and internal audit — ensure comprehensive risk coverage across all operations.\nCredit Risk Management: Enhanced underwriting standards and rigorous monitoring systems have contributed to the dramatic\nimprovement in asset quality. Sector-wise exposure limits and concentration risk management ensure portfolio diversification.\nEarly warning systems and proactive intervention mechanisms have resulted in fresh slippages declining from `12,073 crore in\nFY25 to ` 8,451 crore in FY26, a reduction of 30.01%. The Bank's credit rating profile for the corporate loan book (above ` 25\ncrore) reflects strong quality, with 86% of the portfolio rated 'A & Above' as of March 2026, up from 85% in March 2025.\nMarket Risk Management: Treasury operations are conducted within a well-defined risk appetite framework with prescribed\nlimits for interest rate risk, foreign exchange risk, and investment portfolio concentration. Interest rate risk is managed through","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c982","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":382,"text":"limits for interest rate risk, foreign exchange risk, and investment portfolio concentration. Interest rate risk is managed through\nduration analysis, VaR measurement, and use of interest rate futures and overnight index swaps. Foreign exchange risk is\nmanaged through daily mark-to-market positions and stop-loss limits, with the dealing room supported by advanced trading\nsystems upgraded during FY 2025-26.\nOperational Risk Management: Process automation has significantly reduced operational errors and improved service\nconsistency. The Bank's cybersecurity framework, including 24×7 NGSOC operations and ISO 27001 certification, protects digital\nassets from cyber threats. Business continuity planning ensures service reliability across all channels. The Unified Assurance\nFramework integrates risk, compliance, monitoring, and audit, providing real-time visibility over critical systems and enabling\nfaster corrective action.\nLiquidity Risk Management: The Bank's liquidity position remained robust throughout FY 2025-26. Average LCR stood at\n113.83% against the minimum requirement of 100%, and NSFR at 115.34%, well above the prescribed threshold. Calibrated\nliquidity management through strategic asset-liability matching, Certificates of Deposit issuance, and active participation in the","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c983","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":382,"text":"liquidity management through strategic asset-liability matching, Certificates of Deposit issuance, and active participation in the\nmoney market ensured adequate funding at all times. CASA deposits, at `4,60,077 crore and a ratio of 35.20%, provide a stable\nand low-cost funding base.\nCorporate Credit Rating Profile — FY 2025-26\nRating Category Mar-25 (%) Mar-26 (%)\nA & Above 85% 86%\nBBB 10% 9%\nBB & Below 5% 5%\nNBFC Portfolio — AAA 62% 65%\nNBFC Portfolio — AA 33% 30%\nNBFC Portfolio — A & Below 5% 5%\n30 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nSectoral Credit Exposure — Key Industries (March 2026)\nSector Mar-25 (` Cr) Mar-25 % Share Mar-26 (` Cr) Mar-26 % Share\nInfrastructure 93,034 9.83% 1,04,441 10.03%\nNBFCs and HFCs 1,33,973 14.16% 1,64,839 15.84%\nBasic Metals 23,745 2.51% 28,183 2.71%\nPetroleum / Coal / Nuclear 17,269 1.83% 17,374 1.67%\nFood Processing 25,353 2.68% 4,648 0.45%\nChemical & Chemical Products 12,751 1.35% 14,212 1.37%\nTextiles 16,397 1.73% 16,118 1.55%\nTotal (Sectors 1 to 7) 3,22,522 34.09% 3,49,815 33.61%\nInfrastructure Sub-sector Exposure (March 2026, ` Crore)\nInfrastructure Sub-sector Mar-25 (` Cr) Mar-26 (` Cr) YoY Change\nPower — Total 39,904 48,323 +8,419\n— Renewable Energy 13,394 18,694 +5,300\n— Wind Energy 309 718 +409\n— Solar Energy 8,192 13,736 +5,544\n— Thermal Power 13,957 15,779 +1,822","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c984","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":383,"text":"— Thermal Power 13,957 15,779 +1,822\n— Govt. PSU Power 24,854 23,271 -1,583\n— Non-PSU Power 15,049 25,052 +10,003\nRoad (incl. HAM: ` 9,508 Cr) 31,786 41,692 +9,906\nOthers 21,344 14,426 -6,918\nEASE Framework Performance — FY 2025-26\nThe Enhanced Access and Service Excellence (EASE) programme, introduced by the Government of India, benchmarks public sector banks on technology adoption, customer service, responsible banking, and financial inclusion. Union Bank of India has been a consistent high performer under EASE 8.0, recording 2nd position in Q1 and Q2 bank-wise rankings, and 1st position in Theme 3 (Socio-economic Impact) in Q3.\nEASE 8.0 Quarter Overall Bank Ranking Theme-wise Performance\nQ1 FY 2025-26 2nd Position Top 3 in 3 out of 4 themes\nQ2 FY 2025-26 2nd Position 1st position in 2 out of 4 themes\nQ3 FY 2025-26 — 1st position in Theme 3 (Socio-economic Impact)\nUnder EASERise (Pillar-II) — the Bank's bank-specific 3-year strategic roadmap — six priority initiatives are being pursued: (1) Sustainable Growth in MSME, (2) Sustainable Growth in Mid Segment Corporate, (3) Expanding Agriculture Investment Credit, (4) Strengthening Compliance & Enhancing Compliance Culture, (5) Enhancing Retail Deposit Mobilisation, and (6) Enhancing Customer Experience. These initiatives cover 37 measurable metrics and are reviewed quarterly by senior management.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c985","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":383,"text":"31 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nNatural Capital\nLeading India's Climate-Positive Banking Transformation\nNatural Capital Overview\nNatural Capital encompasses the renewable and non-renewable environmental resources and processes that provide goods\nand services supporting the Bank's products and operations while creating value for stakeholders. At Union Bank of India,\nwe recognise that climate change poses both significant risks and unprecedented opportunities for the banking sector. Our\napproach integrates environmental stewardship into core business strategy, creating a comprehensive climate-positive banking\nframework that aligns banking activities with India's national climate goals and the global net-zero transition.\nIn FY 2025–26, Union Bank significantly strengthened its environmental capital position. The green finance portfolio grew\nby 30.7% to `37,435 crore, with renewable energy financing reaching `33,572 crore — supporting India's ambitious 500 GW\nrenewable energy target. The Bank's EV financing surged by 167.5% to `2,084 crore, reflecting the rapid acceleration of India's\nEV transition. As India's first major bank to become a signatory of the Partnership for Carbon Accounting Financials (PCAF), Union\nBank demonstrated thought leadership in transparent climate risk reporting and financed emissions tracking.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c986","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":384,"text":"Bank demonstrated thought leadership in transparent climate risk reporting and financed emissions tracking.\nOur Natural Capital Philosophy\nPillar Description\nClimate Leadership Pioneering sustainable finance solutions that support India's net-zero ambitions and the global\nenergy transition\nRisk Integration Embedding physical and transition climate risks into all lending, investment, and business\ndecisions through systematic ESG assessment\nInnovation Drive Developing green financial products — loans, deposits, bonds — that create new market\nopportunities and attract ESG-conscious customers\nOperational Sustainability Achieving net-zero in own operations by 2035, through renewable energy adoption, green building\ncertifications, and waste reduction\nTransparency PCAF-based financed emissions tracking and disclosure, consistent with global climate reporting\nstandards\nGovernance & Strategic Framework\nBoard-level ESG oversight ensures that environmental considerations are embedded at the highest governance levels of the Bank.\nThe Environmental, Social & Governance Steering Committee (ESGSC) coordinates strategic ESG direction across all business\nverticals. The Risk Management Committee of the Board (RMC) oversees climate and ESG risks. The Credit Risk Management\nCommittee (CRMC), Operational Risk Management Committee (ORMC), and Asset Liability Committee (ALCO) integrate climate","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c987","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":384,"text":"Committee (CRMC), Operational Risk Management Committee (ORMC), and Asset Liability Committee (ALCO) integrate climate\nrisk considerations into their respective mandates. Day-to-day implementation is managed by the ESG Cell within the Risk\nManagement Department (RMD), supported by all business and control verticals.\nThe Bank has established a dedicated Sustainable Finance Framework using BSR (Banking Sector Reference) codes as the starting\npoint for categorising loans into green and social financing categories. Tagging of green loans is done at account level to improve\ntransparency in reporting and enable precise tracking of the Bank's climate finance impact.\n32 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nESG Strategic Roadmap\nTimeframe Strategic Goals Key Milestones Value Creation\nShort-term (2027) Foundation\nBuilding Employee capacity, green products, bonds Market positioning\nMedium-term\n(2030) Risk Integration End-to-end climate risk solution, customer ESG assessment Competitive\nadvantage\nLong-term (2035) Leadership Position Net-zero operations, industry benchmark Sustainable\nleadership\nGreen Finance Portfolio — FY 2025–26\nGreen Product Suite — FY25 vs FY26 Comparison\nGreen Product FY 2025–26 (`\nFY 2024–25 (`\nYoY Growth Environmental Impact\nCrore)\nCrore)","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c988","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":385,"text":"Crore)\nRenewable Energy Projects 33,572 27,668 +21.3% Massive carbon footprint reduction | Market leadership Union Green Vehicle (EV) 2,084 779 +167.5% Sustainable transportation transition | First-mover advantage Union Green Home 1,779 197 +803.0% Energy-efficient housing | Customer awareness building Total Green Finance Portfolio\n37,435 28,644 +30.7% Comprehensive climate-positive portfolio Green Deposits 29.29 29.05 +0.8% Retail ESG investment facilitation\nThe Bank's green finance portfolio demonstrated exceptional momentum in FY 2025–26. The Union Green Home product\nrecorded the most dramatic growth at 803.0%, from `197 crore to `1,779 crore, reflecting the Bank's successful market\npenetration in energy-efficient housing finance. Union Green Vehicle (EV) financing grew by 167.5% to `2,084 crore, driven by\nrapidly growing EV adoption in India. Renewable Energy financing remained the cornerstone of the portfolio at `33,572 crore —\nsupporting utility-scale solar, wind, and hybrid energy projects across the country.\nPlanned Green Bond Issuance\nBond Category Amount (` Crore) Purpose Climate Impact\nGreen Infrastructure Bonds 5,000 Environmental / Sustainable projects Direct and indirect climate action\nThe planned `5,000 crore Green Infrastructure Bond issuance positions Union Bank among India's few largest green infra","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c989","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":385,"text":"The planned `5,000 crore Green Infrastructure Bond issuance positions Union Bank among India's few largest green infra\nbond issuers. The proceeds will be deployed exclusively into eligible green and sustainable projects, consistent with the Bank's\nSustainable Finance Framework, and will be independently verified and reported against agreed use-of-proceeds criteria.\nSustainable Finance Classification System\nThe Bank has developed a comprehensive Sustainable Finance Classification System using BSR (Banking Sector Reference) codes\nas the basis for categorising loans into green and social financing categories. The classification system follows an account-level\ntagging approach, enabling precise tracking and transparent reporting of green and social loan portfolios. The system aligns\nwith internationally recognised frameworks including the ICMA Green Bond Principles, EU Taxonomy principles, and India's\nregulatory guidance on sustainable finance.\nBuilding upon the BSR framework, the Bank has further refined its classification system to distinguish between climate mitigation\nand adaptation financing. This enhanced categorization enables precise measurement of climate-related financial flows and\ntheir environmental impact.\n33 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c990","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":386,"text":"GRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nTo address overlapping BSR codes and enhance classification accuracy, the Bank is developing machine learning algorithms that analyze the purpose of lending from the credit appraisal system. This innovative approach leverages artificial intelligence to ensure precise categorization based on actual lending intent rather than broad sectoral classifications. The Bank has used such Large Language Model successfully for select credit underwriting cases and is in the process of scaling up the same.\nFinance Category Sub-category Eligible Activities FY26 (` Cr)\nRenewable Energy Solar / Wind / Hydro / Biomass Power generation projects — clean energy 33,572\nGreen Buildings Energy-Efficient Housing Green Home Loans, Green certified buildings 1,779\nClean Transportation Electric Vehicles EV two-wheelers, four-wheelers, commercial EVs 2,084\nTotal Green Finance — Comprehensive climate-positive portfolio 37,435\nSustainable Deposits Retail Green Deposits Deposits earmarked for green project funding 29\nJust Transition Finance Framework","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c991","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":386,"text":"Just Transition Finance Framework\nThe Bank is actively working to identify industrial hotspots that are particularly vulnerable to transition risks as part of developing a comprehensive just transition finance framework. In these identified hotspots, the Bank is analyzing its lending portfolio to map loans extended to individuals & businesses directly and indirectly related to transition risk sector and other MSME loans. This approach will enable the Bank to understand the cascading impact of climate transition on its broader lending portfolio and develop targeted support mechanisms to ensure equitable transition for affected borrowers whose livelihoods depend on carbon-intensive industries.\nCurrent Climate Risk Capabilities\nUnion Bank of India has built a comprehensive climate risk management framework that integrates physical and transition risk assessment into credit decisions, portfolio monitoring, and strategic planning. The Bank's approach is aligned with the recommendations of the Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) and the Taskforce on Nature-related Financial Disclosures (TNFD).\nPhysical Risk Assessment\nFlood, Cyclone, Drought Impact Analysis\nBranch-level geographic vulnerability mapping\nClimate data & precipitation projections\nRisk Scores: 0-100 scale for all locations\nTransition Risk Assessment\nHigh-Emission Sector Identification & analysis","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c992","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":386,"text":"High-Emission Sector Identification & analysis\nMining, Steel, Cement, Petroleum, Infrastructure focus\nCustomer-level transition risk evaluation\nPolicy & technology change impact assessment\nFinanced Emissions Tracking\nPCAF methodology implementation\nAccount level calculation\nQlik dashboard for real-time monitoring\nComprehensive portfolio emissions mapping\n34 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nEnvironmental Social Due Diligence\nThe Bank has implemented a comprehensive Environmental and Social Due Diligence (ESDD) framework for green loans exceeding specified thresholds. This systematic approach ensures that all significant green financing transactions undergo rigorous environmental and social impact assessment before approval.\nThe ESDD process evaluates potential environmental risks, social impacts, and alignment with international sustainability standards. This framework strengthens the Bank's commitment to responsible financing while mitigating environmental and social risks in the lending portfolio.\nInnovation in Climate Risk - End-to-End Climate Risk Solution\nClimate Risk Management Solution\nCustomer ESG Scorecard System\nListed companies assessment\nUnlisted entities evaluation\nMSME borrower ESG\nRisk Monitoring dashboard\nIntegrated System Capabilities\nFinanced Emission calculation\nPhysical risk assessment\nTransition risk evaluation","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c993","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":387,"text":"Transition risk evaluation\nBiodiversity and nature risk assessment\nClimate Stress testing & Scenario Analysis (Impact on PD and LGD)\nDecarbonization plan\nCredit rating impact analysis\n35 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nSystem Integrations\nCRISIL ICON (Internal Rating System)\nLAS (Loan Automated Solution)\nFinacle Core Banking\nRisk Monitoring Dashboard and Reporting\nTechnology Innovation: Our implementation of end-to-end climate risk solution will enable comprehensive customer-level ESG assessments, integrating transition and physical risks with our existing systems.\nNature & Biodiversity Risk Assessment\nAssessment Framework Implementation Strategic Value\nTNFD LEAP Approach Bank is integrating Nature and Biodiversity Risk assessment\nin project loan underwriting Comprehensive risk coverage\nLocate Interfaces Geographic and sector mapping Enhanced due diligence Evaluate Dependencies Nature impact assessment Risk mitigation strategies Assess Opportunities Sustainable project identification New business development Prepare Responses Risk management integration Competitive advantage\nGovernance and Strategy","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c994","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":388,"text":"Governance and Strategy\nBank is integrating Nature and Biodiversity Risk assessment in project loan underwriting by applying TNFD LEAP methodology as part of our commitment to nature-positive financing. This initiative aligns with emerging regulatory frameworks and positions the Bank at the forefront of nature-related financial risk management in the Indian banking sector.\nRisk Management Framework\nOur nature and biodiversity risk assessment framework operates across four key phases:\nLocate (L): Geographic mapping of project locations to identify proximity to sensitive biodiversity areas and protected zones using tools such as WDPA and Google Earth Engine.\nEvaluate (E): Assessment of nature-related dependencies and impacts, including ecosystem services dependencies, biodiversity impacts on local flora and fauna, and effects on water, air, and soil quality.\nAssess (A): Quantification of nature-related risks including operational, regulatory, reputational, and financial risks from potential environmental impacts.\nPrepare (P): Development of risk mitigation strategies including biodiversity management plans, habitat restoration commitments, and ongoing monitoring frameworks.\nKey Risk Categories Assessed:\n• Physical risks: Direct impacts on biodiversity through habitat destruction, deforestation, water pollution, and soil degradation","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c995","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":388,"text":"• Physical risks: Direct impacts on biodiversity through habitat destruction, deforestation, water pollution, and soil degradation\n• Transition risks: Regulatory changes related to biodiversity protection, evolving compliance requirements, and shifting stakeholder expectations\n• Systemic risks: Dependencies on ecosystem services that may be disrupted by climate change or environmental degradation\nMetrics and Targets\nThe Bank assigns nature-related risk ratings (low, medium, high) based on comprehensive assessment across seven key areas: project location sensitivity, ecosystem impact potential, regulatory compliance status, biodiversity dependencies, mitigation measures adequacy, monitoring framework robustness, and overall risk profile.\nFinanced Emissions & PCAF Methodology\nAs India's first major bank to become a signatory of the Partnership for Carbon Accounting Financials (PCAF), Union Bank demonstrated its commitment to transparent climate risk reporting. PCAF methodology provides standardised guidance for measuring and disclosing financed emissions — the greenhouse gas emissions attributable to the Bank's loans and investments. This enables the Bank to set science-based targets, engage borrowers on decarbonisation pathways, and identify high-impact\nsectors for green finance transition.\n36 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c996","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":389,"text":"Financials Reports Notice\nFinanced Emissions Portfolio\nFY2026 FY2025* FY2024*\nStrategic Response Outstanding\nAsset Class\nEmissions\nOutstanding\nEmissions\nOutstanding\nEmissions\n(` Cr)\n(tCO2e)\n(` Cr)\n(tCO2e)\n(` Cr)\n(tCO2e)\nLoans & Advances 10,41,105 6,24,09,582 9,45,952 5,17,26,871 8,73,664 4,71,50,172 Green transition\nsupport\nSovereign Debt 2,62,434 9,83,924 2,82,969 10,60,916 2,73,372 10,24,936 Climate policy\nalignment\nOther Investment 69,248 10,17,674 67,492 9,91,866 - - ESG investment\ncriteria\n13,72,787 6,44,11,180 12,96,413 5,37,79,653 11,47,036 4,81,75,108 Comprehensive\nTotal Portfolio\nmanagement\nPCAF Data Quality 3.70 3.62 3.54\n* Note: Differences in financed emissions for earlier years primarily reflect ongoing improvements in data quality, methodological\nenhancements, expanded portfolio coverage, and alignment with evolving industry standards. Historical figures have been recalculated\nwhere appropriate to ensure consistency and comparability, including updates to emission factors, client data, and financial exposure inputs.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c997","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":389,"text":"where appropriate to ensure consistency and comparability, including updates to emission factors, client data, and financial exposure inputs.\nRBI's proposed RB-CRIS (Reserve Bank – Climate Risk Information System) will significantly improve the availability, consistency, and standardisation of climate-related datasets, including emissions and physical risk data, which are key inputs for financed emissions calculation and climate risk assessment. The Bank is implementing integrated climate risk solution and the same is capable of integration with RB-CRIS.\nPCAF Partnership — Financed Emissions Disclosure\nUnion Bank of India became the first major Indian bank to become a signatory of the Partnership for Carbon Accounting Financials (PCAF) — a global industry-led initiative providing standardised methodologies for measuring and disclosing financed greenhouse gas emissions. PCAF membership enables the Bank to: measure financed emissions using asset class-specific methodologies; disclose emissions data enabling investor and stakeholder comparison; set science-based targets aligned with Paris Agreement pathways; and engage borrowers on decarbonisation plans.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c998","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":389,"text":"The Bank's PCAF implementation roadmap covers the key asset classes in its lending portfolio including corporate loans, project finance (particularly renewable energy), real estate finance, motor vehicle loans, and SME finance. For each asset class, PCAF- prescribed calculation methodologies are applied using borrower data, third-party databases, and national and sector emission factor databases. The Bank is progressively improving data quality and coverage, with an objective of full portfolio coverage within three years.\nFinanced Emissions — High-Impact Sector Framework\nSector Contribution to Financed\nTransition\nEngagement Strategy Bank's FY26\nEmission Intensity\nEmissions\nRisk\nExposure\n`48,323 Cr\nPower Generation 35% of financed emissions Very High Structural Transition finance for renewable energy shift\nIron & Steel 18% of financed emissions Very High Structural Green steel financing — DRI/EAF technology\nIncluded in Basic Metals `28,183 Cr\nIncluded in\nCement 6% of financed emissions Very High Structural Blended cement & energy efficiency loans\nsectors\n`14,212 Cr\nChemicals 1% of financed emissions High Significant Green chemistry & circular economy financing\nIncluded in\nMining & Quarrying 0.30% of financed emissions High Significant ESG due diligence & sustainable mining\nsectors\n37 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c999","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":390,"text":"GRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nThese five sectors contributed 60.30% of the Bank's financed emissions in FY2025-26, making them primary targets for client engagement, green lending transformation, and transition finance opportunities. The Bank conducts regular borrower-level climate dialogues and integrates ESG scorecards into credit decision-making for high-emission sectors.\nBank has already implemented ESG score card for MSME segment.\nTransition Planning Excellence\nHigh-Emission Sector Transformation Strategy\nSector Current Challenge Transition Support Financial Opportunity Mining & Quarrying High financed emissions Clean extraction technologies Green mining finance Cement Production Process emissions Carbon capture & alternative materials Sustainable cement projects Petroleum & Coal Fossil fuel dependency Renewable energy transition Clean energy infrastructure Basic Metals Energy-intensive processes Efficiency & renewable power Green steel financing Chemicals High carbon footprint Green chemistry & recycling Circular economy solutions\nCustomer Transition Support Framework\nDecarbonization Strategies:\n• Clean Energy Adoption: Solar, wind, clean hydrogen financing\n• Energy Efficiency: Process optimization and waste heat recovery\n• Carbon Capture: Technology implementation for hard-to-abate sectors\n• Circular Economy: Material recycling and sustainable resource use","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1000","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":390,"text":"• Circular Economy: Material recycling and sustainable resource use\n• Innovation Finance: R&D support for breakthrough technologies\nIndustry Collaboration:\n• Active participation in sector transformation initiatives\n• Partnership development for circular economy solutions\n• Technology provider connections for customer decarbonization\n• Best practice sharing across industry verticals\nTransition Plan of the Bank\nInfrastructure Basic Metal and\nChemicals and Chemical\nPetroleum, Coal Products\nIndustry Mining and\nCement and\nMetal Products\nQuarrying\nCement Products\nProducts\nand Nuclear\nFuels\nKey transition\nv Clean\nv Clean Fuels\nv Renewable\nv Recycling and\nv Alternative\nv Gradual phase- out of thermal coal\ntechnologies\nv Clean\nElectricity\nenergy\nfeedstocks\nsecondary production\nand Strategies\nv Clean Fuels\nv Smart Grids\nv Clean\nelectricity\nv Transition\nv Clinker\nv CCS\nv Advanced\nv Clean\nhydrogen\nv Thermal coal\nv Elimination\nMetals\nsubstitutes\nElectricity\nbuilding materials\nv CCS\nv CCS\nv Decarbonization of on-site operations\nphase-out\nof single-use plastics\nv Recycling\nv Storage and\nv Modular\nv Inert Anodes\nv Energy\nv Clean fuels\nv CCS\nflexibility\nconstruction\nv Clean Electricity\nv Lifecycle\nv Upgraded\nv Recycling\nEfficiency\nv CCS (Carbon\nmanagement\nraw materials\nv Enhanced\ncapture and storage)\nefficiency\nv Alternative\nbeneficiation and extraction","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1001","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":390,"text":"beneficiation and extraction\n38 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nInfrastructure Basic Metal and\nChemicals and Chemical\nPetroleum, Coal Products\nIndustry Mining and\nCement and\nMetal Products\nQuarrying\nCement Products\nProducts\nand Nuclear\nFuels\nIndustries\nv Bank can support customers by engaging with customers on their transition plan by providing transition\nrequirement\nsolutions.\nfrom Bank\nv Bank can partner for systematic change by collaborating with policymakers and participate in public-\n& financial\nprivate partnerships with aim to finance transition plan.\nopportunities\nv Bank can identify commercial opportunities to support customers in the decarbonization effort.\narising to the\nv Bank can also aim to implement innovative financing solutions and blended finance partnerships.\nBank\nBank’s current approach for Transition Planning\nThe Bank has used the GCAM model in its transition planning process to estimate various futuristic data points (like estimating\nproduction, emissions, raw materials, energy consumption & technology changes) in the hard to abate sectors under two\ndifferent scenarios run on the GCAM model thereby trying to determine various risks & opportunities arising in the hard to abate\nsectors on account of the decarbonization process.\nThe two different scenarios run on the GCAM model are as follows:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1002","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":391,"text":"The two different scenarios run on the GCAM model are as follows:\nv Scenario 1(BAU): India becoming a developed nation i.e. “Viksit Bharat” by 2047.\nv Scenario 2 (Net Zero): India becoming both a developed nation by 2047 and achieving net zero by 2070.\nTechnology Integration Roadmap — Climate & ESG\nInnovation Area Current Status FY 2026–27 Plans Strategic Impact\nESG Data Integration Operational Full operational use in credit ESG assessment embedded in credit decisions\nPilot implementation Detailed physical risk monitoring at asset level\nPhysical Risk Assessment (Lat/Long)\nUnder Implementation\nOperational Improve data quality More precise and granular emissions reporting\nFinanced Emission Assessment\nTransition Risk Assessment\nUnder Implementation\nCustomer-level detailed assessment\nOpportunities for transition finance identification\nFor all sectors Biodiversity & ecosystem risk integration\nTNFD Nature Assessment\nFor project loan underwriting\nOperational Sustainability","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1003","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":391,"text":"Operational Sustainability\nThe Bank has committed to achieving net-zero in its own operations by 2035, ahead of global banking industry targets. This commitment encompasses three dimensions: transitioning 100% of operational energy consumption to renewable sources, eliminating waste to landfill, and embedding green building certification standards in all new and renovated infrastructure. These operational sustainability commitments are tracked through a centralised environmental management system and reported to the Board ESG Governance framework.\n• Net-Zero Operations target by 2035 — carbon neutrality in own operations (Scope 1 & 2 emissions)\n• Green building certifications for Bank's owned and leased premises — standardised rollout under guidance from ESGSC\n• Renewable energy installations at Bank offices — reducing operational costs while lowering carbon footprint\n• Sustainable procurement practices — embedding environmental criteria in vendor selection and supply chain management\n• Digital-first operations — reducing paper consumption, travel, and associated emissions through digital banking platforms\n39 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nOperational Sustainability Initiative Status Target\nNet-Zero Operations Committed by 2035 Scope 1 & 2 carbon neutrality","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1004","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":392,"text":"Net-Zero Operations Committed by 2035 Scope 1 & 2 carbon neutrality\nRenewable Energy Procurement In progress — PPAs for select facilities 100% renewable by 2035\nGreen Building Certification New branches under implementation All new premises certified\nLED Lighting Retrofit Advanced implementation 100% of branches\nPaper Reduction Digitisation of internal processes Zero paper target\nTravel Emission Reduction Video conferencing adoption 50% travel reduction by FY28\nE-Waste Management Certified e-waste recycling partners 100% compliant disposal\nNet-Zero Commitment 2035\nEmissions Profile (tCO2e)\nFinancial Year 2026 2025\nScope 1 (Vehicle fuel, DG sets, AC) 11,143 11,166\nScope 2 (Electricity consumption) 1,60,565 1,66,961\nScope 3 (Supply chain, business travel) 2,18,069 2,34,734\nTotal Emissions 3,89,777 4,12,861\nBaseline & Target\nFY2024 baseline for net-zero journey\n1.5OC pathway alignment (Paris Agreement)\nScience-based target methodology adoption\nReduction Initiatives\nRenewable energy transition (solar installations)\nGreen building certification (IGBC standards)\nEnergy efficiency improvements\nWaste management optimization\nWater Recycling and Conservation Measures\nScope 3 Emission – Other than Category 15 Financed Emission\nScope 3 Category Unit FY 2025-26 FY 2024-25\nCategory 1 – Purchased Goods & services tCO2e 34,305 37,837\nCategory 2 – Capital Goods & services tCO2e 14,132 18,817","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1005","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":392,"text":"Category 2 – Capital Goods & services tCO2e 14,132 18,817\nCategory 3 – Fuel & energy other than scope 1 and 2 tCO2e 35,827 41,347\nCategory 5 – Waste generated in operations tCO2e 10,656 10,590\nCategory 6 – Business travel tCO2e 19,242 23,292\nCategory 7 – Employee commuting tCO2e 1,00,732 1,00,677\nCategory 8 – Upstream leased asset tCO2e 3,175 2,173\nTotal 2,18,069 2,34,734\n40 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nRenewable Energy Infrastructure\nSolar Installation Status Capacity Environmental Benefit\nCommissioned & Working 1,137 KW (22 locations) Immediate emission reduction\nNet Metering Awaited 78 KW (3 locations) Grid integration benefits\nWork Under Progress 80 KW (2 locations) Expansion momentum\nTendered 50 KW (1 location) Future capacity addition\nTotal Planned 1,345 KW Significant carbon savings\nGreen Building Initiative\nCertification Progress FY2026 FY2025 Impact\nEnergy Efficiency Gain Measurable Measurable Operational cost reduction\nCarbon Footprint Reduction Significant Significant Emission intensity improvement\nComprehensive Environmental Management\nInitiative FY2026 FY2025 Environmental Impact\nE-Waste Recycling 4.93 metric tons 18.09 metric tons -\nEnergy Consumption 879,550 GJ 892,585 GJ Efficiency optimization\nRenewable Energy 17,208 GJ 16,542 GJ 4% increase","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1006","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":393,"text":"Renewable Energy 17,208 GJ 16,542 GJ 4% increase\nOperational Excellence: Our increase in renewable energy demonstrates systematic approach to integrate renewable energy\nin own operations.\nValue Chain ESG Assessment\nThe Bank has developed a comprehensive ESG assessment framework for monitoring vendor performance across environmental,\nsocial, and governance parameters. This systematic approach enables continuous evaluation of sustainability practices\nthroughout the value chain ecosystem. Through this monitoring framework, the Bank assigns ESG performance ratings to\nvendors based on their ongoing performance across key sustainability metrics including environmental management systems,\nsocial responsibility practices, and governance standards.\nThe ESG monitoring process is fully integrated with vendor risk management processes, ensuring that sustainability performance\nis continuously tracked and any emerging risks are promptly identified and addressed.\nClimate Risk Integration in Business\nScenario Analysis Framework\nPhysical Risk Assessment:\n• Short-term (2030): Acute weather event impact on operations\n• Medium-term (2040): Chronic climate change effects on asset values\n• Long-term (2050): Systemic climate impact on business model\nBank has assessed physical risk caused by flood by utilizing climate data and precipitation projections. The precipitation","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1007","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":393,"text":"Bank has assessed physical risk caused by flood by utilizing climate data and precipitation projections. The precipitation\nprojections used for physical risk assessment are based on climate data from the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase\n6 (CMIP6), specifically the MIROC model.\nFor physical risk caused by cyclone, Bank has used CLIMADA (open-source package used for assessment of physical risk) provided\nimpact and damage function. For each of the Bank locations, based on latitude and longitude Bank has arrived at Expected\nAnnual Impact (EAI).\n41 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nBank is implementing 17 Physical Risk Hazards (Heatwave, Cold wave, Extreme Precipitation, Drought, Riverine Flood, Coastal\nFlood, Water Stress, Water Demand, Water Variability, Wildfire, Landslide, Earthquake, Sea Level Rise, Soil Liquefaction, Soil Erosion, and Ocean Acidification) from the current 2 (Flood & Cyclone) through the climate risk management solution.\nTransition Risk Evaluation:\n• Policy Risk: Carbon pricing and regulatory changes impact\n• Technology Risk: Stranded asset evaluation in high-carbon sectors\n• Market Risk: Customer preference shifts toward sustainable options\n• Reputation Risk: ESG performance impact on stakeholder relationships","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1008","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":394,"text":"• Reputation Risk: ESG performance impact on stakeholder relationships\nBank has assessed Transition Risk through various approaches like bottom-up approach, usage of PACTA (Paris Agreement Capital Transition Assessment) model and CBAM (Carbon Border adjustment mechanism) analysis for export borrowers operating in hard-to-abate sectors with exposure to European markets.\nThe integrated climate risk solution implemented by the Bank has a Transition Risk Assessment module which includes coverage, sector and asset scope, scenario and time-horizon treatment, high level outputs, functional coverage, and treatment of simplified approaches for MSME & unlisted portfolios. The module is designed primarily as an asset-level transition risk assessment framework for corporate exposures, with subsequent aggregation to borrower-level and portfolio-level views. It covers the full computational chain required to translate transition pathways into operational impacts, financial stress, and downstream credit-risk outputs such as PD, LGD, and Expected Loss.\nStress Testing Implementation\nStress Test Category Methodology Business Application\nClimate Physical Risk Geographic vulnerability mapping Branch network resilience planning, Collateral vulnerability, Impact on cash flow of corporates","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1009","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":394,"text":"Climate Physical Risk Geographic vulnerability mapping Branch network resilience planning, Collateral vulnerability, Impact on cash flow of corporates\nTransition Risk Sector emission intensity analysis Credit portfolio optimization, Impact of carbon tax on reve- nue and profitability of customers\nCombined Scenario Integrated climate impact modelling Strategic business planning\nICAAP Integration: Climate risk is embedded in our Internal Capital Adequacy Assessment Process (ICAAP), ensuring regulatory compliance and strategic risk management.\nAvoided Emissions & Positive Impact: Financed Avoided Emissions Calculation\nPositive Climate Impact\nRenewable Energy Projects\nParticulars FY2026 FY2025\nFinancing `33,572 cr `27,668 cr\nAvoided Emissions 35 million tCO2e 30 million tCO2e\nAnnual Financed Avoided Emissions 6 million tCO2e 3 million tCO2e*\nEquivalent to removing cars from road 2.4 million 1.2 million\n*Note: As per revised computations.\nGreen Transportation Particulars FY2026 FY2025 EV Financing `2,084 cr `779 cr Vehicles Financed 15,859 8204 Annual Financed avoided emissions 10,866 tCO2e 10,204 tCO2e Supporting India’s EV adoption goals\n42 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nEnergy-efficient Housing\nParticulars FY2026 FY2025\nGreen Home Loans `1,779 cr `197 cr\nHomes Financed 1,082 116","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1010","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":395,"text":"Homes Financed 1,082 116\nAnnual Financed avoided emissions 4178 tCO2e 163 tCO2e\nPromoting sustainable urban development\nClimate Positive Impact: Our green financing portfolio potentially avoids 6+ million tCO2e annually, making us a significant\ncontributor to India’s climate goals.\nSustainable Finance Framework\nBoard-Approved Framework Components\nFramework Element Validation Strategic Purpose\nResource Mobilization Moody’s Second Party Opinion Market credibility\nFinancing Framework Independent validation Transparent allocation\nAssurance Mechanisms External verification Stakeholder confidence\nImpact Reporting Regular monitoring Accountability framework\nUN SDG Alignment\nFinancial Allocation\nSDG Target Our Contribution\nMeasured Impact\nFY2026 FY2025\nSDG 7 (Clean Energy) Renewable energy financing `33,572 Cr `27,668 Cr Capacity addition tracking\nSDG 13 (Climate Action) Green product portfolio `3,863+ Cr `975+ Cr Emission reduction metrics\nSDG 11 (Sustainable Cities) Green home financing `1,779 Cr `197 Cr Energy efficiency promotion\nSDG 9 (Innovation) Clean technology support Integrated Innovation financing\nPartnership Ecosystem\nStrategic Climate Partnerships\nPartner Collaboration Area Strategic Value\nPCAF (Partnership for Carbon Accounting Financials) Financed Emission Ecosystem Global best practices","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1011","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":395,"text":"PCAF (Partnership for Carbon Accounting Financials) Financed Emission Ecosystem Global best practices\nGIZ (Deutsche Gesellschaft für Internationale Zusammenarbeit) Steel & cement capacity building Sector expertise\nCEEW (Council on Energy, Environment and Water) GCAM India model use cases Policy insights\nGlobal Recognition:\n Our PCAF signatory status and international partnerships position us as a thought leader in sustainable banking practices.\n In Jan 2026, M/s. Moody’s Ratings has assigned a rating of SQS2 “Very Good Sustainability Quality” to Bank’s Sustainable Financing Framework, which is the second-best rating on Moody’s SQS (Sustainability Quality Score) scale.\nDomestic Recognition:\n On 17th February 2026, M/s. CARE ESG Ratings Limited had assigned an ESG rating of “CareEdge-ESG 1+” ESG Rating to the Bank. The assigned rating is defined as leadership position in managing ESG Risk through best-in-class disclosures, policies and performance.\n43 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\n India’s Most Sustainable Companies (IMSC) by Business World:\nUnion Bank of India has been felicitated by Business World as one of the top 60 India’s Most Sustainable Companies for\n2024–25, underscoring the Bank’s steadfast commitment to sustainable banking practices and responsible growth.\nGlobal Event:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1012","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":396,"text":"Global Event:\n Union Bank of India played a prominent leadership role at Mumbai Climate Week (MCW) 2026, positioning itself as one\nof India’s most proactive public‑sector banks on climate finance, ESG integration, and transition planning. The event was\nheld during 17th –19th February 2026 at the Jio World Convention Centre, BKC, Mumbai, a flagship Global South platform\nfor accelerating climate action.\n The Bank hosted the “Union Bank Climate Dialogue” at the MCW (2026), a high‑level multi‑stakeholder forum focusing on\nhard‑to‑abate sectors transition, bringing together:\n Industry leaders\n Regulators and policymakers\n Financial institutions and investors\n ESG rating agencies and think tanks\nDiscussions covered low‑carbon technologies, data gaps, regulatory evolution, demand‑side pressures, and innovative\ntransition finance instruments.\n Union Bank unveiled three knowledge papers, developed jointly with leading national and international institutions,\naimed at converting climate ambition into bankable and investable pathways for Indian industry at the MCW (2026):\n Decarbonising the Steel & Cement Sectors:\nCo‑developed with GIZ (Germany), this paper outlines technology pathways, sector readiness, and financing\nmechanisms for decarbonising two of India’s most carbon‑intensive industries.\n Climate Scenario Modelling for Transition Planning:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1013","section":"Balance Sheet","tag":"balance","page":396,"text":" Climate Scenario Modelling for Transition Planning:\nDeveloped with CEEW, the document highlights Union Bank’s pioneering use of the GCAM (Global Change Analysis\nModel) to create India‑specific climate scenarios, integrating climate risks and opportunities into credit and portfolio\ndecision‑making.\n Operational Decarbonisation & Net‑Zero Roadmap:\nCo‑created with Ernst & Young (EY), this roadmap details how the Bank is embedding its Net‑Zero 2035 commitment\n(Scope 1 & 2) into governance, strategy, and internal processes.\n Union Bank launched an industry‑first “ESG Certification Program for Indian Banks”, in partnership with Climate Policy\nInitiative (CPI) at the MCW (2026).\nThe programme focuses on capacity building for bank officers in:\n ESG risk management\n Green & transition finance","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1014","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":396,"text":" Sustainability reporting and governance\nThis initiative aims to strengthen ESG integration across India’s banking ecosystem.\n44 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nNatural Capital Risk & Opportunity Matrix\nRisk Assessment & Mitigation\nRisk Category Impact Level Mitigation Strategy Business Opportunity\nPhysical Climate Risk Low Geographic diversification Climate-resilient infrastructure financing\nTransition Risk Medium Sector exposure management Green transition financing\nRegulatory Risk Low Proactive compliance First-mover advantage\nReputational Risk Medium ESG leadership positioning Brand differentiation\nStranded Assets Low-Medium Portfolio monitoring Asset transformation financing\nOpportunity Identification & Capture\nOpportunity Area Market Size Our Position Capture Strategy Renewable Energy Finance $100+ Bn (India) Market leader Expand portfolio to `50,000 Cr Green Bond Market $50+ Bn (India) Early entrant `5,000 Cr issuance Climate Tech Financing $25+ Bn (India) Explorer Leverage RBI proposed Climate Tech Sandbox Carbon Credit Trading $5+ Bn (India) Explorer Market entry preparation\nPerformance Measurement & Reporting\nNatural Capital KPIs\nKPI Category FY2026 Performance FY2025 Performance Long-term Goal\nGreen Finance Portfolio `33,572 Cr renewable `27,688 Cr renewable Be a market leader","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1015","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":397,"text":"Green Finance Portfolio `33,572 Cr renewable `27,688 Cr renewable Be a market leader\nFinanced Emissions Intensity 46.92 41.35 Set sector wise emission reduction target\nOwn Operations Emissions 0.40 Mn tCO2e 0.41 Mn tCO2e Net-zero (2035)\nRenewable Energy Capacity 1.3+ MW planned 1.3+ MW planned Install Solar at all feasible premises with roof rights by 2030\nClimate Risk Dashboard Metrics\nClimate Risk Metrics\nTransition Risk Exposure\nParticulars FY2026 FY2025\nHigh-emission sectors of financed emissions 60.30% 67.36%\nCustomer Transition Plans Under development\nSector-specific risk assessment and monitoring\nPhysical Risk Vulnerability\nBranch risk scores scale mapping: 0-100\nClimate data integration: Operational Planning\nCollateral risk assessment: Geographic Vulnerability\nComprehensive physical risk evaluation framework\nPortfolio Decarbonization\nParticulars FY2026 FY2025\nBaseline Financed Emissions 64.4 Mn tCO2e 53.8 Mn tCO2e\nFinanced Avoided Emissions 6+ Mn tCO2e 3 + Mn tCO2e\nSystematic decarbonization measurement and tracking\n45 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nMarket Development Strategy\nGreen Finance Expansion\n• Make renewable energy financing the flagship and largest portfolio segment — targeting `50,000 crore by FY28\n• Develop sector-specific green financing products for agriculture, MSME, and retail customers","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1016","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":398,"text":"• Develop sector-specific green financing products for agriculture, MSME, and retail customers\n• Create integrated green MSME and retail product suites addressing the full lifecycle of business operations\nClimate Risk Leadership\n• Become India’s benchmark institution for climate risk management in the banking sector\n• Develop proprietary climate risk assessment tools integrated into core banking processes\n• Offer climate advisory services to large corporate clients on transition pathways and green financing structures\n• Lead industry standards development through active participation in RBI and other industry-led climate-related working groups\n• Conduct regular UBI Climate Dialogues — structured engagement with borrowers on climate change adaptation\nSustainable Innovation\n• Partner with climate tech startups for developing next-generation sustainable banking solutions\n• Pioneer carbon credit trading and financing solutions in collaboration with exchange and regulatory bodies\n• Develop nature-positive financing frameworks addressing biodiversity and ecosystem risks\n• Establish international climate finance partnerships to access global green capital markets\nStakeholder Value Creation Through Natural Capital\nStakeholder Natural Capital Value Delivered Scale\nClimate & Environment Renewable energy financing supporting India's 500 GW target | Net-zero\n`33,572 Cr renewable","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1017","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":398,"text":"`33,572 Cr renewable\noperations commitment by 2035\nSociety & Communities Clean energy access in rural areas | Air quality improvement through EV &\n`2,084 Cr EV loans\nrenewable energy financing\nShareholders New revenue streams from green products | Climate risk mitigation\n`37,435 Cr portfolio\nprotecting asset values\nCustomers Sustainable finance products aligned with ESG values | Green Home and EV\n`3,863 Cr new green\nloan products\nretail\nRegulators Proactive TCFD/TNFD compliance | PCAF signatory | RBI climate framework\nFirst-mover leadership\nreadiness\nRegulatory Leadership & Compliance\nRegulatory Framework Alignment\nRegulation/Guideline Our Implementation Compliance Status Strategic Advantage\nDraft RBI Climate Risk Guidelines Comprehensive framework Under implementation Early implementer benefit\nSEBI ESG Disclosure Norms Detailed reporting Meets requirements Stakeholder confidence\nInternational Standards PCAF, TCFD, ISSB S2 alignment Leading practice Global recognition\nTaxonomy Development Active participation Influence development Thought leadership\n46 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nRegulatory Excellence: Our proactive approach to climate risk regulation positions us as an industry leader and preferred partner for regulators.\nIntegration with Other Capitals\nNatural Capital Value Creation Connections\nEnabling Financial Capital:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1018","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":399,"text":"Enabling Financial Capital:\n• `37,435 Cr green portfolio generating superior risk-adjusted returns\n• Climate risk mitigation protecting asset values and reducing provisions\n• New market opportunities creating diversified revenue streams\n• Cost optimization through operational sustainability initiatives\nLeveraging Intellectual Capital:\n• Climate risk technology enhancing assessment and monitoring capabilities\n• Data analytics for emission tracking and portfolio optimization\n• Digital platforms for green product delivery and customer engagement\n• Innovation partnerships for sustainable solution development\nSupporting Manufactured Capital:\n• Green building certification improving operational efficiency\n• Renewable energy installations reducing operational costs\n• Sustainable infrastructure enhancing long-term asset value\n• Climate-resilient facilities ensuring business continuity\nStrengthening Social & Relationship Capital:\n• Community climate benefits enhancing social license to operate\n• Customer engagement through sustainable finance solutions\n• Stakeholder trust through transparent climate action\n• Regulatory relationships through proactive compliance leadership\nFuture Vision: Climate-Positive Banking Leader\nStrategic Pillars for Climate Leadership\n1. Portfolio Transformation (2026-2030)\n• Set sector specific emission reduction target","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1019","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":399,"text":"• Set sector specific emission reduction target\n• Make green portfolio as largest contributor of incremental business\n2. Operational Excellence (2026-2035)\n• Achieve net-zero operations by 2035 (ahead of global banking targets)\n• 100% renewable energy for operations\n• Zero waste to landfill\n3. Innovation Leadership (2026-2030)\n• Implement comprehensive climate risk assessment solution\n• Create integrated ESG advisory services for corporates\n• Pioneer carbon credit trading and financing solutions\n47 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\n4. Market Development (2026-2030)\n• Lead India’s green bond market with major share in issuances\n• Create climate tech financing ecosystem\n• Develop nature-positive financing frameworks\n• Establish international climate finance partnerships\nConclusion: Natural Capital as Strategic Differentiator\nUnion Bank of India’s natural capital strategy represents more than environmental responsibility—it’s a comprehensive business\ntransformation that creates competitive advantage, generates new revenue streams, and builds long-term resilience. Our\n`37,435 Cr green portfolio, comprehensive climate risk framework, and net-zero operational commitment position us as\nIndia’s climate-positive banking leader.\nKey Natural Capital Achievements FY2026:\n• Market Leadership in renewable energy financing","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1020","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":400,"text":"• Market Leadership in renewable energy financing\n• Risk Integration Excellence through PCAF-based emissions tracking and scenario analysis\n• Innovation Pioneering with end-to-end climate risk solution and nature assessment\nStrategic Value Creation: Our natural capital investments generate measurable returns across all stakeholder groups while\ncontributing to India’s climate goals. The integration of environmental considerations into core banking operations creates a\nsustainable competitive advantage that drives long-term value creation.\nFuture-Ready Positioning: As climate considerations become central to banking regulations and customer expectations,\nour comprehensive natural capital framework ensures we remain ahead of market evolution, capturing opportunities while\nmitigating risks in India’s transition to a sustainable economy.\nNatural Capital acts as a key strategic lever that boosts financial performance, fosters innovation, deepens stakeholder engagement,\nand positions Union Bank of India at the forefront of sustainable banking in India.\n48 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nIntellectual Capital\nDriving Innovation Through Knowledge-Based Assets\nIntroduction\nIntellectual Capital represents the intangible, knowledge-based assets embedded within the Bank’s systems, processes,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1021","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":401,"text":"Intellectual Capital represents the intangible, knowledge-based assets embedded within the Bank’s systems, processes,\nrelationships, and organisational culture that enable sustainable value creation. At Union Bank of India, intellectual capital\ncomprises digital banking platforms, technological innovations, robust cybersecurity frameworks, institutionalised employee\nknowledge systems, and customer-centric digital solutions. Together, these capabilities are transforming traditional banking\noperations into a digitally enabled, secure, resilient, and customer-focused service delivery model — strengthening the Bank’s\ncompetitiveness in a rapidly evolving financial ecosystem.\nIn FY 2025–26, the Bank formulated a comprehensive Digital, Analytics & IT Strategy aligned with its business objectives of\ndriving growth, enhancing operational efficiency, and fostering innovation. A key transformation initiative was the Bank’s shift\nfrom a product-centric to a customer-centric approach, leveraging data analytics and customer insights to offer personalised\nproducts and superior experiences. The Bank’s strategic focus areas encompassed: enhancing customer experience through\ndigital transformation, strengthening risk governance and cybersecurity frameworks, advancing data management for evidence-\nbased decision-making, and modernising IT infrastructure for scalability and resilience.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1022","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":401,"text":"based decision-making, and modernising IT infrastructure for scalability and resilience.\nDigital Product Portfolio & Business Impact\nDigital Performance Indicator FY 2025–26 Achievement\nBusiness Mobilised through Digital Lending Journeys `1,18,000+ crore (15+ digital journeys)\nDigital Deposit Mobilisation `21,290+ crore\nUVConn (WhatsApp Banking) Users 75+ lakh users\nUVConn Requests Serviced 4.50 crore requests\nDigital MSME Loan Journeys 1,10,270 accounts | `4,505 crore\nDigi Renewal of MSME Accounts (up to `25 Lakh) 11.95 lakh accounts (98% of total in the slab)\nPM Surya Ghar Digital Sanctioning 51,700 accounts sanctioned out of 69,601 total\nDigital Education Loans (Premier Institutes, Female Students) 192 accounts out of 721 total\nDigital Insurance Products on Platform 44 products (including PMJJBY, PMSBY, Bajaj products)\nMSME Digital Journey Performance (FY 2025–26)\nDigital Journey Accounts Sanctioned Amount (` Crore)\nSTP Shishu Mudra 21,519 104\nSTP Kishore & Tarun Mudra 66,267 3,061\nNari Shakti STP 10,733 621\nMSME Superfast STP 801 324\nUnion Digital Ease 10,902 391\nWorking Capital Enhancement 48 4\nTotal 1,10,270 4,505\n49 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nDigital Journeys Implemented — FY 2025–26\nDuring FY 2025–26, the Bank implemented and expanded multiple digital journeys across asset, liability, and service segments,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1023","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":402,"text":"During FY 2025–26, the Bank implemented and expanded multiple digital journeys across asset, liability, and service segments,\nsubstantially reducing turnaround time and improving customer convenience:\nAsset Journeys\n• Digital Personal Loan: Collateral-free unsecured loan through an end-to-end digital journey for both ETB and NTB\ncustomers, without manual intervention.\n• Digital Vehicle Loan (4-Wheeler): Digital and paperless sanctioning for purchase of new four-wheelers for personal use,\ncovering both ETB and NTB customers — significantly reduced turnaround time.\n• Home Loan Digital Journey: Launched for single owners and select property types for ETB and NTB customers, bringing\ndown TAT and enhancing processing office efficiency.\n• Union Nari Shakti STP: Digital journey for women entrepreneurs, with 10,733 accounts sanctioned and `621 crore\ndisbursed.\n• Digital Kisan Tatkal: Digital KCC journey enabling farmers to obtain credit digitally — winner of Infosys Finacle Product\nInnovation Award.\n• Debt Swap STP: Agri Term Loan up to `0.50 lakh for farmers to mitigate debt burden from non-institutional sources.\n• Digital Rooftop Solar Journey (PM Surya Ghar): 51,700 accounts sanctioned, winner of Infosys Finacle Process Innovation\nAward.\nLiability & Service Journeys\n• Savings Account Journey for ETB Customers: Branch-assisted digital savings account opening for existing customers","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1024","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":402,"text":"• Savings Account Journey for ETB Customers: Branch-assisted digital savings account opening for existing customers\nwithout individual savings accounts.\n• National Pension Scheme (NPS) Digital Journey: Generates and activates PRAN instantly with built-in KYC compliance,\nphoto/signature upload, and initial contribution facility.\n• Re-KYC Journey: Digital re-KYC process for existing customers, reducing branch burden and improving compliance\nefficiency.\n• Digital Insurance Platform: Integration of Jan Suraksha schemes (PMJJBY and PMSBY) through Union Ease and ‘Unisure’\nportal. 44 products available including Bajaj Union Suraksha Kavach and Bajaj Sookshma Udyam Suraksha.\nInnovative Banking Solutions — FY 2025–26\nInnovation Achievement Impact\nUVConn — WhatsApp Banking Multilingual (7 languages) conversational banking\n75+ lakh users; 4.50 crore requests\nwith AI-based search; 70+ services enabled\nserviced\nCBDC — Retail & Wholesale Active participation in both Retail CBDC Cohort and\nDigital currency ecosystem advance-\nWholesale CBDC\nment\nApp Modernisation — DevSec-\nMicroservices architecture on Kubernetes platform\nScalability, resilience, faster time-to-\nOps\n/ Private Cloud — DevSecOps principles\nmarket\nFinacle Treasury v11.20 SWIFT-MX (ISO 20022), INDAS EIR method, real-time\nEnhanced treasury efficiency & com-\naccounting via API integration\npliance","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1025","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":402,"text":"pliance\nDigital SHG Management End-to-end digital Self Help Group management\nInfosys Finacle Business Model Innova-\nplatform\ntion Award\n50 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nInnovation Achievement Impact\nDigital Document Execution\nDigital document execution for PM Surya Ghar\nInfosys Finacle ESG Innovation Award\n(DDE)\nloans — enabled direct disbursement\nITRCoE IT Risk Centre of Excellence implementation Infosys Finacle Transformation Excel-\nlence Award\nHyper-Automation AI/ML based process automation across banking\nFinnoviti Awards 2025 — Banking\noperations\nFrontiers\nDigital Insurance Platform Integration of 44 insurance products including Jan\nExpanded insurance cross-sell ecosys-\nSuraksha schemes\ntem\nDigital NPS Journey PRAN activation with KYC compliance, instant pho-\nReduced NPS onboarding time signifi-\nto/signature upload\ncantly\nTechnology Awards & Recognitions — FY 2025–26\nAwarded by Award Category\nIBA IBA 21st Annual Banking\nBest Technology Bank — Winner | Best Digital Financial Inclusion — Runner\nTechnology Awards 2025–26\nUp | Best Tech Talent — Runner Up | Best Digital Sales — Special Mention\nBusiness Model Innovation — Digital SHG | ESG Innovation — Digital Document\nInfosys Infosys Finacle Innovation\nExecution | Transformation Excellence — ITRCoE | Product Innovation —\nAward 2025","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1026","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":403,"text":"Award 2025\nDigital KCC STP | Process Innovation — Digital Rooftop Solar Journey\nCapgemini IBEX India BFSI Tech Award\nIT Risk & Management — Winner | Excellence in Operational Efficiency —\n2025\nSpecial Mention | Enhanced Customer Service — Special Mention\nBanking Frontiers Finnoviti Awards 2025 HyperAutomation\nQuorus Quorus Finacle Infosystem\nUnion Advith\nBanking Innovation Awards\nCybersecurity & Digital Resilience\nUnion Bank of India has implemented a multi-layered cybersecurity architecture supported by continuous monitoring and\nresponse mechanisms. The Bank’s cybersecurity approach is built on the principles of Defence-in-Depth, Zero Trust Architecture,\nand continuous threat intelligence. The Bank maintains a 24×7 operational Security Operations Centre (SOC/NGSOC) with\nadvanced threat detection and automated response capabilities.\nCybersecurity Indicator FY 2025–26 Outcome\nSOC / Next-Gen SOC (NGSOC) 24×7 Operational\nCybersecurity Certifications ISO 27001 | ISO 31000 | PCI-DSS\nCyber Security Insurance Implemented (comprehensive coverage)\nBitSight Cyber Rating Advanced Category (top tier)\nData Protection Office Operational — DPDP Act compliant\nCyber Awareness SMS Sent (Q4 FY26) ~571.56 crore SMS\nMultilingual Customer Cyber Awareness Communications ~5.77 crore communications\nAwareness Sessions (RSETIs, Schools, SHGs) 48+ sessions in Q4 FY26","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1027","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":403,"text":"Awareness Sessions (RSETIs, Schools, SHGs) 48+ sessions in Q4 FY26\nSenior Citizen Cyber Awareness Programs 2 focused programs conducted\nSystemic Cyber Incidents Impacting Service Zero reported incidents\n51 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nEmployee training and awareness programmes reinforce cyber resilience by promoting secure behaviours and risk awareness\nacross the organisation. The Bank has strengthened its Data Protection framework in compliance with the Digital Personal Data\nProtection (DPDP) Act requirements. The Unified Assurance Framework implemented across vendor operations has reduced\nreconciliation gaps and audit exceptions, while automated reconciliation and fraud monitoring systems have improved control\ncoverage and response time.\nStrategic Outlook — Intellectual Capital\nFuture Innovation Roadmap\nUnion Bank’s intellectual capital strategy focuses on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning integration across credit\nassessment, fraud detection, and customer analytics; Blockchain adoption for enhanced transaction security and trade finance;\nand API-first architecture development enabling open banking and fintech partnerships. The Bank’s roadmap includes advanced\nanalytics implementation for predictive risk management, comprehensive cyber resilience enhancement through AI-powered","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1028","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":404,"text":"analytics implementation for predictive risk management, comprehensive cyber resilience enhancement through AI-powered\nthreat intelligence, and expansion of the digital journeys portfolio to cover additional retail and MSME segments.\n• AI-powered Knowledge Discovery Platforms — enhancing institutional learning and decision-making\n• Predictive Training Systems — personalised employee development using AI analytics\n• Innovation Labs — continuous R&D for emerging banking technologies including GenAI applications\n• Expand digital journeys — new retail loan variants (SRLGE Prime for government officials), Loan Against Mutual Fund,\nVehicle Loan Against FD\n• Co-lending model expansion — partnerships with NBFCs and HFCs for housing loans and MSME credit\n• Analytics-driven cross-selling — using customer analytics to identify and fulfil unmet financial needs\n52 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nHuman Capital\nEmpowering Growth Through People\nIntroduction: The Role of Human Capital in Value Creation\nIn the evolving landscape of global finance, human capital has transformed from being a supportive resource to a strategic\nenabler of value creation. At Union Bank of India, we recognise that our employees are the cornerstone of our mission to","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1029","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":405,"text":"enabler of value creation. At Union Bank of India, we recognise that our employees are the cornerstone of our mission to\nbecome a next-generation, digitally enabled universal bank. Our approach to managing human capital is fully aligned with the\nIntegrated Reporting Framework, which identifies human capital as one of the six key capitals essential for sustainable value\ncreation.\nOur holistic human capital strategy addresses every stage of the employee journey — from onboarding to specialisation and\nleadership development. This strategy integrates business objectives with people practices, enabling long-term value creation for\nboth the organisation and its stakeholders while building a high-performance culture supported by inclusive policies, employee-\ncentric systems, and leadership nurturing.\nIn FY 2025–26, the Bank strengthened its human capital governance significantly by transitioning from ISO 30414:2018 to ISO\n30414:2025 — the globally recognised standard for Human Capital Reporting and Disclosure. A dedicated ISO data-capture\nmodule was deployed, enabling centralised, auditable, and secure management of human capital data aligned with evolving ESG\nand sustainability disclosure requirements.\nWorkforce Profile — FY 2025–26\nWorkforce Parameter FY 2025–26\nTotal Employees 73,885\nSpecialised Employees 6,198\nSports Persons 88","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1030","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":405,"text":"Sports Persons 88\nLocal Bank Officers (LBOs) — recruited for regional coverage Multiple batches — Induction programs conducted\nBusiness Correspondents trained during FY26 2,800+\nLearning & Development Excellence\nThe Bank made sustained and focused investments in the Learning & Development (L&D) ecosystem during FY 2025–26, ensuring\nthat every Unionite has access to best-in-class learning resources. Aligned with the Bank’s strategic vision and evolving business\npriorities, the L&D ecosystem continued to foster a culture of continuous learning, performance excellence, and leadership\nreadiness across all levels.\nAll L&D units were certified under ISO 9001:2015 and ISO 21001:2018, reinforcing standardised and learner-centric quality\nsystems. A Performance Improvement Plan (PIP) framework was introduced to align learning outcomes with measurable business\nimpact. Structured learning pathways for senior leadership and critical roles were finalised to support systematic succession\nplanning. The launch of a Digital Mentorship Program facilitated knowledge transfer and leadership grooming, while job cards\ndeveloped for key field operations enabled the integration of SOPs and regulatory compliance into day-to-day execution.\n53 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nLearning & Development — Key Metrics (FY 2025–26)","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1031","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":406,"text":"Learning & Development — Key Metrics (FY 2025–26)\nL&D Metric FY 2025–26 Achievement\nTotal Learning Hours Imparted 23.10 Lakh+\nAverage Learning Hours per Employee per Quarter 31.24 hours\nISO Certification of L&D Units ISO 9001:2015 & ISO 21001:2018\nHuman Capital Reporting Standard ISO 30414:2025 (upgraded from 2018)\nE-Learnathon Completion Rate (Mandatory eLearning) 94%+ among officers\nCoursera Partnership Renewed — access to global certifications\nIIBF Partnership Renewed — JAIIB and CAIIB e-learning support\nRashtriya Karmayogi Program ('Seva Bhav') 33,300+ employees trained\nSign Language Training Conducted — reinforcing inclusive learning\nCapacity Building — Training Delivery Matrix (FY 2025–26)\nTraining Channel Programs / Sessions Participants / Beneficiaries\nULAs — Classroom & Locational Programs 629 programs 19,386 employees\nULAs — Virtual Training Programs 160 programs 9,108 employees\nULAs — Webinars 350 webinars 64,912 participants\nZLCs — Classroom Programs 1,080 programs 26,683 participants\nZLCs — Locational Programs 574 programs 22,016 participants\nZLCs — Virtual Programs 194 programs 11,395 participants\nZLCs — Webinars 290 webinars 24,927 participants\nExternal Training Programs — 1,212 employees (incl. 26 overseas)\nBusiness Correspondent Training (Hybrid / Local Languages) Multiple sessions 2,800+ BCs\nLeadership & Specialised Development Programs\nLeadership Development","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1032","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":406,"text":"Leadership Development\nLeadership development remained a key priority in FY 2025–26, with 42 Leadership Development Programs attended by 867 senior\nexecutives at Scale V to Scale VII. These programs enhanced strategic leadership capabilities, decision-making competencies,\nand change management skills at the highest levels of the Bank’s management hierarchy. The Bank also launched a Digital\nMentorship Program to facilitate structured knowledge transfer and leadership grooming across levels — pairing experienced\nleaders with high-potential talent.\nLeadership Initiative Details\nLeadership Development Programs 42 programs | 867 senior executives (Scale V to Scale VII)\nInstitution Collaborations IIM Indore, ISB Hyderabad, MDI Gurgaon — 5 focused domains\nOfficers Trained at Premier Institutions 346 officers across Risk Management, Strategy & Finance, Sales &\nMarketing, Operational Excellence, Digital Financial Inclusion / FinTech /\nCo-lending\n54 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nLeadership Initiative Details\nTrain-the-Trainer (TTT) Programs 100 internal faculty — upskilled by premier institutions\nDigital Mentorship Program Launched — knowledge transfer & leadership grooming\nGen AI Training for Senior Executives Conducted — AI, Gen AI and Use Cases in Banking\nOrientation Training Programs (OTP) 6,780 officers | 3,027 clerical staff","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1033","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":407,"text":"Orientation Training Programs (OTP) 6,780 officers | 3,027 clerical staff\nReconnect Program (Women — Maternity\nStructured reintegration and capability enhancement\nReturn)\nLocal Language Training (Employees Outside\nProvided to improve customer engagement\nLinguistic Regions)\nSUCCESS Programs (Frontline Officers & CSAs) Aligning service excellence with organisational goals\nKnowledge Creation & Faculty Development\nKnowledge Activity FY 2025–26 Achievement\nResearch Projects Completed 40 research projects\nCase Studies Developed 350\nFaculty Development Programs Attended 312 faculty members at reputed institutions\nExternal Certification Courses (Faculty) 1,039 courses completed\nArticles Published in Reputed Journals 307 articles\nAcademic & Training Sessions at NIBM/NABARD/FORE/BIRD 216 sessions\nReview of Academic Books 1 book reviewed\nEmployees — External Certification Courses 2,916 employees | 4,043 courses\nEmployees — Internal Certification Courses 3,460 employees | 9,038 courses\nPost-Course Evaluation (PCE) Framework Implemented in LMS\nQuantitative Business Parameters (QBP) Integrated into LMS for impact assessment\nKey HR Strategic Initiatives — FY 2025–26\nISO 30414:2025 — Human Capital Reporting\nDuring FY 2025–26, the Bank strengthened its human capital governance by transitioning from ISO 30414:2018 to ISO 30414:2025","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1034","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":407,"text":"During FY 2025–26, the Bank strengthened its human capital governance by transitioning from ISO 30414:2018 to ISO 30414:2025\n— the globally recognised standard for Human Capital Reporting and Disclosure. This transition enables standardised, consistent,\nand comparable reporting of workforce metrics across key human capital areas, aligned with evolving ESG and sustainability\ndisclosure requirements. A dedicated ISO data-capture module was deployed, enabling centralised, auditable, and secure\nmanagement of human capital data.\nPerformance Management & Recognition\nThe Performance Management System (PMS) was further refined to ensure fair, transparent, and objective assessment of\nemployee performance, with a direct link between individual contributions and organisational goals. The Rewards & Recognition\nframework was actively utilised to acknowledge employee contributions, reinforcing a culture of high performance and\nmeritocracy.\n55 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nDigital Learning Enablement\nThe Bank significantly expanded digital learning outreach during the year through the ELearnathon Campaign, achieving over 94%\ncompletion of mandatory e-learning among officers. New and updated modules were launched across KYC/AML, cybersecurity,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1035","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":408,"text":"completion of mandatory e-learning among officers. New and updated modules were launched across KYC/AML, cybersecurity,\nclimate risk, and ethics in banking. The Bank expanded micro-learning outreach through podcasts, radio broadcasts, micro-bytes,\nshort videos, and recordings of important webinars, while restructuring programs into Level I (Basic) and Level II (Advanced)\nformats to enable progressive skill development.\nAwards & Recognition — Human Capital FY 2025–26\nAward / Category Awarded by Date\nNational Best Employer Brand 2025 World HRD Congress February 2026\nInnovative HR Practices 2025 World HRD Congress February 2026\nExcellence in Training Award World HRD Congress February 2026\nExcellence in Continuous Learning & Upskilling Asia L&D Employer Branding Conf. (IBC) February 2026\nBest Learning Experience Platform Asia L&D Employer Branding Conf. (IBC) February 2026\nApex India HR Excellence Award 2025 Apex India Foundation November 2025\nApex India Training Excellence Award 2025 Apex India Foundation November 2025\nDigital Learning & Upskilling Initiative of the Year People Tech Summit Awards (IBC) August 2025\nBest HR Digital Transformation Initiative People Tech Summit Awards (IBC) August 2025\nExcellence in Learning & Development International Business Conferences May 2025\nExcellence in HR Digitisation International Business Conferences May 2025","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1036","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":408,"text":"Excellence in HR Digitisation International Business Conferences May 2025\nKeerthi Puraskar — Patrika (First) Rajbhasha Vibhag, MHA, GoI September 2025\nKeerthi Puraskar — Karyaniayan (Third) Rajbhasha Vibhag, MHA, GoI September 2025\nRegional Rajbhasha Award — Office Category (1st, 2nd, 3rd) Rajbhasha Vibhag, MHA, GoI January 2026\nRegional Rajbhasha Award — Narakas Category (2nd, 3rd×2) Rajbhasha Vibhag, MHA, GoI February 2026\n33rd Aashirwad Bhasha-Setu Puraskar 2025 Utkrisht Rashtriyakrut Bank category February 2026\nStrategic Outlook — Building the Workforce of Tomorrow\nUnion Bank’s human capital roadmap emphasises digital HR transformation, AI-powered learning systems, and predictive\nanalytics for talent management. The Bank will continue expanding its leadership development programs, enhancing employee\nwellness initiatives, and strengthening its diversity and inclusion framework. Key priorities for FY 2026–27 include expanded\ndigital learning, advanced certifications, succession planning for critical roles, improved learning management systems, and\nstrengthened field enablement through standardised job aids.\n• SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being): Comprehensive wellness programs and mental health support\n• SDG 4 (Quality Education): Continuous learning initiatives and skill development programs — 23.10 lakh hours","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1037","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":408,"text":"• SDG 4 (Quality Education): Continuous learning initiatives and skill development programs — 23.10 lakh hours\n• SDG 5 (Gender Equality): Women leadership programs, Reconnect initiative, inclusive workplace policies\n• SDG 8 (Decent Work and Economic Growth): Meaningful employment and career development for 73,885 employees\n• SDG 10 (Reduced Inequalities): Inclusive hiring, support for differently-abled employees, Sign Language Training\n• SDG 16 (Peace, Justice and Strong Institutions): Ethical governance frameworks and transparent practices\n56 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nEmployee Wellness & Well-being Framework\nUnion Bank of India recognises that employee well-being is foundational to sustained organisational performance and to\ndelivering the high levels of customer service that are integral to the Bank’s brand. The Bank’s comprehensive wellness framework\naddresses physical health, mental health, financial security, and work-life balance for all employees and their families.\nWellness Dimension Initiative Description\nPhysical Health Health Check-up Camps Regular medical camps at branches and offices across zones\nMental Health Employee Assistance\nConfidential counselling and mental wellness support\nProgram\nFinancial Security Group Insurance Coverage Health, accident, and life insurance for employees and dependents","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1038","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":409,"text":"Financial Security Group Insurance Coverage Health, accident, and life insurance for employees and dependents\nHousing Employee Home Loan\nConcessional interest rates on housing loans\nConcessions\nWork-Life Balance Flexible Work Arrangements For women returning from maternity leave (Reconnect program)\nNutrition Subsidised Canteen Facilities At select offices and processing centres\nChildcare Crèche Facilities At select high-density employee locations\nDiversity, Equity & Inclusion (DEI)\nUnion Bank of India is committed to creating a diverse and inclusive workplace where all employees have equal opportunities for\ngrowth and advancement regardless of gender, region, religion, caste, or differently-abled status. DEI initiatives are embedded\nacross the entire employee lifecycle — from recruitment to career development, promotion, and leadership.\nWomen’s Leadership & Empowerment\nThe Bank has actively supported women’s career progression through dedicated leadership programs, flexible work\narrangements for women returning from maternity leave, and the establishment of 6 Nari Shakti branches led by and serving\nwomen entrepreneurs. The Reconnect Programme provides structured support for women officers returning from maternity\nleave, helping them reintegrate smoothly. Women constitute a significant proportion of the Bank’s branch-level frontline staff,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1039","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":409,"text":"leave, helping them reintegrate smoothly. Women constitute a significant proportion of the Bank’s branch-level frontline staff,\nand targeted career development programs aim to increase the share of women in senior management roles.\nDifferently-Abled Employees\nThe Bank has taken specific steps to support differently-abled employees through infrastructure accessibility improvements at\nbranches and offices, provision of assistive technologies, and Sign Language Training for staff to better serve hearing-impaired\ncustomers. The Bank’s Digital Accessible Library — the largest accessible online digital library for visually impaired users in\nthe banking sector — was maintained and expanded during FY 2025-26 through partnerships with DAISY Forum of India and\nSugamya Pustakalaya.\nRegional Representation & Language Access\nThe Bank recruits Local Bank Officers (LBOs) from different linguistic regions to improve service quality in local languages and\nstrengthen regional market penetration. Employees posted outside their home linguistic regions receive local language training\nto improve customer engagement. The Bank’s communications — both customer-facing and internal — are progressively being\nmade available in multiple regional languages, consistent with Rajbhasha policy.\n57 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1040","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":410,"text":"GRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nIndustrial Relations & Employee Engagement\nUnion Bank of India maintains constructive and cooperative industrial relations with employee unions and associations. Regular\nbipartite forums, staff meetings, and structured communication channels ensure employee concerns are heard, addressed, and\nresolved promptly. Key engagement platforms include Union Manch (cross-functional knowledge sharing), UBIQUE (employee\ninnovation submissions), structured town halls, and MD’s communication sessions that keep employees aligned with the Bank’s\nstrategic vision.\nEngagement Platform Purpose Frequency\nUnion Manch Cross-functional knowledge sharing and collaboration Ongoing\nUBIQUE Employee innovation and problem-solving submissions Annual\nMD's Communication Sessions Strategic vision alignment and two-way communication Quarterly\nTown Halls (Zonal/Regional) Local performance updates and employee engagement Quarterly\nBipartite Forums (Union Consultations) Wage negotiations, policy issues, employee welfare As required\nE-Learnathon Mandatory e-learning campaign for compliance and knowledge Annual (94%+\ncompletion)\nRashtriya Karmayogi (Seva Bhav) National public service ethos training Throughout year\n(33,300+ trained)\n58 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nManufactured Capital","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1041","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":411,"text":"Manufactured Capital\nPromoting Environmentally Conscious Financial Practices\nIntroduction\nManufactured Capital at Union Bank of India represents the physical, digital, and operational infrastructure that enables delivery\nof safe, reliable, scalable, and inclusive banking services. It includes the Bank’s branch and ATM network, digital platforms,\ndata centres, cybersecurity systems, business continuity arrangements, and outsourced service infrastructure. Manufactured\nCapital plays a strategic role in value creation, supporting enhanced customer experience and service accessibility, operational\nefficiency, scalability and cost optimisation, financial inclusion across rural, semi-urban, and underserved regions, and resilience\nagainst operational, cyber, and systemic risks.\nDuring FY 2025–26, Union Bank’s Manufactured Capital directly enabled global business growth of 10.24% to `24.86 lakh crore,\nreflecting the capacity of physical and digital infrastructure to scale operations. Sustained digital transaction growth, with\nvolume of digital transactions rising steadily through FY26, reaching approximately 37,420 lakh transactions by March 2026.\nImproved productivity metrics — Business per Branch at `274.29 crore and Business per Employee at `32.28 crore — highlight\nthe increasing efficiency, scalability, and output intensity of the Bank’s Manufactured Capital base.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1042","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":411,"text":"the increasing efficiency, scalability, and output intensity of the Bank’s Manufactured Capital base.\nPhysical Infrastructure — Branches, Offices & ATMs\nDomestic Branch & ATM Network — FY 2025–26\nInfrastructure Parameter FY 2023–24 FY 2024–25 FY 2025–26\nTotal Branches (Domestic) 8,462 8,619 8,695\nOverseas Branches 2 2 2\nTotal Branches 8,464 8,621 8,697\nTotal ATMs — 8,910 8,656\nBC Points — 23,500+ 28,035\nNew Branches Added in FY26 — — 100\nPlanned Branch Additions FY 2026–27 — — 200\nBranch Distribution by Area Category (Domestic — March 2026)\nArea Category FY 2023–24 Share FY 2024–25 Share FY 2025–26 Share\nRural 2,540 30% 2,597 30% 2,628 30%\nSemi-Urban 2,436 29% 2,521 29% 2,562 30%\nUrban 1,728 20% 1,735 20% 1,739 20%\nMetro 1,760 21% 1,766 21% 1,766 20%\nTotal Domestic 8,462 100% 8,619 100% 8,695 100%\n59 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nGeographical Region-wise Branch Distribution (March 2026)\nZone Rural Semi-Urban Urban Metro Total Share Central 709 420 331 294 1,754 20% Eastern 331 301 277 128 1,045 12% North Eastern 35 48 47 — 130 1% Northern 224 292 262 267 1,037 12% Southern 1,045 1,198 654 621 3,519 40% Western 284 303 168 456 1,210 14% Total 2,628 2,562 1,739 1,766 8,695 100%\nWith 60% of the branch network located in rural and semi-urban areas, the Bank’s infrastructure continues to enable proximity-","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1043","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":412,"text":"With 60% of the branch network located in rural and semi-urban areas, the Bank’s infrastructure continues to enable proximity-\nbased access to banking services while supporting local economic activity and financial inclusion mandates. The Southern Zone\naccounts for the largest share of branches at 40% (3,519 branches), followed by the Central Zone at 20% (1,754 branches). The\noverseas branch network spans Dubai and Sydney, supporting international banking requirements.\nSpecialised Processing Centres & Branches\nType of Branch/Centre Purpose FY 2024–25 FY 2025–26\nMSME Loan Points (MLP) Specialised credit underwriting for MSME 135 138\nUnion MSME First Branches (UMFB) Specialised branches for MSME customers 114 117\nMid Corporate Branches (MCB) Mid-segment corporate customers 38 38\nLarge Corporate Branches (LCB) Large corporate customers 12 12\nRetail Loan Points (RLP) Specialised retail loan cells 137 143\nAgriculture Loan Points (ALP) Specialised agriculture credit cells 54 76\nGold Loan Points (GLP) Specialised gold loan outlets 1,685 1,675\nStressed Asset Management Branches (SAMB) Managing stressed asset portfolio 3 3\nAsset Reconstruction Branches (ARB) Managing NPA portfolio 30 28\nNari Shakti Branches Services for women entrepreneurs — 6\nDuring FY 2025–26, the Bank launched the Nari Shakti branches, extending a specialised women-focused banking model to","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1044","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":412,"text":"During FY 2025–26, the Bank launched the Nari Shakti branches, extending a specialised women-focused banking model to\nunderserved segments. Currently 6 Nari Shakti branches are operational, offering loan and deposit products suitable for women\nentrepreneurs, advisory services, workshops for startups, skill development programs, and mentorship & networking events.\nTotal business under Nari Shakti Branches: `436 crore (Advances: `312 crore | Deposits: `124 crore). Additionally, 4 new MSME\nFirst Branches and 3 new MSME Loan Points were established during FY 2025–26, and 62 Customer Relationship Executives were\nposted at potential locations to deepen MSME relationships.\nDigital & Technology Infrastructure\nUnion Bank of India has made sustained investments in strengthening its digital and technology infrastructure, recognising\nit as a core pillar of Manufactured Capital and a key enabler of scalable, customer-centric banking. The Bank’s operations are\nsupported by a robust Core Banking System (CBS) and integrated digital platforms that enable real-time processing, scalability,\nand system reliability. The technology architecture is designed to support high transaction volumes across digital channels —\nincluding CBS, mobile banking (Union Ease and Union eBiz), internet banking, UPI, BBPS, and API-enabled services — while\nensuring data integrity and service availability.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1045","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":412,"text":"ensuring data integrity and service availability.\nThe Bank adopted an Application Modernisation platform based on Kubernetes and DevSecOps principles for the development\nand deployment of microservices-based banking applications. Microservices architecture enables scalability, resilience, faster\ntime-to-market, and independent lifecycle management of application components. This has enabled the Bank to modernise\nexisting monolithic applications, leverage cloud-native capabilities, and develop market-standard applications aligned with latest\nsoftware development best practices.\n60 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nInclusion-Enabling Infrastructure Performance Metrics (FY 2025–26)\nFinancial Inclusion Indicator FY 2025–26 Performance\nPMJDY Accounts 3.40 crore (Balance: `15,964 crore)\nPM SVANidhi Accounts 11.14 lakh accounts\nUnion Guaranteed Emergency Credit Line (UGECL) 3.92 lakh accounts\nSelf-Help Groups (SHGs) Outstanding (` Crore) `33,212 crore | 5,11,000 SHGs\nBC Points (as on March 2026) 28,035 points\nManufactured Capital Productivity & KPIs\nProductivity Indicator Mar-25 Mar-26 Change","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1046","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":413,"text":"Productivity Indicator Mar-25 Mar-26 Change\nBusiness per Branch (` Crore) 261.59 274.29 +4.9% Business per Employee (` Crore) 30.50 32.28 +5.8% Operating Profit per Branch (` Crore) 3.57 3.66 +2.5% Operating Profit per Employee (` Crore) 0.42 0.43 +2.4% Digital Transactions Volume (Lakh) — March Quarter — 37,420 — Global Business (` Lakh Crore) 22.55 23.85 +5.78%\nThe improvements in Business per Branch and Business per Employee reflect better utilisation of Manufactured Capital rather\nthan expansion-led growth alone. Zero major operational disruptions were reported despite rising transaction volumes and\ndigital adoption. Integrated assurance visibility across zones and regions enabled faster decision-making and tighter control over\ninfrastructure-related risks.\nVendor & Outsourced Infrastructure Management\nUnion Bank relies on a network of third-party vendors and service providers for certain technology and operational functions.\nThe Bank follows a structured framework for managing outsourced infrastructure, covering vendor due diligence and risk\nassessment, clearly defined service level agreements (SLAs), ongoing performance monitoring and audits, and data security and\nconfidentiality obligations. Vendor management practices are designed to minimise operational dependency risks and ensure\nservice continuity.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1047","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":413,"text":"service continuity.\nA Unified Assurance Framework was implemented across vendor operations, reducing reconciliation gaps and audit exceptions.\nAutomated reconciliation and fraud monitoring systems improved control coverage and response time. Strong data governance\nensured compliance with emerging Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act requirements.\nForward-Looking Investments — FY 2026–27\nLooking ahead, the Bank will continue to invest in strengthening its Manufactured Capital through technology modernisation\nand digital scalability, enhanced cybersecurity and resilience capabilities, branch modernisation and green infrastructure\ninitiatives, and data and analytics capabilities. These investments are aligned with regulatory expectations, national digital public\ninfrastructure initiatives, and the Bank’s ESG roadmap.\n• 200 new branch additions planned for FY 2026–27\n• Bond FRA/Future Desk: Dedicated structured derivatives desk for real-time hedging of interest rate risks\n• Enhanced digital payment infrastructure — UPI, BBPS, and API-enabled service expansion\n• Green building certifications for new and renovated branch infrastructure\n• Expanded BC network to further deepen last-mile financial inclusion coverage\n61 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nSocial & Relationship Capital","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1048","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":414,"text":"Social & Relationship Capital\nInclusive Growth | Community Development | Financial Empowerment | Stakeholder Trust\nIntroduction\nSocial and Relationship Capital encompasses the relationships, trust, shared values, and networks that Union Bank of India\nbuilds and maintains with communities, customers, government bodies, and society at large. Under the IIRC <IR> Framework,\nsocial capital reflects the Bank’s ability to create shared value through inclusive financial services, CSR investments, community\ndevelopment, and its role as a responsible corporate citizen. Union Bank of India’s social capital is anchored in its foundational\ncommitment to financial inclusion, its statutory mandate to serve priority sectors, and its voluntary investment in community\ndevelopment through the Union Bank Social Foundation Trust (UBSFT).\nIn FY 2025–26, the Bank achieved Priority Sector Lending of 41.04% of ANBC — exceeding the 40% regulatory benchmark — with\nagricultural credit at `1,83,394 crore, MSME credit at `1,62,007 crore, and retail credit at `2,53,083 crore. The Bank trained 4.33\nlakh individuals through its 42 RSETI centres with a settlement ratio of 73%, approved `61.69 crore in CSR projects across 147\ninitiatives, and maintained 3.70 crore PMJDY accounts with a balance of `15,964 crore.\nSocial Capital at a Glance — FY 2025–26\nSocial Capital Indicator FY 2025–26 Achievement","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1049","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":414,"text":"Social Capital Indicator FY 2025–26 Achievement\nCSR Donations Approved (UBSFT) `61.69 crore | 147 Projects\nCSR Amount Disbursed `35.88 crore\nPMJDY Accounts 3.40 crore (`15,964 crore balance)\nRSETIs — Persons Trained 4.33 lakh | 42 RSETI Centres | 73% settlement ratio\nSHGs Sanctioned 5,11,000 SHGs | `33,212 crore outstanding\nUnion Nari Shakti (Q4 FY26) 3,080 applications sanctioned | `560 crore\nWomen Beneficiary Outstanding (` Crore) `1,51,258 crore (+8.5% YoY)\nSmall & Marginal Farmers Outstanding (` Crore) `90,018 crore\nGreen Finance Portfolio (` Crore) `37,435 crore (Renewable: `33,572 crore)\nUnion Bank Social Foundation Trust (UBSFT) — CSR\nUnion Bank of India established the Union Bank Social Foundation Trust (UBSFT) on 2nd March 2006 as its dedicated CSR arm\nfor uplifting underprivileged communities and significantly improving the living standards of the poor and marginalised. UBSFT\nis registered under Section 12AA of the Income Tax Act, 1961, and holds a certificate under Section 80G, enabling tax-efficient\nphilanthropy for donors.\nCSR Parameter FY 2025–26\nTotal CSR Donations Approved by UBSFT `61.69 crore\nAmount Disbursed `35.88 crore\nTotal Projects / Programmes Approved 147\nCSR Focus Areas Healthcare | Education | Sanitation | Disaster Relief\nDisaster Relief Geographies Punjab, J&K, Uttarakhand, HP & Gujarat","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1050","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":414,"text":"Disaster Relief Geographies Punjab, J&K, Uttarakhand, HP & Gujarat\nRegulatory Basis Section 135 of Companies Act 2013 | up to 1% of prior year net profits\n62 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nPriority Sector Lending — FY 2025–26\nBank remains deeply committed towards extending credit facility to the needy segments of society. The Bank’s Priority Sector\nAdvance stood at ` 3,74,078 crore for FY 2025–26, with 41.04% of Adjusted Net Bank Credit (ANBC) against the statutory target\nof 40%, after netting PSLC sales/purchases and including investments in RIDF and other funds with NABARD, SIDBI, MUDRA, and\nNHB.\nParticulars (Post PSLC sale/purchase & incl. RIDF) FY 2025–26 (` Crore)\n% to ANBC — Avg of\n4 Qtrs Benchmark FY\n— Avg of 4 Qtrs\n2025–26 Priority Sector Credit 3,74,078 41.04% 40% Agriculture Priority Sector 1,77,205 19.44% 18% Small & Marginal Farmers (# post PSLC sales `10,000 Cr) 97,787 10.73% 10% Micro Enterprises 82,161 9.01% 7.50% Weaker Section (incl. net PSLC-SMF sales) 1,15,921 12.72% 12%\nThe Bank sold `10,000 crore under PSLC-Small & Marginal Farmer in FY 2025–26, earning `133.87 crore as non-interest income.\nPSLC sales and purchases are an integral part of the Bank’s priority sector management strategy, enabling it to meet specific\nsub-targets while optimising capital deployment.\nAgricultural Credit — FY 2025–26","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1051","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":415,"text":"Agricultural Credit — FY 2025–26\nAgriculture lending has always been the priority area for the Bank. Agricultural credit constitutes 18% of Gross Domestic Advances\nand 17% of Gross Global Advances of the Bank as on 31.03.2026. Bank achieved 19.44% of ANBC under Agriculture Priority for\nFY 2025–26 against a statutory target of 18%. Credit to Agriculture stood at `1,83,394 crore as on 31.03.2026.\nAgriculture Indicator FY 2025–26 Total Agricultural Credit (` Crore) `1,83,394 crore % to Gross Domestic Advances 18% % to ANBC (Agriculture Priority) 19.44% vs benchmark of 18% Credit to Small & Marginal Farmers (` Crore) — Post PSLC sale `97,787 crore SMF as % of ANBC 10.73% vs benchmark of 10% PSLC-Small & Marginal Farmer Sales (` Crore) `10,000 crore Non-Interest Income from PSLC Sales (` Crore) `133.87 crore Fresh Kisan Credit Cards Issued (Lakh) 2.58 lakh cards KCC Amount Sanctioned (` Crore) `6,462 crore Weaker Section Finance (` Crore) `1,15,921 crore (12.72% to ANBC vs benchmark of 12%)\nSpecific Lending for Social Upliftment — FY 2025–26\nWomen Beneficiaries\nWith a view to promoting entrepreneurship among women and making them self-reliant, the Bank encourages credit to women\nentrepreneurs. During FY 2025–26, total outstanding loans to women beneficiaries increased from `1,39,406 crore as of 31st\nMarch 2025 to ` 1,51,258 crore in March 2026 — a growth of 8.5%.\nMinority Communities","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1052","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":415,"text":"Minority Communities\nThe Bank extends finance to minority communities — Muslims, Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists, Zoroastrians, and Jains — in line\nwith Government of India directives on welfare of minority communities. As on March 31, 2026, outstanding credit to minority\ncommunities stood at ` 53,334 crore.\n63 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nWeaker Section\nThe Bank has been actively financing weaker sections of society. Finance to weaker sections, including net sale of PSLC-SFMF,\nstood at ` 1,15,921 crore for FY 2025–26, constituting 12.72% of ANBC against the benchmark of 12%.\nGovernment Schemes — FY 2025–26\nScheme Beneficiaries/Accounts Amount (₹ Crore)\nPMMY (Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana) 6,94,138 applications sanctioned 20,311\nPMEGP (PM Employment Generation Programme) 4,856 individuals enabled 729\nPM Vishwakarma 15,191 artisans assisted 138\nUnion Solar (New Product — Businesses for Greener Energy) 152 units assisted 190\nPM SVANidhi (Street Vendors) 11.14 lakh accounts —\nUnion Guaranteed Emergency Credit Line (UGECL) 3.92 lakh accounts —\nPMJDY Accounts 3.40 crore accounts Balance `15,964 Cr\nSelf-Help Groups (SHGs) Sanctioned 5,11,000 SHGs O/S `33,212 Cr\nUnion Nari Shakti (Q4 FY26) 3,080 applications 560\nPM Surya Ghar — Digital Sanctioning 51,700 accounts —\nRSETIs — Rural Skill Development","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1053","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":416,"text":"RSETIs — Rural Skill Development\nUnion Bank operates 42 Rural Self-Employment Training Institutes (RSETIs) across India — one of the most extensive rural\nskill development networks in the Indian banking sector. RSETIs provide free, residential skill training to rural youth, helping\nthem establish self-employment ventures and access livelihood opportunities. The programmes span traditional crafts, modern\ntechnology, healthcare trades, agriculture, and services — catering to diverse regional livelihoods. Post-training outcomes\ninclude both independent businesses and wage employment, with a settlement ratio of 73% comparing favourably with national\nbenchmarks.\nRSETI Parameter FY 2025–26\nTotal RSETI Centres 42 (including 25 in Lead Bank districts)\nPersons Trained 4.33 lakh individuals\nSettlement Ratio 73% (successful self-employment / employment outcomes)\nTraining Cost Free of charge with residential facility\nTarget Beneficiaries Rural youth from economically weaker sections\nRegional Rural Banks (RRBs) — Andhra Pradesh Grameena Bank (APGB)\nPreviously, our Bank served as the Sponsor Bank of Chaitanya Godavari Grameena Bank (CGGB). All four Regional Rural Banks\n(RRBs) in the state of Andhra Pradesh have been amalgamated into a single entity i.e. Andhra Pradesh Grameena Bank, under\nthe sponsorship of our Bank, with Head office at Guntur with effect from 01.05.2025.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1054","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":416,"text":"the sponsorship of our Bank, with Head office at Guntur with effect from 01.05.2025.\nIt has a network of 1359 CBS Branches, spread over across the Andhra Pradesh state. Andhra Pradesh Grameen Bank, with\nDeposits of ` 64,673 Crore and Advances of ` 71,664 Crore, Bank has achieved Total Business of ` 1,36,337 Crore with net profit\nof ` 1836 crore as on 31.03.2026. The Gross NPA is 0.91% and Net NPA is 0% as on 31.03.2026.\n64 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nMSME Social Capital — Schemes & Awards\nCredit Guarantee Coverage — FY 2025–26\nCredit Guarantee Scheme Accounts Guarantee Amount (₹ Crore)\nCGTMSE 2,13,516 22,589 CGFMU (including Agri Allied) 4,14,113 29,549 Total 6,27,629 52,138\nAward: The Bank received the 1st Position Award in 'Best MSME Bank' category and Runners-Up in 'Best MSME Friendly Bank' category at MSME Banking Excellence Awards FY 2024–25, conducted by Chamber of India Micro Small & Medium Enterprises (CIMSME) on 22.12.2025.\nEASE Framework — FY 2025–26 Performance\nUnion Bank of India has been an active participant in the Enhanced Access and Service Excellence (EASE) framework introduced\nby the Government of India for public sector banks. In EASE 8.0:\n• Q1 Index: Bank secured 2nd position in bank-wise rankings, featuring in top three in three out of four themes","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1055","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":417,"text":"• Q1 Index: Bank secured 2nd position in bank-wise rankings, featuring in top three in three out of four themes\n• Q2 Index: Bank secured 2nd position in bank-wise rankings and 1st position in two out of four themes\n• Q3 Index: Bank secured the top position in Theme 3 — ‘Socio-economic Impact’\nAs the nation transitions to EASE 9.0, the progress under EASE 8.0 has laid a strong foundation for building a more resilient,\nagile, and competitive institution. Under EASERise (Pillar–II) — the Bank’s specific 3-year strategic roadmap — six initiatives have\nbeen prioritised covering 37 measurable metrics: Sustainable growth in MSME, Sustainable growth in Mid Segment Corporate,\nExpanding Agriculture Investment Credit, Strengthening Compliance & Enhancing Compliance Culture, Enhancing Retail Deposit\nMobilisation, and Enhancing Customer Experience.\nStakeholder Value Created & Distributed — FY 2025–26\nStakeholder Group Channel of Value Delivery Scale\n` 90,018 Cr (SMF) | 5,11,000 SHGs\nRural Communities & Farmers\nPriority sector credit, SHG lending, Agri advances, SMF credit\nUGECL, PM SVANidhi, Mudra Yojana, PMEGP ` 20,311 Cr Mudra | 11.14 lakh SVANidhi\nMicro Entrepreneurs & Street Vendors\n` 1,51,258 Cr | 5,11,000 SHGs\nWomen & Gender Groups SHGs, Nari Shakti, APY, PMJJBY, Women Beneficiary credit\nPM Vishwakarma | Union Solar 15,191 artisans | ` 138 Cr\nArtisans & Traditional Craftspersons","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1056","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":417,"text":"Artisans & Traditional Craftspersons\nCSR — equipment, infrastructure grants 147 Projects | ` 61.69 Cr approved\nGovernment Hospitals & Schools\nRelief materials across 6 geographies Punjab, J&K, Uttarakhand, HP, Gujarat\nDisaster-Affected Communities\nRural Youth & Skill Seekers RSETIs — free residential training 4.33 Lakh trained | 73% settled\nMinority Communities Dedicated credit under GoI directive ` 53,334 Crore outstanding\nSmall Businesses (MSME) CGTMSE, CGFMU, digital MSME journeys ` 1,62,007 Cr total | ` 52,138 Cr guaranteed\n65 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nStakeholder Engagement Framework\nStakeholder Engagement Channel Frequency Key Topics Addressed\nCustomers Digital platforms, branch, call centre, surveys, DCC\nContinuous Product needs, grievances, financial literacy, digital support\nShareholders AGM, investor presentations, quarterly earnings, disclosures\nQuarterly / Annual Financial performance, strategy, dividend, governance\nEmployees PMS reviews, town halls, union forums, e-learning\nOngoing Career development, wellness, compliance, skill building\nCommunities RSETIs, CSR camps, FI drives, BC outreach\nContinuous Skill training, financial inclusion, social welfare\nGovernment / Regulators\nEASE, RBI inspections, compliance reporting\nOngoing Policy compliance, GNPA, PSL, technology, governance","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1057","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":418,"text":"Ongoing Policy compliance, GNPA, PSL, technology, governance\nEnvironment PCAF platform, ESG working groups, Climate Dialogues\nAnnual / Ongoing Financed emissions, climate risk, green targets\nCustomer Satisfaction & Grievance Redressal\nThe Bank maintains a multi-channel customer grievance redressal mechanism: branch-level complaint boxes and dedicated\ncustomer service officers; call centre support with 24×7 availability; Digital Contact Centre providing AI-powered personalised\nservice; Union Ease app-based complaint registration and tracking; an online grievance portal; and RBI Integrated Ombudsman\nScheme escalation. The Bank tracks resolution times, turnaround metrics, and customer satisfaction scores — using feedback\nto continuously improve service quality and product design. Internal audit reviews the quality and timeliness of complaint\nresolution on a quarterly basis.\nFinancial Literacy & Digital Education\nUnion Bank of India recognises that sustainable financial inclusion requires not only access to banking services but also the\nfinancial literacy to use them effectively. The Bank conducts regular financial literacy camps — particularly in rural and semi-\nurban areas — covering savings habits, responsible borrowing, digital payments, cyber safety, and insurance. RSETIs contribute","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1058","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":418,"text":"urban areas — covering savings habits, responsible borrowing, digital payments, cyber safety, and insurance. RSETIs contribute\nsignificantly to this effort, with training programmes that equip rural youth with both vocational skills and the financial knowledge\nto manage their incomes effectively and build sustainable livelihoods.\nThe Bank’s cybersecurity awareness programme is a critical component of its consumer protection mandate. During Q4 FY\n2025-26, approximately 571.56 crore cyber awareness SMS were sent to customers, and approximately 5.77 crore multilingual\ncustomer communications were dispatched. 48+ awareness sessions were conducted at RSETIs, schools, and SHGs during the\nquarter. Two focused senior citizen cybersecurity awareness programs were held — a segment particularly vulnerable to digital\nfraud — and 2 programs specifically addressing differently-abled community members were also conducted.\nFinancial Literacy Initiative Target Audience Coverage — FY 2025-26\nCyber Awareness SMS Campaigns All bank customers 571.56 crore SMS (Q4 FY26)\nMultilingual Awareness Communications Regional language customer base 5.77 crore communications\nAwareness Sessions at RSETIs/Schools/SHGs Rural communities, students 48+ sessions in Q4 FY26\nSenior Citizen Cyber Safety Programs Elderly account holders 2 dedicated programs","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1059","section":"Sustainability / BRSR","tag":"esg","page":418,"text":"Senior Citizen Cyber Safety Programs Elderly account holders 2 dedicated programs\nRSETI Skill + Financial Training Rural youth — economically weaker sections 4.33 lakh trained | 73% settled\nBC Agent Training (Financial Literacy) Business Correspondents 2,800+ BCs trained\n66 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1060","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":419,"text":"Corporate Governance & Ethics\nUnion Bank of India’s corporate governance framework is anchored by a strong Board of Directors comprising experienced\nprofessionals from diverse backgrounds including banking, finance, law, economics, and public policy. The Board oversees the\nBank’s strategic direction, risk management, financial performance, regulatory compliance, and ESG commitments. Key Board\ncommittees provide specialised oversight — the Audit Committee on financial controls and reporting, the Risk Management\nCommittee on credit and market risks, the Stakeholders Relationship Committee on customer and employee welfare, and the\nESG Steering Committee on environmental and social commitments.\nThe Bank’s Code of Conduct and Ethics applies to all employees, officers, and directors, setting clear expectations for integrity,\ntransparency, and accountability. A robust whistleblower policy encourages employees to report suspected violations without\nfear of retaliation. Anti-money laundering (AML), Know Your Customer (KYC), and anti-bribery controls are embedded in all\ncustomer-facing and operational processes, with mandatory e-learning modules ensuring that every officer is trained on these\ncritical compliance areas.\nThe compliance culture has been systematically strengthened through the EASE framework’s Compliance theme, which","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1061","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":419,"text":"The compliance culture has been systematically strengthened through the EASE framework’s Compliance theme, which\nbenchmarks compliance quality across public sector banks. Regular internal audits, concurrent audits, and RBI inspections\nprovide independent assurance on the effectiveness of controls. The Unified Assurance Framework integrates risk, compliance,\nmonitoring, and audit across all zones and regions, enabling centralised visibility and faster remediation of control exceptions.\nBoard Committees & ESG Governance Structure\nCommittee Composition ESG / Risk Mandate\nBoard of Directors MD & CEO, Executive Directors, Independent Directors, Government Nominee Director\nOverall strategic oversight including ESG, risk, and financial performance\nRisk Management Committee (RMC)\nBoard Sub-committee with independent directors\nOversight of credit, market, operational, liquidity, and climate risks\nAudit Committee Independent Directors — majority Financial reporting integrity, internal controls, statutory compliance\nStakeholders Relationship Committee\nBoard members Customer grievance, employee welfare, and social responsibility\nESG Steering Committee (ESGSC)\nSenior management with Board oversight Strategic coordination of ESG agenda across all verticals\nCredit Risk Management Committee (CRMC)\nSenior management committee Credit risk policy, sector limits, NPA management","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1062","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":419,"text":"Senior management committee Credit risk policy, sector limits, NPA management\nALCO (Asset-Liability Committee)\nSenior management committee Liquidity, interest rate, and market risk management\n67 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nGRI Standards Index\nDisclosure Against GRI Standards | ISSB S1 & S2 | TNFD Reporting Index\nUnion Bank of India's Integrated Annual Report 2025–26 has been prepared in accordance with the GRI Standards: Core option. The report also draws on the ISSB S1 (General Requirements) and ISSB S2 (Climate-related Disclosures) frameworks, as well as the Taskforce on Nature-related Financial Disclosures (TNFD) recommendations. The Bank is committed to progressive improvement in the quality, completeness, and comparability of its sustainability disclosures.\nGRI 2: General Disclosures\nDisclosure Requirement Section/Sub-section Page Reference\n2-1 Organizational details Value Creation Story | Infrastructure Overview — Manufactured Capital\nFinancial & Manufactured Capital\nSubsidiaries and Joint Ventures Manufactured Capital\n2-2 Entities included in sustainability reporting\nValue Creation Story\n2-6 Activities, value chain and business relationships\nBank's Value Creation Model | Capital Interconnectedness\nNatural Capital\n2-22 Statement on sustainable development strategy","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1063","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":420,"text":"2-22 Statement on sustainable development strategy\nOur Natural Capital Philosophy | Value Creation Story\nNatural Capital\n2-23 Policy commitments ESG Governance Structure | Sustainable Finance Framework\n2-29 Approach 