nt & Miscellaneous Provisions) Scheme, 1970 (as amended), the Management Committee of the Board consists of -\n12 22 August 2025 28 26 February 2026\n13 30 August 2025 29 17 March 2026\n14 09 September 2025 30 24 March 2026\nMembers\nSl. No.\n15 18 September 2025 31 30 March 2026\n16 26 September 2025\n1 Managing Director & CEO\n5.2 Audit Committee of the Board (ACB)\n2 Executive Directors\n5.2.1 Composition:\n3 RBI Nominee Director\nThe Audit Committee of the Board (ACB) has been constituted by the Bank as per the guidelines of Reserve Bank of India and Ministry of Finance, Government of India. The ACB at present consists of following members –\n4 Three other Non-Executive Directors under Section 9 (3) (e), (f), (h) & (i) nominated by the Board for a period of one year and may be re- nominated thereafter for a period of six months each for two times.\nSl. No. Members","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1356","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":521,"text":"Sl. No. Members\nShri Asheesh Pandey, Managing Director & CEO of the Bank chaired the Committee.\n1 Nominees of Govt. of India & Reserve Bank of India\n5.1.2 Functions:\n2 Three other Non-Executive Directors / Independent Directors\nPursuant to the directives of Ministry of Finance, Government of India, Management Committee of the Board is constituted by the Board of Directors for considering various business matters viz. sanctioning/ review of credit proposals, loan compromise/write-off proposals, approval of capital and revenue expenditure beyond the powers of Credit Approval Committee-I, acquisition and hiring of premises, investments, donations, etc.\nExecutive Directors are the invitees to the meeting.\nShri Suraj Srivastava, Non-Executive Independent Director chaired the Committee.\nCompany Secretary acts as Secretary to the ACB in terms of Regulation 18(1)(e) of the SEBI (LODR) Regulations, 2015.\n169 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCorporate Governance Report\nThe Committee met 13 times during the year 2025-26.\n5.2.2 Functions:\nDetails of meetings are given below:\nThe ACB reviews the functions of the Bank as mandated\nby calendar of items issued by the RBI. The major\nDate of Meeting\nSl. No.\nDate of Meeting Sl. No.\nfunctions of ACB are enumerated below:\n1. 08 May 2025 8. 17 October 2025","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1357","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":522,"text":"1. 08 May 2025 8. 17 October 2025\n1. ACB provides directions as also oversees the\noperation of the total audit function in the Bank.\n2. 22 May 2025 9. 30 October 2025\nTotal audit function implies the organization,\n3. 24 June 2025 10. 24 December 2025\noperationalization and quality control of internal\n4. 19 July 2025 11. 14 January 2026\naudit and inspection within the Bank and follow-up\n5. 24 July 2025 12. 06 March 2026\non the statutory / external audit of the Bank and\n6. 30 August 2025 13. 16 March 2026\ninspection by RBI.\n7. 18 September 2025\n2. ACB reviews the internal inspection/audit functions\nin the Bank i.e. the system, its quality and\n5.3 Risk Management Committee (RMC)\neffectiveness in terms of follow-up. It reviews the\n5.3.1 Composition:\ninspection reports of specialized and extra-large\nbranches and all branches with unsatisfactory\nThe Bank had constituted Supervisory Committee\nratings. It also specially focuses on the follow-up of:-\nof Directors on Risk and Asset Liability Management\nin terms of RBI guidelines, now covered under\n• Inter-branch adjustment accounts\nprovisions of Reserve Bank of India (Commercial\n• Un-reconciled long outstanding entries in Inter-\nBanks-Governance) Directions, 2025. In terms of SEBI\nBank accounts and Nostro accounts\n(LODR) Regulations, 2015, the Board of Directors shall\nconstitute a Risk Management Committee (RMC) in top","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1358","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":522,"text":"constitute a Risk Management Committee (RMC) in top\n• Arrears in balancing of books at various\n1000 listed entities determined on the basis of market\nbranches\ncapitalization. Considering the similarity in functions,\n• Frauds\nthe Board in its meeting dated 06.12.2019 changed\nthe name of Supervisory Committee of Directors on\n• All other major areas of housekeeping.\nRisk & Asset Liability Management (SCR & ALM) to Risk\n3. ACB obtains and reviews quarterly reports from the\nManagement Committee (RMC).\nCompliance Officers appointed in the Bank in terms\nThe Committee consists of the following members :\nof guidelines of RBI and SEBI.\nMembers\nSl. No.\n4. Regarding statutory audits, ACB follows up on all\nthe issues raised in the Long Form Audit Reports. It\n1 Non-executive Director (Chairman)\ninteracts with the external auditors before and after\nthe finalization of annual / semi-annual financial\n2 Managing Director & CEO\naccounts and on the audit reports.\n3 Three Non-Executive / Independent Directors\n5. ACB reviews the accounting policies and practices,\n5.3.2 Functions:\nrelated party transactions, Mechanism for Whistle-\nThe Committee is constituted to supervise the functions","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1359","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":522,"text":"Blower, Management Discussion and Analysis and\nof Risk and Asset Liability Management in the Bank. The\nQuarterly and Annual Financial Results of the Bank.\nCommittee is responsible for identifying, evaluating and\n6. In addition to the above, the functions of the Audit\nmonitoring the overall risks faced by the Bank.\nCommittee include the role of the Audit Committee\nMinistry of Finance, Govt. of India vide letter no. F.No.\nand review of information by audit committee as\n16/19/2019-BO.Idated 30.08.2019, envisaged institution\ndefined under Part - C of Schedule II - Corporate\nof a Risk Appetite Framework for a structured approach\nGovernance of SEBI (LODR) Regulations, 2015.\nto manage, measure and control risk, consisting of - i.\n170 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nFunctions:\nA Risk Appetite Statement and Risk Limits for the Bank; ii. Policies, Processes, Controls and Systems for both material and reputational risks iii. Delineation of roles and responsibilities for overseeing implementation and monitoring.\nThe major function of the Special Committee is to monitor and review all the cases of frauds of Rs 1 crore and above so as to:\n• Identify the systemic lacunae, if any that facilitated perpetration of the fraud and put in place measures to plug the same.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1360","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":523,"text":"• Identify the systemic lacunae, if any that facilitated perpetration of the fraud and put in place measures to plug the same.\nIt was further envisaged that the Risk Management Committee may be given a mandate to periodically review adherence to the Risk Appetite framework of the Bank and to fix accountability in the event of breach of approved Risk Appetite\n• Identify the reasons for delay in detection, if any and/or reporting to top management of the Bank and RBI.\nThe Committee met 4 times during the year 2025-26. Dr. Priti Jay Rao is the chairperson of this committee.\n• Monitor progress of CBI /Police Investigation and recovery position.\n• Ensure that the staff accountability is examined at all levels in all the cases of frauds and staff side action, if required, is completed quickly without loss of time.\nSl.\nDate of Meeting Sl.\nDate of Meeting\nNo.\nNo.\n1. 20 June 2025 3. 05 January 2026\n2. 21 August 2025 4. 16 March 2026\n• Review the efficacy of the remedial action taken to prevent recurrence of frauds, such as strengthening of internal controls.\n5.4 Special Committee of the Board for monitoring and follow-up cases of Frauds (SCBMF)\n• Put in place other measures as may be considered relevant to strengthen preventive measures against frauds.\n5.4.1 Composition:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1361","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":523,"text":"5.4.1 Composition:\nSpecial Committee of the Board of Directors for monitoring of frauds of `1 crore and above is constituted as per the guidelines issued by Reserve Bank of India. At present the Audit Committee of Board (ACB) is required to oversee the internal inspection, statutory audit, inter branch/inter bank accounts and major areas of housekeeping etc. The ACB is also required to focus attention on preventive aspects and follow–up action being initiated by the bank on frauds. However, this Special Committee focuses on Monitoring and following up of cases of frauds involving amounts of `1 crore and above exclusively while ACB continues to monitor all the cases of frauds in general.\nThe Committee met 4 times during the year 2025-26. Shri Prakash Chandra Kandpal chaired the committee under report.\nSl.\nDate of Meeting Sl.\nDate of Meeting\nNo.\nNo.\n1. 23 May 2025 3. 23 December 2025\n2. 21 August 2025 4. 16 March 2026\n5.5 Recovery Management Committee of the Board (ReMC)\n5.5.1 Composition:\nThe Special Committee is constituted with following members of the Board of Directors:\nA Board level Sub Committee for Recovery Management has been formed as per Ministry of Finance, Government of India guidelines to monitor the progress in recovery on regular basis and this Committee would submit its report to the Board.\nMembers\nSl. No.\n1 Non-executive Director (Chairman)\nMembers\nSl. No.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1362","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":523,"text":"Sl. No.\n2 Managing Director & CEO\n1 Managing Director & CEO\n3 Two members from the Audit Committee of the Board\n2 Executive Directors\n3 Government of India Nominee Director\n4 Two other members from the Board excluding RBI nominee Director\nShri Asheesh Pandey, Managing Director & CEO of the Bank is the chairperson of the Committee.\n171 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1363","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":524,"text":"Corporate Governance Report\n5.5.2 Functions:\n5. IT automation of all HR related activities\nTo monitor the progress in recovery on regular basis and submit the report to the Board.\nThe Committee has held 3 meetings during the year 2025-26.\nThe Committee has held 3 meetings during the year 2025-26.\nNo. Date of Meeting S l .\nSl.\nNo. Date of Meeting\n1. 23 May 2025 3. 21 January 2026 2. 22 September 2025\nSl. No. Date of Meeting Sl. No. Date of Meeting\n5.7 Stakeholders Relationship Committee of the Board (SRC)\n1. 24 June 2025 3. 05 January 2026\n5.7.1. Composition:\n2. 29 September 2025\nPursuant to the SEBI (Listing Obligations & Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, the Stakeholders Relationship Committee (SRC) has been constituted with Executive Directors and Three Non-Executive Directors.\n5.6 Human Resources Sub-Committee of the Board (HRSC)\n5.6.1 Composition:\nThe Committee consists of Managing Director & CEO, Executive Directors, Government Nominee Director and any two Directors nominated by the Board. In addition, two experts in Human Resources also participate as special invitees.\nShri Prakash Chandra Kandpal, Shareholder Director, chaired the committee under report.\n5.7.2. Functions:\nShri Asheesh Pandey, Managing Director & CEO of the Bank is the chairperson of the Committee.\nStakeholders related:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1364","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":524,"text":"Stakeholders related:\n1. Monitoring and resolving the grievances of the security holders of the Bank including complaints related to transfer/transmission of shares, non- receipt of annual report, non-receipt of declared dividends, issue of new/duplicate certificates, general meetings etc.\n5.6.2 Functions:\nTo oversee & review the implementation of following aspects:\n1. Overall Strategy for the Bank on HR.\n• Overall manpower plan and skills gap identification.\n2. Review of measures taken for effective exercise of voting rights by shareholders.\n• Systems, procedures and structures to attract and groom right talent.\n3. Review of adherence to the service standards adopted by the Bank in respect of various services being rendered by the Registrar & Share Transfer Agent.\n• Succession planning.\n2. Development of performance management system covering all staff in the Bank\n• Performance assessment on transparent Key Responsibility Areas.\n4. Review of the various measures and initiatives taken by the Bank for reducing the quantum of unclaimed dividends and ensuring timely receipt of dividend warrants/annual reports/statutory notices by the shareholders of the company.\n• System of providing developmental feedback to all staff.\n3. Fine tuning of policies on HR in line with Bank’s strategy and market realities.\nCustomer Service related:\n• Reward and incentives","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1365","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":524,"text":"• Reward and incentives\n1. To oversee the functions of overall customer grievance redressal in the Bank\n• Promotions\n• Deployment\n2. To monitor the customer services and guide in improving the customer service in the Bank\n4. Training\n• Specialist business skills training\n3. To frame and review the Policies in the interest of customers’ interest protection.\n• General retraining / reorientation for all staff\n172 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nCorporate Social Responsibility related:\n5.8 Information Technology Strategy Committee (ITSC)\n1. To review the Corporate Social Responsibility Policy of the Bank\n5.8.1 Composition:\nAs a part of IT Governance measures, RBI has recommended creation of IT Strategy Committee of the Board to advise the Board on strategic direction on IT and to review IT Investments on behalf of the Board. The Committee consists of:\n2. To approve and review the activities / projects undertaken by the Union Bank Social Foundation Trust periodically.\nESG related:\n1. To exercise oversight over ESG related activities.\nSl. No. Members\n2. Provide strategic guidance and oversight over all matters and activities relating to ESG;\n1. Managing Director & CEO\n2. Executive Directors\n3. Monitor implementation and execution of ESG related initiatives and policies;\n3. Govt. Nominee Director","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1366","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":525,"text":"3. Govt. Nominee Director\n4. Two Non-Executive Directors one of whom shall be independent Director\n4. Assess impact of various ESG initiatives;\n5. Review disclosure of ESG matters to internal and external stakeholders;\n5. One Outside IT Expert\n6. Chief Information Officer (CGM/GM heading the IT function of the Bank)\n6. Identify and recommend to the Board and the Committees of the Bank, the emerging risks related to ESG\nThe Committee has held 4 meetings during the year 2025-26\nThe Committee has held 4 meetings during the year 2025-26.\nSl. No. Date of Meeting Sl. No. Date of Meeting\nSl. No. Date of Meeting Sl. No. Date of Meeting\n1. 24 April 2025 3. 23 December 2025\n2. 22 August 2025 4. 16 March 2026\n1. 20 June 2025 3. 23 December 2025 2. 22 September 2025 4. 16 March 2026\nShri Prakash Chandra Kandpal, Shareholder Director chaired the committee under report.\n5.7.3. Name & Designation of Compliance Officer:\nPursuant to Regulation 6 of SEBI (Listing Obligations & Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, Shri Ashish Mishra has been appointed as Company Secretary and designated as the Compliance Officer of the Bank for Investor Grievances w.e.f. 28.01.2026.\n5.8.2 Functions:\n• Approving IT strategy and policy documents.\n• Ensuring that the management has put an effective strategic planning process in place.\n5.7.4.Details of Shareholder Complaints during the year 2025-26:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1367","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":525,"text":"5.7.4.Details of Shareholder Complaints during the year 2025-26:\n• Ratifying that the business strategy is indeed aligned with IT strategy.\nA comparative chart showing number of complaints received, responded and pending for the financial year ended 31.03.2026 vis-à-vis 31.03.2025 is as under: -\n• Ensuring that the IT organizational structure complements the business model and its direction.\n• Ascertaining that management has implemented processes and practices that ensure that the IT delivers value to the business.\nFor F.Y.\nFor F.Y.\nSr. No Particulars\nended 31.03.25 a. No. of shareholders complaints pending at the beginning of the year\nended 31.03.26\n• Ensuring IT investments represent a balance of risks & benefits and that budgets are acceptable.\n0 0\n• Monitoring the methods that management uses to determine the IT resources needed to achieve strategic goals and provide high level direction for sourcing & use of IT resources.\n22 15\nb. No. of shareholders complaints received during the year\n22 15\nc. No. of shareholders complaints resolved during the year\n• Ensuring proper balance of IT investments for sustaining bank’s growth.\n0 0\nd. No. of shareholders complaints pending at the end of the year\n173 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCorporate Governance Report","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1368","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":526,"text":"Corporate Governance Report\n• Ensure adequate mitigation for exposure towards IT risks & controls, evaluating effectiveness of management’s monitoring.\nCompanies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings)\nAct, 1970. The Composition of the Committee at present\nis as under:\n• Assessing Senior Management’s performance in implementing IT strategies.\nSl. No. Members\n• Issuing high level policy guidance (e.g. related to risk, funding or sourcing tasks).\n1 Non-executive Director (Chairman)\n• Confirming whether IT or business architecture is to be designed, so as to derive maximum business value from IT.\n2 Two Non-Executive Directors nominated under section 9(3)(h) of the Act\n• Overseeing the aggregate funding of IT at a bank- level, and ascertaining if the management has resources to ensure the proper management of IT risks.\n5.9.2 Functions:\nTo undertake a process of due diligence to determine\nthe ‘fit and proper’ status of the persons to be elected as\n• Reviewing IT performance measurement and contribution of IT to business (i.e. delivering the promised value).\ndirectors under Section 9(3)(i) of the Banking Companies\n(Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act, 1970.\nShri Prakash Chandra Kandpal, Shareholder Director\n• To build up mechanism to undertake IT disaster management.\nchaired the committee under report.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1369","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":526,"text":"chaired the committee under report.\n• To act as Board level Sub-Committee on Digital Transactions to advise, guide and monitor enhancing digital transactions of the Bank.\nThe Committee met 2 times during FY 2025-26.\nSl. No. Date of Meeting\n5.9 Nomination & Remuneration Committee (NRC)\n1. 20 June 2025\nThe Bank earlier had two separate Committees viz. Nomination Committee and Remuneration Committee constituted in terms of earlier guidelines issued by RBI and MOF. MOF vide its communication F. No. 16/19/2019-BO.1 dated 30.08.2019 advised that in place of separate Nomination Committee of the Board and Remuneration Committee of the Board, the Bank may constitute a single committee named Nomination and Remuneration Committee for carrying out the functions entrusted to the said two committees and having composition as specified Reserve Bank of India (Commercial Banks-Governance) Directions, 2025 dated 28th November 2025.\n2. 15 September 2025\n5.10 Disciplinary Proceedings Committee – Vigilance/Non-\nvigilance (DPC)\nThe Bank earlier had two separate Committee viz.\nDirectors Promotion Committee (DPC) and Disciplinary\nProceedings Committee - Vigilance/Non-Vigilance\n(DPC-V).\nMOF vide its communication F. No. 16/19/2019-BO.1\ndated 30.08.2019 advised to review in its Board the\nneed for continuation of Board committees set up","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1370","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":526,"text":"need for continuation of Board committees set up\nThus, pursuant to the above-mentioned MOF guidelines, the Board of Directors approved the constitution of a single Nomination and Remuneration Committee (NRC) in place of two separate Committees.\nat the bank’s own initiative and the possibility of their\nfunctions being discharged by another Board committee\nor the Board, with a view to rationalise their number.\n5.9.1 Composition:\nThe Board of Directors with a view to rationalize its\nAs per the Reserve Bank of India (Commercial Banks- Governance) Directions, 2025 dated 28th November 2025, In terms of Para 22 of this directions, the Bank is required to constitute a Nomination and Remuneration Committee for undertaking a process of due diligence to determine the ‘fit and proper’ status of the persons to be elected as directors under Section 9(3)(i) of the Banking\nCommittees and considering the MOF guidelines dated\n24.10.1990 and basic composition, decided to merge\nDirectors’ Promotion Committee and Disciplinary\nProceedings Committee - Vigilance/ Non-Vigilance w.e.f.\n06.12.2019.\n174 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\n5.10.1 Composition:\n5.11.2 Functions:\nThe Board of Directors has approved the constitution of the Committee as below-\nWith a view to affecting speedy transfer of shares, the","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1371","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":527,"text":"With a view to affecting speedy transfer of shares, the\nBank has constituted a Share Transfer Committee of the\nBoard with powers to confirm transfer, transmission,\nSl. No. Members\ndemat and issue of duplicate shares etc.\n1 Managing Director & CEO\nFurther, in the interest of investors and with a view\n2 Government Nominee Director\nto enhance ease of dealing in securities markets by\ninvestors, SEBI, vide its circular dated 25.01.2022,\n3 RBI Nominee Director\ndecided that the listed entities shall henceforth issue the\nIndependent members / Outside experts to be inducted while conducting interview for the promotion process from Scale VI to VII and Scale VII to VIII.\nshares in dematerialized mode only while processing\nthe following service requests:\na. Issue of duplicate securities certificate.\nShri Asheesh Pandey, Managing Director & CEO of the Bank chaired the Committee.\nb. Claim from Unclaimed Suspense Account.\n5.10.2 Functions:\nc. Renewal / Exchange of securities certificate.\n• To conduct Promotion Process from TEGS VI to TEGS VII and TEGS VII to TEGS VIII, consider appeals of Executives in TEGS VI & VII against non-promotions to TEGS VII & VIII respectively,\nd. Endorsement.\ne. Sub-division / Splitting of securities certificate.\nf. Consolidation of securities certificates / folios.\n• to consider promotions to TEGS VII & VIII in cases where Sealed Cover Procedure is adopted","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1372","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":527,"text":"• to consider promotions to TEGS VII & VIII in cases where Sealed Cover Procedure is adopted\ng. Transmission.\nh. Transposition.\n• To review Vigilance, Non-Vigilance disciplinary cases and departmental enquiries.\nThe Committee met 4 times during FY 2025-26.\n• To review APAR marks of Top Executives upon their representation within 15 days from the date of disclosure.\nSl. No. Date of Meeting Sl. No. Date of Meeting\n1. 20 June 2025 3. 23 December 2025\n• To review the appeal against the Regular Departmental Action for major penalty for General Managers\n2. 29 August 2025 4. 17 March 2026\n5.12 Review Committee of the Board for Willful Defaulters\nThe Committee has held 2 meetings during the year 2025-26.\n(RCBWD)\nThe Bank earlier had two separate Committee\nSl. No. Date of Meeting\nviz. Review Committee for Classification of Non-\nCooperative Borrower (RCNCB) and Review Committee\n1. 15 September 2025\non Willful Defaulters of the Bank (RCBWD). MOF vide\n2. 21 January 2026\nits communication F. No. 16/19/2019-BO.1 dated\n30.08.2019 advised to review in its Board the need for\n5.11 Share Transfer Committee of the Board (STCB)\ncontinuation of Board committees set up at the bank’s\n5.11.1 Composition:\nown initiative and the possibility of their functions being\nThe Committee consists of:\ndischarged by another Board committee or the Board,\nwith a view to rationalise their number.\nSl. No. Members","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1373","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":527,"text":"Sl. No. Members\nThe Board of Directors with a view to rationalize its\n1 Managing Director & CEO or in absence, Executive Director in charge of Board Secretariat\ncommittees and based on the composition of the\nabove two committees, decided to merge the same and\n2 Two Non-Executive Directors\nto constitute a single Committee for identification of\nWillful Defaulters and classification of Non-Cooperative\nShri Asheesh Pandey, MD & CEO of the Bank chaired the Committee.\nBorrowers namely Review Committee for Non-\n175 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCorporate Governance Report\nemployees, customers, existing shareholders and/ or any other persons as decided by the Board and as provided under SEBI regulations and the timing of such issue(s), calling the issue open at its discretion subject to applicable Rules and Regulations and GoI & RBI approval.\nCooperative Borrowers and Willful Defaulters with the following composition w.e.f. 06.12.2019 -\nSl. No. Members\n1 Managing Director & CEO\nThe Committee has held one meeting on 14 August 2025 during the year 2025-26.\n2 Any two Independent Directors\n5.14 Board Committee for Performance Evaluation (BCPE)\nShri Asheesh Pandey, Managing Director & CEO of the Bank chaired the Committee.\n5.14.1 Composition:\n5.12.1 Functions:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1374","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":528,"text":"5.12.1 Functions:\nMinistry of Finance vide communication no. F. No. 9/5/2009-IR dated 30.08.2019 advised the Bank to constitute a Board Committee for Performance Evaluation of Managing Directors & CEO, Executive Directors in charge of internal Control Functions (Risk, Compliance and Audit) and General Managers in charge of internal control Functions (Risk, Compliance and Audit) of the bank.\n• The committee shall review the orders of the Approving Committee i.e. Executive Director Headed Committee recording the Borrower to be non-cooperative. The order shall become final only after it is confirmed by the Review Committee of the Board.\n• On a half-yearly basis reviews the status of non- cooperative borrowers for deciding whether their names can be declassified as evidenced by their return to credit discipline and cooperative dealings before its submission to the Board.\nFurther as per MOF communication dated 14.11.2019, the Board Committee for Performance Evaluation is to be constituted with the approval of the Board with following members -\n• Review & confirm the orders passed by Committee headed by Executive Director on classification of Borrowers as Willful Defaulters.\nSl. No. Members\n1 Non-Executive Director (Chairman)\n• Reviewing the quarterly Return submitted to CRILC.\n2 Government Nominee Director\nThe Committee has held 3 meetings during the year 2025-26.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1375","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":528,"text":"The Committee has held 3 meetings during the year 2025-26.\n3 Longest serving Shareholder Director on the Board\nIn case of vacancy in the office of NEC, the Chairman of Audit Committee of the Board shall be a member of the Committee in place of NEC.\nDate of Meeting\nSl. No.\nDate of Meeting Sl. No.\n5.14.2 Functions:\n1. 25 June 2025 3. 24 March 2026\nTo appraise, review and accept the Annual Performance Appraisal Reports of the Managing Director and CEOs, Executive Directors and Chief General Managers in charge of Risk, Compliance and Audit.\n2. 18 July 2025\n5.13 Committee of Directors for Raising of Capital Fund (CDRCF)\n5.13.1 Composition:\nThe Committee has held One meeting on 22 September 2025 during the year 2025-26.\nAs per the approval given by the Board the committee is constituted to complete the necessary formalities for raising capital funds. The Committee consists of MD & CEO and Executive Directors.\n5.15 Credit Approval Committee-I (CAC-I)\n5.15.1 Composition:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1376","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":528,"text":"5.15.1 Composition:\nAs per clause 13A of the Nationalised Banks (Management and Miscellaneous Provisions) Scheme, 1970, the Bank has constituted the Credit Approval Committee-I. The Committee shall exercise the powers of the Board in respect of any single credit proposal upto ` 800 crore (in case of A & above externally rated accounts having valid rating) and up to ` 600 crores in case of other accounts and group exposure upto ` 800 crore and in case exposure exceeds such limits, it shall be considered by the Management Committee of the Board.\nShri Asheesh Pandey, Managing Director & CEO of the Bank chaired the Committee.\n5.13.2 Functions:\nThe Committee is authorized by the Board / Shareholders, as the case may be to complete the necessary formalities for raising of capital funds and to do all such acts, deeds, and things as it may in its absolute discretion deem necessary, proper and desirable including but not limited to decide on quantum & mode(s), number of tranches, price or prices, discount/premium, reservations to\n176 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nThe composition of CAC-I is as under:\n5.16 Board Committee for Compromise Settlements (BCCS)\n5.16.1 In terms of RBI Communication RBI/2023-24/40– DOR.STR.REC.20/21.04.048/2023-\nSl. No. Members","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1377","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":529,"text":"Sl. No. Members\n1 Managing Director & CEO 2 Executive Directors 3 Chief General Manager / General Manager in- charge of the Credit 4 Chief General Manager / General Manager in- charge of the Finance/Chief Financial Officer 5 Chief General Manager / General Manager in- charge of Risk Management\n24 dated June 8, 2023, a new Committee with the name Board Committee for Compromise Settlements (BCCS) is constituted for approval of Compromise Settlements. The delegation powers for such approvals vest with an authority which is at least one level higher in hierarchy than the authority vested with power to sanction the credit/investment exposure\nShri Asheesh Pandey, Managing Director & CEO of the Bank chaired the Committee.\n5.16.2 Composition: The committee shall consist of:\nSl. No. Members\n5.15.2 Functions:\nAll credit related matters including approval/review- renewal, miscellaneous requests, interest concessions, compromise/write off proposals, approval of capital and revenue expenditure, acquisition and hiring of premises, etc. within its delegated authority are being put up before the CAC-I for approval.\n1 Managing Director & Chief Executive Officer (Permanent Member)\n2 All Executive Directors (Permanent Members)\n3 Two Non-Executive Directors/Independent Directors nominated under u/s 9 (3) (g) /(h) / (i) of the Act.\nThe Committee has held 25 meetings during the year 2025-26.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1378","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":529,"text":"The Committee has held 25 meetings during the year 2025-26.\n5.16.3 Functions:\nSl. No. Date of Meeting Sl. No. Date of Meeting\n• The credit proposals sanctioned by MCB, for compromise settlement and Technical write off the proposals shall be placed to this committee.\n1 09 April 2025 14 20 October 2025\n2 24 April 2025 15 29 October 2025\n3 07 May 2025 16 19 November 2025\n• The Compromise settlements and technical write offs approved by the MD & CEO/ BCCS would be reported to the Board on quarterly basis.\n4 19 May 2025 17 10 December 2025\n5 09 June 2025 18 16 December 2025\n6 21 June 2025 19 24 December 2025\nManaging Director & CEO to be the Chairman of the Committee.\n7 14 July 2025 20 05 January 2026\n8 29 July 2025 21 17 January 2026\n5.16.4 Frequency: As and when required\n9 12 August 2025 22 11 February 2026\n10 28 August 2025 23 05 March 2026\n5.17 The details of Directors, their attendance in the Board and other Committee Meetings during the year is appended at the end of this Corporate Governance Report.\n11 19 September 2025 24 23 March 2026\n12 25 September 2025 25 25 March 2026\n13 17 October 2025\n6. GENERAL BODY MEETINGS\nThe details of the General Body Meetings of the Shareholders held during last 3 years are given below:\nNature of Meeting Date & Time Venue Special Resolution\nCentral Office, Union Bank, Mumbai through VC or OAVM","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1379","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":529,"text":"Central Office, Union Bank, Mumbai through VC or OAVM\nRaising of Capital of the Bank by way of issuance of fresh Equity Shares and / or by issuance of Additional Tier-1 / Tier-2 Capital as per BASEL III Guidelines for an amount not exceeding ` 10,100 Crore. Appointment of Shri Laxman S Uppar (DIN: 02453845) as Part - Time Non- Official (Independent) Director of the Bank. Appointment of Shri Srinivasan Varadarajan (DIN: 00033882) as Part-Time Non-Official (Independent) Director and Non-Executive Chairman of the Bank.\n21st Annual General Meeting\n4th August,2023 at 11:00 a.m.\n177 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCorporate Governance Report\nNature of Meeting Date & Time Venue Special Resolution\nCentral Office, Union Bank, Mumbai through VC or OAVM\nRaising of Capital of the Bank by way of issuance of fresh Equity Shares and / or by issuance of Additional Tier-1 / Tier-2 Capital as per BASEL III Guidelines for an aggregate amount not exceeding ` 10,000 crore.\n26th July 2024 at 11:00 a.m.\n22nd Annual General Meeting\nTo elect two Directors from amongst the shareholders of the Bank, other than the Central Government.\nCentral Office, Union Bank, Mumbai through VC or OAVM\nRaising of Capital of the Bank by way of issuance of fresh Equity Shares and / or by issuance of Additional Tier-1 / Tier-2 Capital as per BASEL III Guidelines.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1380","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":530,"text":"Raising of Capital of the Bank by way of issuance of fresh Equity Shares and / or by issuance of Additional Tier-1 / Tier-2 Capital as per BASEL III Guidelines.\n23rd Annual General Meeting\n01st August 2025 at 11:00 a.m.\nAppointment of Shri Suraj Srivastava (DIN: 09444372) as Part-Time Non- Official Director of the Bank.\nAppointment of Shri Rohan Chand Thakur (DIN: 07944078) as Government Nominee Director of the Bank.\nAppointment of M/s Ragini Chokshi & Co., Company Secretaries as a Secretarial Auditor of the Bank for a period of 2 years from FY2025-26 to FY 2026-27.\n7. DISCLOSURES\ndirectors are as per clause 8 of the Nationalised Banks (Management and Miscellaneous Provisions) Scheme, 1970. The details of the same are given in the notes to accounts.\nThe Bank is governed by the Banking Regulations Act 1949, Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act, 1970, Nationalised Banks (Management & Miscellaneous Provisions) Scheme, 1970, SEBI (Listing Obligations & Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015 and guidelines/circulars issued by RBI, GoI and SEBI.\nSitting Fees:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1381","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":530,"text":"Sitting Fees:\nThe Directors appointed under clause (e), (f), (g), (h) and (i) of sub-section (3) of section (9) of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act, 1970, are entitled to sitting fees as mentioned below in accordance with circular F.No.15/1/2011-BO.I dated October 28, 2025 issued by Department of Financial Services, Ministry of Finance, Government of India, in terms of clause 17(1) of the Nationalised Banks Scheme for attending meetings of the Board and meetings of the committees of the Board, along with additional fees for chairing the meeting of the Board and for chairing the meeting of committees of the Board, as decided by the Board of Directors.\nIt is stated that the Bank is complying with the applicable mandatory requirements of Listing Regulations.\nThere is no case where Board of the Bank has not accepted the recommendation of any Committee of the Board which is mandatorily required in the financial year 2025-26.\nNo such agreement binding on the Bank has been entered as enumerated under Clause 5A of paragraph A of Part A of Schedule III of the SEBI (LODR) Regulations, 2015 during the FY 2025-26.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1382","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":530,"text":"No such agreement binding on the Bank has been entered as enumerated under Clause 5A of paragraph A of Part A of Schedule III of the SEBI (LODR) Regulations, 2015 during the FY 2025-26.\nThe Board of Directors in its meeting held on February 05, 2026, approved payment sitting fees of `90,000 with effect from October 28, 2025 for attending per meeting of the Board and `45,000 for attending per meeting of the committees of the Board. Additional fees of `10,000 for chairing per meeting of the Board and `5,000 for chairing per meeting of committees of the Board was also approved.\nCompliance with respect to non-mandatory requirements is also given in this report. The other disclosure requirements stipulated by the Listing Regulation are as under:\n7.1 Remuneration of Directors:\nManaging Director & CEO and Executive Directors are being paid remuneration and reimbursement of traveling & halting expenses as per the rules framed by Government of India in this regard. Other terms and conditions of the appointment of whole-time\nThe above information is also available on Bank’s website under following link: https://www.unionbankofindia. bank.in/en/common/shareholders-information\n178 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nTravelling & Halting Allowance:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1383","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":531,"text":"Travelling & Halting Allowance:\nIn addition to fees to which a director is entitled to be paid, every such director travelling in connection with the work of the Bank shall be reimbursed his Travelling & Halting expenses, if any, in terms of the provisions of clauses 17 of the Nationalized Banks (Management and Miscellaneous Provisions) Scheme, 1970, on such basis as may be fixed by Central Government from time to time.\n7.2 Particulars of Senior Management including the changes therein since the close of the previous financial year :\nSl. No. Name of Person Designation Place of Posting\n1 Shri Biraja Prasad Das CGM Zonal Head - Mumbai\n2 Shri Tata Venkat Venugopal CGM Deptt. Of Information Technology\n3 Shri Anil Kuril CGM (CISO) CISO\n4 Shri G K Sudhakar Rao CGM AGRI & FI\n5 Shri Vipan Singh CGM Audit & Inspection\n6 Shri Mangesh Mandrekar Ex-Company Secretary Board Sectt.\n7 Shri Ashish Mishra Company Secretary Board Sectt.\n8 Shri Avinash Basant Prabhu Ex-Chief Financial Officer Finance & Accounts\n9 Shri Dhirendra Jain Chief Financial Officer Finance & Accounts\n10 Shri Sarvesh Ranjan CGM Central Recon, Transaction Monitoring & AML\n7.3 Disclosure on Material Significant Related Party Transactions:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1384","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":531,"text":"7.3 Disclosure on Material Significant Related Party Transactions:\nnature of Debentures (Series I) eligible for financing of infrastructure & affordable housing of the Bank, of face value `1.00 Lakh each (“Bonds”) for cash at par aggregating ` 3,000 Crore on private placement basis.\nOther than those in the normal course of banking business, the Bank has not entered into any materially significant Related Party Transaction that has potential conflict with interests of the Bank at large.\nBank has utilised the proceeds of the issue for funding/ refinancing long term projects in infrastructure sub- sectors and affordable housing in India. A certificate from Statutory Central Auditor of the Bank on end use of funds raised through aforementioned Bonds has been obtained by the Bank.\nIt is an established practice in the Bank that Directors do not take part in the deliberations of the Board and other Sub-Committees of the Board, when matters relating to them or to their relatives/firms/companies in which they are interested are discussed.\n7.6 Penalties or Strictures:\n7.4 Disclosure of Pecuniary Relationship or Transactions:\nNo penalties or strictures were imposed on the Bank by any of the Stock Exchanges, SEBI or any Statutory Authority on any matter relating to Capital Markets during the last three years.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1385","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":531,"text":"No penalties or strictures were imposed on the Bank by any of the Stock Exchanges, SEBI or any Statutory Authority on any matter relating to Capital Markets during the last three years.\nThe Bank’s Non-Executive Directors do not have any pecuniary relationship or transaction with the Bank except to the extent of transactions done in the normal course of banking business and the sitting fees paid to them by the Bank for their attendance in the Board and Committee meetings of the Board of the Bank.\n7.7 Whistle Blower Policy:\nThe Bank has put in place the Whistle Blower Policy and same can be accessed via following link - https://www. unionbankofindia.bank.in/en/common/policies-and- disclosures\n7.5 Proceeds/utilization from Public issues, Right issues, Preferential issues etc.:\nDuring the year 2025-26, the Bank has not raised any equity capital but issued and allotted Senior, rated, listed, unsecured, redeemable, taxable, transferable Long Term fully paid- up Non-Convertible Bonds in the\nThe Audit Committee of the Board periodically reviews the functioning of the said policy. It is further stated that no employee has been denied access to the Audit Committee of the Board.\n179 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCorporate Governance Report\n7.8 Policy for determining Material Subsidiary:\nPurpose of closure","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1386","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":532,"text":"Purpose of closure\nDates of closure of trading window\nIn compliance with Regulation 46(2)(h) of SEBI (Listing Obligations & Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, the Bank has formulated Policy for determining Material Subsidiary and same can be accessed via following link https://www.unionbankofindia.bank.in/ en/common/policies-and-disclosures However as on date there is no material subsidiary of the Bank.\n1st October 2025 to 01st November 2025 Declaration of Financial Results for the quarter ended 30th September 2025. 1st January 2026 to 16th January 2026 Declaration of Financial Results for the year ended 31st December 2025.\n7.9 Related Party Transaction Policy:\nThe Bank has formulated Related Party Transaction Policy on dealing with Related Party Transactions. The said policy can be accessed via following link https:// www.unionbankofindia.bank.in/en/common/policies- and-disclosures","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1387","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":532,"text":"7.13 Management Discussion and Analysis:\nThe same has been given separately in the Annual Report.\nCompliance Reports on Corporate Governance:\nThe Bank didn’t have any materially significant related party transactions that had potential conflict with the interest of the Bank at large during the FY 2025-26.\nThe Bank has submitted quarterly compliance reports on Corporate Governance in the specified format to BSE Ltd. (BSE) & National Stock Exchange of India Ltd. (NSE) within stipulated timeline.\n7.10 Dividend Distribution Policy:\nThe Bank has formulated Policy for declaration of dividend for the year 2025-26. The said policy can be accessed via following link https://www.unionbankofindia.bank.in/ en/common/policies-and-disclosures\n7.14Dissemination of Information on Website:\nThe Bank has disseminated the required information under clauses (b) to (i) of sub-regulation 46 of Listing Regulations on its website www.unionbankofindia.bank. in.\n7.11 Takeover Code:\nThe Bank has also complied from time to time with the provisions of SEBI (Substantial Acquisition of Shares and Takeovers) Regulations, 2011 as amended.\n7.15 Details of Fees paid to Statutory Auditors:\nTotal fees for all services paid by the Bank and its subsidiaries, on a consolidated basis, to Statutory Auditors during Financial Year 2025-26 is ` 90.27 Crore.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1388","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":532,"text":"Total fees for all services paid by the Bank and its subsidiaries, on a consolidated basis, to Statutory Auditors during Financial Year 2025-26 is ` 90.27 Crore.\n7.12 Compliance with SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015:\n7.16 Disclosures in relation to the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013:\nIn pursuance of the Regulations, the Bank has formulated Code of Conduct for Prevention of Insider Trading for Designated Employees and Directors for dealing in Shares of the Bank. Various forms have been designed to receive periodical information from the Designated Employees and the Directors of the Bank, as required in terms of these Regulations. Further, the Trading Window for dealing in shares of the Bank was kept closed for the Directors and Designated Employees of the Bank as per the following details:\na. number of complaints pending as on 31.03.2025: 3 (Three)\nb. number of complaints filed during the financial year 2025-26: 08 (Eight)\nc. number of complaints disposed of during the financial year 2025-26: 10 (Ten)\nd. number of complaints pending as on end of the financial year 2025-26: 1 (One)\nPurpose of closure\nDates of closure of trading window\n1st April 2025 to 10th May 2025 Declaration of Financial Results for the year ended 31st March 2025.\n7.17 Branch Network:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1389","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":532,"text":"7.17 Branch Network:\n• The branch network of your Bank is widely spread across the country with 8,695 branches and 2 overseas branches (Sydney, Dubai DIFC) as on March 31, 2026. Out of these 59 percent of the branches are in rural and semi-urban centres.\n1st July 2025 to 21st July 2025 Declaration of Financial Results for the quarter ended 30th June 2025.\n180 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\n• As on 31.03.2025 your bank was having total 8,619 branches and 02 overseas branches. During FY 2025-26 total 24 branches were rationalized and 100 new branches were opened\nThe category-wise Branch Network on 31.03.2026 is as follows: -\nROW LABELS RURAL SEMIURBAN URBAN METRO GRAND TOTAL\nTotal Branches as of 01.04.2025 2597 2521 1735 1766 8619\nRationalised during F.Y. 2025-26 0 -8 -8 -8 -24\nOpened During F.Y 2025-26 27 44 20 9 100\n-12\nChange in Category by RBI -1 +5\n-4 +9\n-1 -18 +18\n+4\nTotal As of 31.03.2026 2628 2562 1739 1766 8695\nState wise position of branches as on 31.03.2026 in India is shown in the Map Below.\n181 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1390","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":534,"text":"Corporate Governance Report\n7.18 Credit Rating\nList of all credit ratings obtained along with revisions during the financial year 2025-26, for all debt instruments or any fixed deposit programme or any scheme or proposal of the Bank involving mobilization of funds, whether in India or abroad:\nBasel III Certificates of\nInfrastructure\nBond Outlook ESG Rating Additional Tier 1 Tier 2\nRating Agency\nDeposits\nBrickwork BWR AA+ BWR AAA - - Stable -\nCRISIL CRISIL AA+ CRISIL AAA - - Stable -\nCARE CARE AA+ CARE AAA - CARE AAA Stable CareEdge\nESG+1\nIndia Ratings IND AA+ IND AAA IND A1+ - Stable -\nICRA Ltd - ICRA AAA ICRA A1+ CARE AAA Stable -\nFITCH Ratings Ltd\nCategory Rating\nLong Term Issuer Default Rating (IDR) “BBB-” Outlook Stable\nShort Term Issuer Default Rating (IDR) F3\nViability Rating (VR) bb\nStandard & Poor’s Global Ratings\nCategory Rating Issuer Credit Rating (Long term/ Short Term) BBB/Stable/A-2\nSACP bbb-\nOutlook: Stable\n7.19 Details of Material Subsidiaries of the Bank: None of the Subsidiaries of the Bank is a Material Subsidiary.\n8. MEANS OF COMMUNICATION","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1391","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":534,"text":"8. MEANS OF COMMUNICATION\nThe quarterly, half-yearly and annual financial results of the Bank were published in leading newspapers including Business Standard (English), The Free Press Journal (English), Navbharat (Hindi) and Navshakti (Marathi). The results are simultaneously displayed on the Bank’s website www.unionbankofindia.bank.in Similarly, the press releases issued by the Bank, related presentations, shareholding pattern, etc. are also simultaneously placed on the Bank’s website under the head “Investor Relations”.\n9. SHAREHOLDERS’ INFORMATION\n9.1 Financial Year – 1st April 2025 to 31st March 2026\n9.2 Listing of Equity Shares & Bonds - The Bank’s equity shares are listed on BSE and NSE and Bonds listed on NSE. The details of stock scrip code are as follows: -\nName Code\nBSE Limited (BSE), Phiroze Jeejeebhoy Towers, Dalal Street, Mumbai - 400 001\n532477\nNational Stock Exchange of India Limited (NSE), Exchange Plaza, Plot No.C/1, G Block, Bandra-Kurla Complex, Bandra (E), Mumbai-400 051\nUNIONBANK-EQ\nUBI-AT/BB\nThe Annual Listing Fee for Equity Shares for the financial year 2026-27 has been paid to NSE on 17-04-2026 and BSE on 30-04-2026.\n182 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1392","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":535,"text":"Financials Reports Notice\nThe Bank has issued Unsecured Non-Convertible Bonds in the nature of Promissory Notes (Tier I & II capital) and long-term bonds for financing of infrastructure & affordable housing from time to time. The relevant details thereof as on 31.03.2026 are as under:\nSl. No. ISIN Bond Description Series Amount\nMaturity\nDate of Allotment\nCoupon Rate (% p.a)\n(` in Cr)\nDate\n1 INE692A08029 Basel III Compliant Additional Tier I Bond Series XX 1,000 15-Sep-16 Perpetual 9.50\n2 INE692A08169 Basel III Compliant Additional Tier I Bond Series XXXII 2,000 22-Nov-21 Perpetual 8.70\n3 INE692A08177 Basel III Compliant Additional Tier I Bond Series XXXIII 1,500 20-Dec-21 Perpetual 8.40\n4 INE692A08185 Basel III Compliant Additional Tier I Bond Series XXXIV 1,500 02-Mar-22 Perpetual 8.50\n5 INE692A08193 Basel III Compliant Additional Tier I Bond Series XXXV 1,320 25-Jul-22 Perpetual 8.69\n6 INE692A08227 Basel III Compliant Additional Tier I Bond Series XXXVII 663 23-Dec-22 Perpetual 8.40\n7 INE692A08045 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series XXII 750 24-Nov-16 24-Nov-26 7.74\n8 INE112A08051 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series II 1,000 8-Nov-19 8-Nov-29 8.93\n9 INE692A08102 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series XXVI 1,000 26-Nov-20 26-Nov-35 7.18\n10 INE692A08144 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series XXX 850 24-Jun-21 24-Jun-31 7.19","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1393","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":535,"text":"10 INE692A08144 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series XXX 850 24-Jun-21 24-Jun-31 7.19\n11 INE692A08151 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series XXXI 1,150 9-July-21 9-July-36 7.25\n12 INE692A08219 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series XXXVI-A 1,500 29-Nov-22 29-Nov-37 7.85\n13 INE692A08201 Basel III Compliant Tier II Bond Series XXXVI-B 700 29-Nov-22 29-Nov-32 7.80\n14 INE692A08235 Infrastructure Bond Bond Series I 3,000 24-Mar-26 24-Mar-36 7.16\nTotal 17,933.00\n9.3 Dividend:\nThe Board of Directors has recommended a dividend of ` 5.00 per equity share of face value ` 10/- for FY 2025-26, after deducting applicable taxes (if any).\nParticulars of AGM:\nThursday, 23rd April 2026\nBoard Meeting for considering Accounts\nDate, Time & Venue of AGM Friday, 10th July 2026 at 11.00 AM through Video Conferencing (VC) or Other Audio- Visual Means (OAVM) facility at Central Office, Union Bank of India, Mumbai (the deemed venue of the Meeting)\nDates of Book Closure Starts on Saturday, 04th July 2026 and ends on Friday, 10th July 2026 (both days inclusive)\nOpening & Closing of E-Voting Starts on Tuesday, 07th July 2026 (9:00 a.m. IST) and ends on Thursday, 09th July 2026 (5:00 p.m. IST)\n183 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCorporate Governance Report\n9.4 Financial Calendar:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1394","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":536,"text":"9.4 Financial Calendar:\nThe tentative calendar for declaration of results for the financial year 2026-27 is given below:\nFinancial Results Likely release of results\nFor the quarter ending June 30, 2026 By July 31, 2026\nFor the quarter ending September 30, 2026 By October 31, 2026\nFor the quarter ending December 31, 2026 By January 31, 2027\nFor the year ending March 31, 2027 By April 30, 2027\n9.5 Share Transfer System and Redressal of Investors’ Grievances:\nThe Bank has constituted the Share Transfer Committee of the Board to consider the transfer of shares and other related matters. In terms of SEBI guidelines dated 08.06.2018 & SEBI Press Release dated 03.12.2018, physical transfer of shares is not permitted after 31.03.2019, thus, shareholders are requested to open a demat account and dematerialise their physical shareholding.\nFurther, in the interest of investors and with a view to enhance ease of dealing in securities markets by investors, SEBI, vide its circular dated 25.01.2022, decided that the listed entities shall henceforth issue the shares in dematerialized mode only while processing the following service requests:\ni. Issue of duplicate securities certificate;\nii. Claim from Unclaimed Suspense Account;\niii Renewal / Exchange of securities certificate;\niv. Endorsement;\nv. Sub-division / Splitting of securities certificate;","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1395","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":536,"text":"v. Sub-division / Splitting of securities certificate;\nvi. Consolidation of securities certificates / folios;\nvii. Transmission;\nviii Transposition;\nThe Shareholder / claimant shall submit duly filled up Form ISR- 4 (ISR - 5 for Transmission) and the RTA / listed entity shall, after processing the service requests, issue a “Letter of Confirmation” instead of physical certificates to the shareholder / claimant within 30 days of such requests after removing objections, if any.\nThe Letter of Confirmation shall be valid for 120 days from the date of its issuance within which the shareholder / claimant shall make a request to the depository participant for dematerializing the said securities.\nThe RTA shall issue a reminder after the end of 45 days and 90 days from the date of issuance of Letter of Confirmation, informing the securities holder / claimant to submit the demat request.\nThe RTA shall retain the physical share certificate as per the existing procedure and deface the certificate with a stamp “Letter of Confirmation Issued” on the face / reverse of the certificate, subsequent to processing the service request.\nDepository Participant shall generate the demat request on the basis of Letter of Confirmation and forward the same to the Listed entity / RTA for processing the demat request.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1396","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":536,"text":"Depository Participant shall generate the demat request on the basis of Letter of Confirmation and forward the same to the Listed entity / RTA for processing the demat request.\nIf demat request is not received within 120 days of the Letter of Confirmation, shares shall be credited to the Suspense Escrow Demat Account of the entity.\nIn compliance with SEBI (Listing Obligations & Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, the Bank has appointed KFIN Technologies Limited as its Registrars and Share Transfer Agent (RTA) with a mandate to process transfer of Shares, dividend, recording of shareholders’ requests, solution of shareholders’ grievances amongst other activities connected with the issue of shares. The Investors may lodge their transfer deeds / requests / complaints with the RTA at the address mentioned below.\n184 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\nThe Bank has also established Investor Services Division at its Central Office, Mumbai. The Shareholders may contact Company Secretary, Investor Services Division for any of their requests/complaints.\n1. Debenture Trustee\nRegistrar & Share Transfer Agent (RTA) KFIN Technologies Limited Unit: Union Bank of India Selenium, Tower B, Plot 31-32, Gachibowli, Financial District, Nanakramguda, Hyderabad - 500032","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1397","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":537,"text":"Registrar & Share Transfer Agent (RTA) KFIN Technologies Limited Unit: Union Bank of India Selenium, Tower B, Plot 31-32, Gachibowli, Financial District, Nanakramguda, Hyderabad - 500032\nCompany Secretary Investor Services Division Union Bank of India 12th Floor, Central Office, 239, Vidhan Bhavan Marg, Nariman Point, Mumbai-400 021. Tel-(022) 2289 6636 Fax-(022) 22025238 Email: investorservices@unionbankofindia. bank.in\nIDBI Trusteeship Services Limited Asian Building, Ground Floor, 17, R. Kamani Marg, Ballard Estate, Mumbai - 400001 Tel- (022) 40807001 Fax- (022) 66311776 Email:\nToll Free /Phone No: 1800 309 4001\nDedicated Toll Free Number for Senior Citizens : 1800 309 4006\nitsl@idbitrustee.com, response@idbitrustee.com\nWhatsApp Number: (91) 910 009 4099\n2.Catalyst Trusteeship Limited\nEmail: einward.ris@kfintech.com\nUnit No-901, 9th Floor, Tower-B, Peninsula Business Park, Senapati\nInvestor Support Centre : https://kprism.kfintech.com\nBapat Marg, Lower Parel (W), Mumbai - 400013\nKFINTECH Corporate Website : https://kfintech.com\nTel. No. +91 22 4922 0555\nRTA Website : https://ris.kfintech.com\nWebsite: www.catalysttrustee.com\nKPRISM (Mobile Application) : https://kprism.kfintech.com/signup\nE-mail: compliancectl-mumbai@ ctltrustee.com\n9.6 Other communications:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1398","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":537,"text":"9.6 Other communications:\nIn addition to timely responses to the queries of the shareholders, the Bank proactively sends a half yearly communication to the shareholders to promote good investors’ relations.\nThe Bank sent Half-yearly Communication through email to all the shareholders whose email id is registered with the Bank / DP.\n9.7 Dematerialisation of shares:\nThe Bank has entered into agreements with both the Depositories viz. National Securities Depositories Ltd. (NSDL) and Central Depository Services (India) Ltd.\n(CDSL) for dematerialisation of the Bank’s shares. The ISIN code allotted to the Bank’s Equity Shares is INE692A01016.\nTherefore, it is requested that the shareholders holding the shares in physical mode may get their shares dematerialized in their own interest as it will save them from the need of safe custody of the share certificates which at times may lead to loss/ mutilation. Besides, this would also provide them instant liquidity as the shares of the Bank is traded in demat form. This would also result in easy and faster collection of dividend payments.\nParticulars of shares in Demat and Physical form held by the shareholders as of 31.03.2026 are as under:\nCategory No. of Shareholders No. of Shares % of shareholding\nPhysical 77,427 1,44,29,265 0.19\nDemat\nNSDL 3,13,105 1,73,52,02,156 22.73\nCDSL 5,37,965 5,88,39,74,186 77.08","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1399","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":537,"text":"CDSL 5,37,965 5,88,39,74,186 77.08\nTOTAL 9,28,497 7,63,36,05,607 100.00\nNote: Entire Shareholding of the Promoter of the Bank is in Dematerialized Form.\nFurther, in pursuance of the circular issued by SEBI, a practicing Company Secretary has also conducted reconciliation\n185 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nCorporate Governance Report\nof Share Capital Audit on a quarterly basis. During the course of reconciliation of Share Capital audit, no discrepancy in updation/maintenance of the Register of Members or processing of demat requests was found and the capital held in physical mode and demat mode tallied with the issued capital.\nThe Bank has sent various communications to its shareholders holding shares in physical form to dematerialize the same. As a result, 1497 shareholders dematerialized their 3,41,533 shares held in physical form during the year 2025-26.\n9.8 Market Price, Volume of shares traded in Stock Exchanges:\nBSE NSE BSE SENSEX\nVolume (Nos. in\nVolume (Nos. in\nMonths\nHigh (`) Low (`)\nHigh (`) Low (`)\nHigh Low\nlakhs)\nlakhs)\nApr-2025 132.7 111.8 99.69 132.89 112.52 2,675.11 80661.31 71425.01\nMay-2025 147.9 112.7 138.13 147.90 114.50 3,586.58 82718.14 78968.34\nJun-2025 158.6 138.35 129.45 158.65 138.32 2,902.63 84099.53 80354.59\nJul-2025 155.85 128.0 153.25 155.90 128.00 2,984.40 83935.01 80575.45","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1400","section":"Corporate Governance","tag":"governance","page":538,"text":"Jul-2025 155.85 128.0 153.25 155.90 128.00 2,984.40 83935.01 80575.45\nAug-2025 138.55 124.55 101.30 138.55 124.64 1,782.27 82231.17 79741.76\nSep-2025 141.6 125.5 133.51 141.63 125.45 1,998.74 83141.21 79818.38\nOct-2025 152.1 135.3 152.85 152.00 135.29 2,686.33 85290.06 80159.90\nNov-2025 157.6 147.85 88.27 157.51 147.80 2,145.44 86055.86 82670.95\nDec-2025 160.1 145.85 123.86 160.15 145.87 2,625.47 86159.02 84150.19\nJan-2026 183.4 152.75 278.80 183.34 152.70 4,576.07 85883.50 81088.59\nFeb-2026 205.45 165.5 144.59 205.49 165.11 3,407.41 85871.73 79899.42\nMar-2026 201.15 161.6 120.46 201.20 163.08 3,716.41 80632.55 71774.13\nClosing Price as on 31.03.26 ` 164.15 ` 164.20\nMarket Cap as on 31.03.2026 ` 125,305.63 Crore ` 125,34