,81,114\nII. Interest on Reserve Bank of India/Inter Bank Borrowing 2,74,80,286 2,60,34,198\nIII. Others 1,39,99,172 1,60,47,472\nTOTAL 69,47,73,321 68,91,62,784\n(` in 000’)\nParticulars For the year ended\n31st March, 2026 For the year ended\n31st March, 2025\nSCHEDULE 16 - OPERATING EXPENSES :\nI. Payments to and Provisions for Employees 15,68,73,138 14,99,63,027\nII. Rent, Taxes and Lighting 1,40,43,185 1,20,08,086\nIII. Printing and Stationery 13,79,478 13,53,106\nIV. Advertisement and Publicity 14,90,141 14,34,981\nV. A) Depreciation on Bank's Property other than leased assets 1,20,38,742 1,08,38,813\nB) Depreciation on Bank's Property on leased assets - -\nVI. Directors' Fees, Allowances and Expenses 1,09,960 83,598\nVII. Auditors' Fees and Expenses (including Branch Auditors) 9,02,709 7,09,580\nVIII. Law Charges 16,76,127 18,92,973\nIX. Postage, Telegrams, Telephones, etc. 47,80,674 42,91,758\nX. Repairs and Maintenance 52,26,169 42,68,410\nXI. Insurance 1,31,91,086 1,41,63,925\nXII. Amortization of Goodwill, if any - -\nXIII. Other Expenditure 9,03,45,886 7,94,33,707\nTOTAL 30,20,57,295 28,04,41,964\n313 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nSignificant Accounting Policy for 2025-26 on Consolidated Basis:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1685","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":666,"text":"Significant Accounting Policy for 2025-26 on Consolidated Basis:\n(For required disclosures under schedule 17 forming part of Balance Sheet)\nBackground:\nS. No. Nature Entities Stake\nUnion Bank of India (UBI or the Bank) is a banking and financial services statutory body engaged in providing a wide range of products and services to individuals, commercial enterprises, large corporates, public bodies, and institutional customers. The Bank is governed by the Banking Regulation Act, 1949. Following are the Significant Accounting Policies i.e., the specific accounting principles and methods of applying these principles in the preparation and presentation of financial statements of the Bank.\n1 Subsidiary Union Asset Management Company Pvt Ltd 100%\n2 Subsidiary Union Trustee Co Pvt Ltd 100%\n3 Subsidiary Union Bank of India (UK) Ltd 100%\n4 Subsidiary Andhra Bank Financial Services Ltd 100%\n1. Basis of Preparation\n5 Subsidiary UBI Services Ltd. 100%","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1686","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":666,"text":"5 Subsidiary UBI Services Ltd. 100%\nThe financial statements have been prepared and presented under the historical cost convention and accrual basis of accounting, unless otherwise stated. These are prepared following the Going Concern concept, in accordance with requirements prescribed under the Third Schedule of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949. The accounting and reporting policies of the Bank used in the preparation of these financial statements conform to Generally Accepted Accounting Principles in India (Indian GAAP), the guidelines issued by Reserve Bank of India (RBI) from time to time and the Accounting Standards (AS) issued by the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI) to the extent applicable and practices generally prevalent in the banking industry in India. In respect of foreign offices, statutory provisions and practices prevailing in foreign countries are complied with.\n6 JV Star Union Dai-Ichi Life Insurance Co Ltd. 25.10%\n7 JV ASREC (India) Ltd 26.02%\n8 JV India International Bank (Malaysia) BHD* 25.00%\n9 Associate Andhra Pradesh Grameena Bank 35%\n* Up to November 6, 2025\nThe consolidated financial statements are prepared on the basis of:\n1) Audited Accounts of the parent bank (Union Bank of\nIndia)\n2) Consolidation of Subsidiaries: Line by Line aggregation\nof the Income/Expenditure/Assets/Liabilities of the\n2. Use of Estimates","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1687","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":666,"text":"2. Use of Estimates\nsubsidiaries with the respective line item of the parent\nThe preparation of financial statements in conformity with GAAP requires the management to make estimates and assumptions considered in the reported amount of Assets and Liabilities (including Contingent Liabilities) as of the date of the financial statements and the reported Income and the Expenses during the reporting period. Management believes that the estimates wherever used in the preparation of the financial statements are prudent and reasonable. Actual results can differ from these estimates. Any revision in the accounting estimates is recognized prospectively in the current and future period.\nbank, after eliminating all intra-group transactions,\nunrealized profits/loss in terms of AS 21 on Consolidated\nFinancial Statements issued by Institute of Chartered\nAccountants of India (ICAI).\n3) Consolidation of Associates: The Investment in\nAssociate is accounted for consolidation as per Equity\nMethod in terms of AS 23 on Accounting for Investments\nin Associates in Consolidated Financial Statement issued\nby Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI).\n4) Consolidation of Joint Ventures: Line by Line\nconsolidation is done with proportionate share in Joint\n3. Basis of consolidation\nVenture in terms of AS-27 on Financials Reporting in","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1688","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":666,"text":"Venture in terms of AS-27 on Financials Reporting in\na) Bank is having 5 subsidiaries, 3 JVs and 1 associate. The details are as under: -\nInterest of Joint Venture issued by Institute of Chartered\nAccountants of India (ICAI).\n314 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\na) On interest bearing securities, it is recognized\nb) In case of Domestic Associate/Subsidiaries and Joint Venture, accounting adjustments arising due to different accounting policies followed by parent bank and associate/subsidiaries and Joint Venture have not been carried out due to practical difficulties on the basis of data provided by associates/ subsidiaries and Joint Venture as the amounts are not material.\nonly at the time of sale/redemption.\nb) On Zero- coupon securities, it is accounted for\nover the balance tenor of the securities on a constant yield basis.\nvi) Dividend is accounted on an accrual basis where\nthe right to receive the dividend is established.\nc) The difference between cost to the Group of its investment in the subsidiaries and the Parent’s portion of the equity of the subsidiaries is recognized in the CFS as Goodwill / Capital Reserve, as the case may be.\nvii) Sale of NPAs accounted in terms of extant RBI\nguidelines.\nviii) Interest on Income-tax refunds is accounted for\non receipt of Intimation order from the Income Tax Department.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1689","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":667,"text":"on receipt of Intimation order from the Income Tax Department.\nd) Minority interest in the net assets of the consolidated subsidiaries consists of:\nb) Non-Banking entities\ni) The amount of equity attributable to the minority at the date on which investment in a subsidiary is made and\nLife Insurance\ni. Premium Income\nPremium (net of GST) is recognized as income when due. For linked business, premium is recognized when the associated units are created. Top up premiums are considered as single premium. Premium on lapsed policies is recognized as income when such policies are reinstated. Commission received on reinsurance ceded is recognized as income in the period in which reinsurance premium is ceded.\nii) The minority share of movements in revenue reserves / loss and equity since the date the parent subsidiary relationship came into existence.\niii) The excess, and any further losses applicable to the\nminority, are adjusted against the majority interest except to the extent that the minority has a binding obligation to, and is able to, make good the losses.\n4. Revenue Recognition\nii. Income from linked funds\na) Banking entities\nIncome from linked funds which includes premium allocation charges, policy administrative charges, mortality charges, fund management charges etc. are recovered from the linked funds in accordance with the terms and conditions of policies issued.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1690","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":667,"text":"Income from linked funds which includes premium allocation charges, policy administrative charges, mortality charges, fund management charges etc. are recovered from the linked funds in accordance with the terms and conditions of policies issued.\ni) Income and Expenditure have been accounted for on accrual basis unless otherwise stated.\nii) Income on Non-Performing Assets (NPAs) is recognized to the extent realized as per the prudential norms prescribed by the RBI. Income accounted for in the preceding year and remaining unrealized is derecognized in respect of assets classified as NPAs during the year.\niii. Reinsurance Premium\nCost of reinsurance ceded is accounted for at the time of recognition of premium income in accordance with the treaty or in principle arrangement with the reinsurer. Profit commission on reinsurance ceded is netted off against premium ceded on reinsurance.\niii) Commission on Letters of Credit (LC), Bank\nGuarantee (BG), Deferred Payment Guarantee and Government Business are recognised on accrual basis proportionately over the period.\niv. Benefits paid (including claims)","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1691","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":667,"text":"iv. Benefits paid (including claims)\nBenefits paid comprise of policy benefits & claim settlement costs, if any. Death, rider & surrender claims are accounted for on receipt of intimation. Survival benefit claims and maturity claims are accounted for when due. Withdrawals & surrenders under linked policies are accounted for in the respective schemes when the associated units are cancelled. Reinsurance recoveries on claims are accounted for in the same period as the related claims.\niv) Exchange and brokerage earned, rent on Safe\nDeposit Lockers, income from Aadhaar cards, Minimum balance charges etc. are accounted for on realization basis.\nv) Income (Other than interest) on investments in “Held to Maturity” (HTM) category acquired at discount to the face value is recognized as follows:\n315 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nGRI Index Capitals Strategy About\nSignificant Accounting Policy for 2025-26 on Consolidated Basis\nv. Acquisition Costs\nFor Running Accounts:\nAcquisition costs are costs that vary with and are primarily related to acquisition of insurance contracts and are expensed in the period in which they are incurred.\n Towards expenses & costs etc.\n Towards interest held in dummy ledger (unapplied interest) including unrecovered interest reversed at the time of NPA.\nvi. Liability for life policies\n Towards running ledger balance.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1692","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":668,"text":" Towards running ledger balance.\nActuarial liability for life policies in force and for policies in respect of which premium has been discontinued but a liability exists, is determined by the Appointed Actuary using the gross premium method and in case of group business, unearned premium reserve method, in accordance with accepted actuarial practice, requirements of Insurance Act, 1938, IRDA regulations and the stipulations of Institute of Actuaries of India.\n5.2. In case borrower stipulates terms of appropriation differently than above and if such different terms of appropriation is accepted by Bank then appropriation of recoveries is as per the sanction terms.\n5.3. In case of OTS & all NCLT accounts, recovery either through resolution/liquidation:\nAppropriation of recovery to be done as discussed here under or as per the sanction stipulations\nAsset Management\n Towards principal.\ni. Investment management fees are recognized net of tax on an accrual basis as a percentage of the average daily net assets of the mutual fund schemes (excluding the investments made by the company in the schemes) such that it does not exceed the limit prescribed by the SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996 and any further amendments.\n Towards interest held in dummy ledger (unapplied interest) including unrecovered interest reversed at the time of NPA.\n Towards expenses & costs etc.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1693","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":668,"text":" Towards expenses & costs etc.\n5.4. In case of Non-Performing Investment recovery is apportioned as mentioned below:\nii. Investment advisory fees are recognized on accrual basis in accordance with the terms of contract with the customers.\na. Towards expenses & costs etc.\nb. Towards unrecovered interest reversed on the date of NPI.\niii. Interest income is recognized using the time proportion\nmethod, based on the rates implicit in the transaction.\nc. Interest held in dummy ledger (unapplied interest).\niv. Dividend income is recognized when right to receive is\nestablished.\nd. Towards arrears of principal/EMI till the date of recovery.\n5. Appropriation of Recovery:\ne. Towards running ledger balance\nRecoveries other than by way of OTS/NCLT was appropriated as under:\nAny recovery made in the NPA account other than prescribed above, is dealt in accordance with the Recovery Management Policy of the bank.\n5.1. When there is no agreement between the debtor and creditor as to how monies paid by the debtor are required to be appropriated by the creditor, the order of appropriation is as under:\n5.5. In case of failed OTS:\nIn case any OTS is failed/Cancelled, the Recovery Received before the cancellation should be appropriated as per the Point No.5.3 of this policy, but Recovery Received after cancellation of the OTS should be appropriated as per above Point No.5.1 of this policy.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1694","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":668,"text":"In case any OTS is failed/Cancelled, the Recovery Received before the cancellation should be appropriated as per the Point No.5.3 of this policy, but Recovery Received after cancellation of the OTS should be appropriated as per above Point No.5.1 of this policy.\nFor Term Loans:\n Towards expenses & costs etc.\n Towards unrecovered interest reversed on the date of NPA.\n6. Investments\n Interest held in dummy ledger (unapplied interest).\n6.1. Categorization of investments\n Towards arrears of principal/EMI till the date of recovery.\na) Bank will classify their entire investment portfolio (except investments in their own subsidiaries, joint\n Towards principal.\n316 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\n6.1.1 HTM\nventures and associates) under three categories, viz., Held to Maturity (HTM), Available for Sale (AFS) and Fair Value through Profit and Loss (FVTPL). Held for Trading (HFT) is a separate investment subcategory within FVTPL. The category of the investment is decided by the bank before or at the time of acquisition and this decision is properly documented.\na) Securities that fulfil the following conditions is\nclassified under HTM:\n(i) The security is acquired with the intention\nand objective of holding it to maturity, i.e., the\nfinancial assets are held with an objective to\ncollect the contractual cash flows; and","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1695","section":"Balance Sheet (Consolidated)","tag":"balance","page":669,"text":"collect the contractual cash flows; and\nb) Bank will continue to present the investments in the Balance Sheet as set out in The Third Schedule to the BR Act (Form A, Schedule 8 - Investments) as under:\n(ii) The contractual terms of the security give rise\nto cash flows that are “solely payments of\nprincipal and interest” on principal outstanding\n(‘SPPI criterion’) on specified dates.\n(i) Government securities\nb) Notwithstanding the intent with which the following\n(ii) Other approved securities\nsecurities are acquired, they would not meet the\nSPPI criteria and therefore would not be eligible for\n(iii) Shares\nclassification either as HTM or AFS:\n(iv) Debentures & Bonds\n(i) Instruments with compulsorily, optionally or\n(v) Subsidiaries and / or joint ventures\ncontingently convertible features.\n(vi) Others (to be specified)\n(ii) Instruments with contractual loss absorbency\nc) From the context of inputs used for valuation, the investments is either of the following:\nfeatures such as those qualifying for Additional\nTier 1 and Tier 2 under Basel III Capital","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","do