ed under\nb) IFR is eligible for inclusion in Tier II capital. The cap applicable on recognition of General Provisions and Loss Reserves as Tier II capital is not applicable on IFR.\nAFS against which there are cumulative gains in AFS-Reserve,\nthe provision required may be created by charging the same\nto AFS-Reserve to the extent of such available gains.\nc) Bank would, be permitted to draw down the balance available in IFR in excess of two percent of its AFS and FVTPL (including HFT) portfolio, for credit to the balance of profit/loss as disclosed in the profit and loss account at the end of any accounting year.\nProvided further that in the case of an investment\ncategorised under AFS against which there are cumulative\nlosses in AFS-Reserve, the cumulative losses is transferred\nfrom AFS-Reserve to the Profit and Loss Account.\nd) In the event the balance in the IFR is less than two percent of the AFS and FVTPL (including HFT) investment portfolio, a draw down is permitted subject to the following conditions:\ne) Upon an account being upgraded as per IRACP norms,\nany provision previously recognised is reversed and\nsymmetric recognition of MTM gains and losses can\nresume.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1716","section":"Notes to Financial Statements","tag":"notes","page":676,"text":"resume.\nI. The drawn down amount is used only for meeting the minimum CET 1/Tier 1 capital requirements by way of appropriation to free reserves or reducing the balance of loss.\nf) Investments in Government securities and Government\nguaranteed investment.\nI. Investments in Central Government Securities and\nII. The amount drawn down would not be more than the extent the MTM provisions/losses during the aforesaid year exceed the net profit on sale of investments during that year.\nState Government Securities would not be classified\nas NPI.\nII. Investments in Central Government guaranteed\nsecurities would also not be classified as NPI until the\n6.8 Derivatives\nCentral Government has repudiated the guarantee\n6.8.1\u0007Bank would comply with the requirements of the Guidance Note on Accounting for Derivative Contracts (revised 2021) issued by the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India except for paragraph 63 of the said Guidance Note. Bank would present their derivative asset and liabilities as separate line items under Schedule 11:’Other Assets’ and Schedule 5:’Other Liabilities’ respectively. Bank may make adjustments to the carrying value of their investments in compliance with the hedge accounting requirements of the said Guidance Note.\nwhen invoked. In respect of such securities held in\nAFS and FVTPL, bank would continue to recognise","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1717","section":"Notes to Financial Statements","tag":"notes","page":676,"text":"AFS and FVTPL, bank would continue to recognise\nMTM gains/losses in AFS-Reserve and Profit and Loss\nrespectively. However, any income is recognised\nonly on realisation basis.\nIII. Investment in State Government guaranteed\nsecurities, would attract prudential norms for\nidentification of NPI and provisioning, when\ninterest/ instalment of principal (including maturity\nproceeds) or any other amount due to the bank\nremains unpaid for more than 90 days.\n6.8.2 \u0007Bank would categorize their derivatives portfolio into three fair value hierarchies viz. Level 1, Level 2, and Level 3 as defined in Clause 4 of the RBI MASTER CIRCULAR and disclose the same in the notes to accounts of their financial statements as per template specified in disclosure template.\ng) NPI appropriation process remains unchanged.\n6.7 Investment Fluctuation Reserve\na) Bank would create an Investment Fluctuation Reserve\n(IFR) until the amount of IFR is at least two per cent of the\nAFS and FVTPL (including HFT) portfolio, on a continuing\n6.8.3 \u0007Bank would not pay dividends out of net unrealised gains recognised in the Profit and Loss Account arising on fair valuation of Level 3 derivatives assets and liabilities on their Balance Sheet. Further, such net unrealised gains on Level 3 derivatives recognised in the Profit and Loss\nbasis, by transferring to the IFR an amount not less than\nthe lower of the following:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c1718","section":"Notes to Financial Statements","tag":"notes","page":676,"text":"the lower of the following:\nI. Net profit on sale of investments during the year.\nII. Net profit for the year, less mandatory\nappropriations.\nAccount is deducted from CET 1 capital.\n324 Union Bank of India | Integrated Annual Report 2025-26\nFinancials Reports Notice\n7. Advances\nrebates. Subsequent expenditure incurred on assets put\nto use is capitalized only when it increases the future\ni) Advances in India, are classified under four categories,\nbenefits from such assets or their functional capability.\ni.e.\nLand and Buildings, if revalued are stated at revalued\n(a) Standard,\namount. The Bank revalues immovable assets at every\nthree years. The increase in Net Book Value of the\n(b) Sub-standard,\nasset due to revaluation is credited to the Revaluation\n(c) Doubtful and\nReserve Account without routing through the Profit and\nLoss Account. Additional Depreciation on the revalued\n(d) Loss assets.\nasset is charged to the Profit and Loss Account and\nProvisions required on such advances are made as per\nappropriated from the Revaluation Reserves to Revenue\nthe extant prudential norms issued by the RBI in terms\nReserve. The revalued asset is depreciated over the\nof RBI Master Directions RBI/2025-2026/164 DOR.STR.\nbalance useful life of the asset as assessed at the time of\nREC.83/21.04.048/2025-26 dated November 28, 2025.\nrevaluation.\n